{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9GFM4j18NpZLXhakJJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RkJPic59oiFF7MUH1x2z1K","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/START_HERE.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4853,"cache_read_input_tokens":25536,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4853},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"923cc5cc-b97c-4848-b2f7-e062f5e73d83","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:13.952Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9GF6g2Rhhjvmqb5q6u","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01RkJPic59oiFF7MUH1x2z1K":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/START_HERE.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RkJPic59oiFF7MUH1x2z1K","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Shardfall and the Pavilion engine: start here\n2\t\n3\tA Rust game engine built for AI agents (Pavilion), the hack-and-slash built on it (Shardfall)\n4\tand a pavilion of demo rooms. This page is for an agent receiving the source, from a clone of\n5\thttps://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall or from a handoff archive (no Git history, build\n6\tcaches, saved heroes or executables; `git init` if you want to track your changes).\n7\t\n8\t## Which route\n9\t- **Make a new game on the engine:** read `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` whole. It is the only page\n10\t  you need to start; it begins from the starter game (`crates/pav_core/src/games/`).\n11\t- **Work on the engine or on Shardfall:** read `docs/DOCTRINE.md`, `AGENTS.md` and\n12\t  `docs/PROGRESS.md` (Claude Code loads them through `CLAUDE.md`); `docs/DESIGN.md` and\n13\t  `docs/GAME.md` when the work touches the design.\n14\t\n15\tPrompts to give the receiving agent:\n16\t\n17\t> Make a game: read START_HERE.md and docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md, install the Linux packages and\n18\t> build the `pav` CLI. Then make *(your idea, e.g. \"a top-down maze where you collect five\n19\t> keys and escape while ghosts patrol\")* on the engine, starting from the starter game. Give\n20\t> it a bot that wins and the tests from the guide, check it with the capture tools, tell me\n21\t> how to play it, and end with the engine feedback the guide asks for.\n22\t\n23\t> Work on the engine: read START_HERE.md, then follow AGENTS.md. *(the task)*\n24\t\n25\tAgents in auto modes may refuse to unpack an archive or run its scripts unless the prompt\n26\tsays to; say so in the prompt when you hand over an archive.\n27\t\n28\t## Linux setup\n29\tUse a Linux machine with Rust installed through rustup. `rust-toolchain.toml` selects\n30\tRust 1.98.1 automatically. The first build needs network access to download the\n31\ttoolchain and dependencies; `Cargo.lock` pins the dependency versions.\n32\t\n33\tOn Debian / Ubuntu, install the native build and runtime dependencies below. If\n34\talready running as root, omit `sudo`. On a managed machine, ask its administrator\n35\tto install missing packages.\n36\t\n37\t```sh\n38\tsudo apt-get update\n39\tsudo apt-get install -y build-essential pkg-config libasound2-dev libudev-dev \\\n40\t  mesa-vulkan-drivers vulkan-tools xvfb libxkbcommon-x11-0 libx11-xcb1 \\\n41\t  libxcursor1 libxrandr2 libxi6\n42\t```\n43\t\n44\tSimulation tools need no display or GPU. Screenshot tools use Vulkan and can use\n45\tMesa lavapipe (software rendering) on a machine without a GPU. Keep builds on disk\n46\tand limit parallel jobs on machines with little RAM.\n47\t\n48\t## Build and inspect headlessly\n49\t\n50\t```sh\n51\tCARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 cargo build --locked -p pav_tools --bin pav\n52\t./target/debug/pav help\n53\t./target/debug/pav play scene=starter bot=true seeds=5\n54\t./target/debug/pav capture scene=world ticks=60 width=640 height=360 out=out/pavilion.png\n55\t```\n56\t\n57\t`starter` is the starter game; `world` is the pavilion of demo rooms; `town` is Shardfall's\n58\ttown; `arena` is the wave arena; `lab` is the Menagerie; `level/1` starts the descent. A room\n59\tkey such as `drift` loads that demo by itself. `pav repl` keeps a session across commands:\n60\t\n61\t```sh\n62\tprintf 'load scene=world\\ngoto room=drift\\ncapture width=640 height=360 out=out/drift.png\\n' | ./target/debug/pav repl\n63\t./target/debug/pav levelmap depth=7 nav=true out=out/level7.png\n64\t./target/debug/pav campaign scene=town from=1 to=3 wall=60\n65\tcargo test --locked -p pav_core\n66\t```\n67\t\n68\tInspect the generated images with the agent's image viewer. `docs/REFERENCE.md` describes\n69\tevery tool. `.mcp.json` exposes the same CLI as the `shardfall` MCP server; run the `cargo\n70\tbuild` above before starting an MCP client, because a first build takes longer than clients\n71\twait for a server.\n72\t\n73\t## Run the game window\n74\t\n75\t```sh\n76\tCARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 cargo build --locked -p pav_app\n77\t./target/debug/shardfall --scene starter # the starter game\n78\t./target/debug/shardfall --scene world   # tech demo\n79\t./target/debug/shardfall --scene town    # Shardfall\n80\t```\n81\t\n82\tWithout a graphical desktop, use `xvfb-run -a` around the game command, or use the\n83\theadless capture tools above. The demo's F2 menu jumps between rooms. Startup\n84\tsettings and hero saves are written next to the game executable.\n85\t\n86\tFor Windows cross-compilation and Wine checks, `scripts/setup-linux.sh` installs the\n87\tadditional tools and `scripts/build-windows.sh` builds the executables.\n88\t\n89\t## The studios (live editing in the window)\n90\t- **Objects:** `scripts/asset-studio.sh`, with `.mcp.studio.json` for the LLM: `assets`,\n91\t  `asset_edit`, `asset_preview`, `asset_spawn` (`docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`,\n92\t  `docs/ASSET_STUDIO_LIVE.md`).\n93\t- **Animation:** `scripts/animation-studio.sh`, with `.mcp.animation-studio.json` (or `pav\n94\t  live` in a second terminal): `anim_edit`, `anim_preview` (`docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`).\n95\t- **Creatures:** Node 22.18 or newer, then `scripts/creature-studio.sh`; `creature_edit`\n96\t  returns a job to follow with `creature_status` (`docs/CREATURE_STUDIO.md`).\n97\t\n98\tEdits save to readable files and show in the window without a rebuild. `.mcp.json` runs a\n99\tseparate headless session; the studio configurations control the visible window.\n100\t\n101\t## Make another handoff archive\n102\tFrom a Git checkout, `scripts/package-agent.sh` packages the tracked files (with local edits)\n103\tinto `out/agent/shardfall-linux-agent.tar.gz` and a SHA-256 checksum; add new files to Git\n104\tfirst. It leaves out `pavilion-lite/` (an older engine, frozen). `scripts/check-handoff.sh`\n105\tunpacks a fresh archive elsewhere, builds it and plays the starter, as a receiving agent\n106\twould: run it after changing what the archive carries or this page.\n107\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"2c56eb84-d46a-4b93-a531-1f95a6394ab4","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:14.171Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/START_HERE.md","content":"# Shardfall and the Pavilion engine: start here\n\nA Rust game engine built for AI agents (Pavilion), the hack-and-slash built on it (Shardfall)\nand a pavilion of demo rooms. This page is for an agent receiving the source, from a clone of\nhttps://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall or from a handoff archive (no Git history, build\ncaches, saved heroes or executables; `git init` if you want to track your changes).\n\n## Which route\n- **Make a new game on the engine:** read `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` whole. It is the only page\n  you need to start; it begins from the starter game (`crates/pav_core/src/games/`).\n- **Work on the engine or on Shardfall:** read `docs/DOCTRINE.md`, `AGENTS.md` and\n  `docs/PROGRESS.md` (Claude Code loads them through `CLAUDE.md`); `docs/DESIGN.md` and\n  `docs/GAME.md` when the work touches the design.\n\nPrompts to give the receiving agent:\n\n> Make a game: read START_HERE.md and docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md, install the Linux packages and\n> build the `pav` CLI. Then make *(your idea, e.g. \"a top-down maze where you collect five\n> keys and escape while ghosts patrol\")* on the engine, starting from the starter game. Give\n> it a bot that wins and the tests from the guide, check it with the capture tools, tell me\n> how to play it, and end with the engine feedback the guide asks for.\n\n> Work on the engine: read START_HERE.md, then follow AGENTS.md. *(the task)*\n\nAgents in auto modes may refuse to unpack an archive or run its scripts unless the prompt\nsays to; say so in the prompt when you hand over an archive.\n\n## Linux setup\nUse a Linux machine with Rust installed through rustup. `rust-toolchain.toml` selects\nRust 1.98.1 automatically. The first build needs network access to download the\ntoolchain and dependencies; `Cargo.lock` pins the dependency versions.\n\nOn Debian / Ubuntu, install the native build and runtime dependencies below. If\nalready running as root, omit `sudo`. On a managed machine, ask its administrator\nto install missing packages.\n\n```sh\nsudo apt-get update\nsudo apt-get install -y build-essential pkg-config libasound2-dev libudev-dev \\\n  mesa-vulkan-drivers vulkan-tools xvfb libxkbcommon-x11-0 libx11-xcb1 \\\n  libxcursor1 libxrandr2 libxi6\n```\n\nSimulation tools need no display or GPU. Screenshot tools use Vulkan and can use\nMesa lavapipe (software rendering) on a machine without a GPU. Keep builds on disk\nand limit parallel jobs on machines with little RAM.\n\n## Build and inspect headlessly\n\n```sh\nCARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 cargo build --locked -p pav_tools --bin pav\n./target/debug/pav help\n./target/debug/pav play scene=starter bot=true seeds=5\n./target/debug/pav capture scene=world ticks=60 width=640 height=360 out=out/pavilion.png\n```\n\n`starter` is the starter game; `world` is the pavilion of demo rooms; `town` is Shardfall's\ntown; `arena` is the wave arena; `lab` is the Menagerie; `level/1` starts the descent. A room\nkey such as `drift` loads that demo by itself. `pav repl` keeps a session across commands:\n\n```sh\nprintf 'load scene=world\\ngoto room=drift\\ncapture width=640 height=360 out=out/drift.png\\n' | ./target/debug/pav repl\n./target/debug/pav levelmap depth=7 nav=true out=out/level7.png\n./target/debug/pav campaign scene=town from=1 to=3 wall=60\ncargo test --locked -p pav_core\n```\n\nInspect the generated images with the agent's image viewer. `docs/REFERENCE.md` describes\nevery tool. `.mcp.json` exposes the same CLI as the `shardfall` MCP server; run the `cargo\nbuild` above before starting an MCP client, because a first build takes longer than clients\nwait for a server.\n\n## Run the game window\n\n```sh\nCARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 cargo build --locked -p pav_app\n./target/debug/shardfall --scene starter # the starter game\n./target/debug/shardfall --scene world   # tech demo\n./target/debug/shardfall --scene town    # Shardfall\n```\n\nWithout a graphical desktop, use `xvfb-run -a` around the game command, or use the\nheadless capture tools above. The demo's F2 menu jumps between rooms. Startup\nsettings and hero saves are written next to the game executable.\n\nFor Windows cross-compilation and Wine checks, `scripts/setup-linux.sh` installs the\nadditional tools and `scripts/build-windows.sh` builds the executables.\n\n## The studios (live editing in the window)\n- **Objects:** `scripts/asset-studio.sh`, with `.mcp.studio.json` for the LLM: `assets`,\n  `asset_edit`, `asset_preview`, `asset_spawn` (`docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`,\n  `docs/ASSET_STUDIO_LIVE.md`).\n- **Animation:** `scripts/animation-studio.sh`, with `.mcp.animation-studio.json` (or `pav\n  live` in a second terminal): `anim_edit`, `anim_preview` (`docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`).\n- **Creatures:** Node 22.18 or newer, then `scripts/creature-studio.sh`; `creature_edit`\n  returns a job to follow with `creature_status` (`docs/CREATURE_STUDIO.md`).\n\nEdits save to readable files and show in the window without a rebuild. `.mcp.json` runs a\nseparate headless session; the studio configurations control the visible window.\n\n## Make another handoff archive\nFrom a Git checkout, `scripts/package-agent.sh` packages the tracked files (with local edits)\ninto `out/agent/shardfall-linux-agent.tar.gz` and a SHA-256 checksum; add new files to Git\nfirst. It leaves out `pavilion-lite/` (an older engine, frozen). `scripts/check-handoff.sh`\nunpacks a fresh archive elsewhere, builds it and plays the starter, as a receiving agent\nwould: run it after changing what the archive carries or this page.\n","numLines":107,"startLine":1,"totalLines":107}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RkJPic59oiFF7MUH1x2z1K","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9GFM4j18NpZLXhakJJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PyoKtTb4ozVzNtaWrBwZ3q","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/README.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4853,"cache_read_input_tokens":25536,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4853},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"0ecb75bb-3821-4c3b-a2d3-eddaf3389dbb","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:14.341Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9GF6g2Rhhjvmqb5q6u","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01PyoKtTb4ozVzNtaWrBwZ3q":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/README.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PyoKtTb4ozVzNtaWrBwZ3q","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Shardfall\n2\t\n3\tA procedural hack-and-slash adventure built in Rust and WebGPU, with a playable Pavilion\n4\ttech demo. Explore Emberwatch and the endless Depths, or visit the pavilion's rooms to try\n5\tmovement, physics, procedural animation, visual effects, rendering styles, and genre demos.\n6\tEverything is generated from code, every parameter can be tuned live, and agents can drive\n7\tthe game through the same tools a person uses.\n8\t\n9\tPlay Shardfall in the browser from one self-contained file: `scripts/build-web.sh\n10\t--single-file` writes `target/web/shardfall.html`, which runs from disk (add `?scene=world` to\n11\tits address for the Pavilion tech demo). Use recent Chrome or Edge with WebGPU and hardware\n12\tacceleration enabled. Native builds: `scripts/build-windows.sh` (Windows `.exe`) and\n13\t`cargo build --profile dist -p pav_app` (Linux, `target/dist/shardfall`). Phones and tablets play by touch (Chrome\n14\ton Android, Safari on iOS 26 or later): drag anywhere to move, attacks are automatic, and the\n15\tbuttons in the corner dodge, cast and drink.\n16\t\n17\t- Make a game on the engine: [`docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`](docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md), an agent's guide\n18\t  that starts from the starter game (`load scene=starter`, `crates/pav_core/src/games/`).\n19\t- Agent handoff: [`START_HERE.md`](START_HERE.md) (routes and prompts). Run\n20\t  `scripts/package-agent.sh` to create a standalone source archive and checksum in\n21\t  `out/agent/`; `scripts/check-handoff.sh` checks it the way a receiving agent uses it.\n22\t- [`pavilion-lite/`](pavilion-lite/): an older compact engine with a CPU renderer. Frozen: a\n23\t  finished experiment, outside the doctrine because its renderer isn't WebGPU, and left out\n24\t  of the handoff archive.\n25\t- Design: [`docs/DESIGN.md`](docs/DESIGN.md). Current state: [`docs/PROGRESS.md`](docs/PROGRESS.md);\n26\t  history and decisions: [`docs/HISTORY.md`](docs/HISTORY.md). Principles:\n27\t  [`docs/DOCTRINE.md`](docs/DOCTRINE.md). Rules for agents working here: [`AGENTS.md`](AGENTS.md).\n28\t\n29\t## Live animation editing with an LLM\n30\t\n31\tRun `scripts/animation-studio.sh` or `shardfall.exe --animation-studio` to open Animation Studio.\n32\tConnect your LLM with the MCP configuration in `.mcp.animation-studio.json`.\n33\tThe LLM can create clips, copy existing animations, change key poses, and see the results through captured images.\n34\tYou see each accepted edit in the game window, without a rebuild.\n35\t\n36\tThe studio includes playback, time and speed controls, source credits, and 32 levels of undo and redo.\n37\tEdits save to readable JSON files. The preview uses the game's renderer and leaves the current game state intact.\n38\tSee [`docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`](docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md) for setup, example prompts, and tool arguments.\n39\t\n40\t## The adventure\n41\tA fast hack-and-slash built on the engine ([`docs/GAME.md`](docs/GAME.md)). `shardfall.exe`\n42\tstarts in **Emberwatch**, the town: trade with Hilda the smith, gamble with Odo, buy stronger\n43\tpotions from Mother Wren, keep loot in the stash, and take the portal down. Twelve designed\n44\tlevels each introduce one mechanic (shrines, powder kegs, spike plates, rift gates, wind,\n45\ttotems, lava, ice, crumbling floors, darkness, cursed chests, time bubbles) and mix in the\n46\tones before; after level 12 the Depths generate new combinations forever. The portal also\n47\tleads to the Proving Grounds (wave arena) and the Menagerie (generated creatures). Your hero\n48\tis saved next to the executable on every trip and on quit. The pavilion of engine rooms is\n49\tstill there: Esc menu → Load scene → `world` (or `shardfall.exe --scene world`).\n50\t\n51\t| Key | Action |\n52\t|---|---|\n53\t| W A S D · mouse | move · aim |\n54\t| Left / right mouse, Q E R F | skills (hold to repeat) · Shift: attack in place |\n55\t| Space | dodge roll |\n56\t| 1 | potion |\n57\t| G | use: trade, stash, portal, gamble, brew, the way down, cursed chests |\n58\t| I (Tab) · P · C · K · M | inventory · passive tree · character · skill bar · map |\n59\t| T | town portal |\n60\t| Esc | menu (difficulty sliders for play-testing, new hero, Look & filters) |\n61\t\n62\tItems drop as you fight (walk over magic+ items or click their names); hover an item to compare\n63\tit with what you wear. Gamepad: X Y B RB LB RT skills, A dodge, D-pad up potion, D-pad right use,\n64\tD-pad left map, D-pad down the hero's panels (LB / RB switch between inventory, character,\n65\tskills and passive tree). With a window open, the stick moves a cursor: A clicks, X\n66\tright-clicks, hold Y for shift (take a whole path in the tree), the right stick scrolls and\n67\tzooms, B closes. Start opens the menu, which works the same way.\n68\t\n69\t## Playing\n70\tWindows: run `shardfall.exe`. It writes `shardfall.toml` next to itself on first start (Vulkan by\n71\tdefault; `backend = \"dx12\"` switches). `shardfall.exe --room drift` starts in a room of the\n72\tpavilion (`--scene world` for the pavilion itself).\n73\tBrowser: serve the web build (below) and open it in a recent Chrome or Edge; add `?room=drift`\n74\tto the address to start in a room.\n75\t\n76\t| Key | Action |\n77\t|---|---|\n78\t| W A S D | move (Shift: walk slowly) |\n79\t| Space | jump (hold for higher) |\n80\t| C / Ctrl | crouch (hold) · dodge roll while running |\n81\t| Z | crawl (toggle) |\n82\t| F / left click | throw a bomb, or fire the blaster in shooting rooms |\n83\t| E | get in / out of a vehicle |\n84\t| Backspace (hold) | rewind time |\n85\t| Right-drag · wheel · 1-8 | rotate · zoom · camera presets |\n86\t| Esc | menu (Look & filters: pixel art, cel shading, outlines, oil paint, halftone, ASCII, pencil, palettes, grading, haze... on the whole scene, the characters & objects or the environment) |\n87\t| H | how the room works (the station guide); outside rooms the field guide |\n88\t| F1 | tuning panel (every parameter, presets) |\n89\t| F2 | jump to any room |\n90\t| F4 / F5 | leave the room / reset it |\n91\t| F3 · F12 | boot diagnostics · screenshot |\n92\t\n93\tGamepads work too. Each room shows an info card on entry (what it demonstrates, what to try) and\n94\tits own controls. Every room is also a lesson: H (or the card's **How it works**) opens its\n95\tstation guide with what you are seeing, how the engine does it step by step, live sliders for\n96\tthe settings involved, where games use it, phrases to ask for it, what it costs, the engine's\n97\town code and the words to know; stepping on a pad shows what that pad changed and why. The\n98\t**Field guide** (Esc menu) collects every word and every room's \"ask for it\" phrases.\n99\t\n100\t## Rooms\n101\t| Wing (corridor) | Rooms |\n102\t|---|---|\n103\t| Movement & Feel Lab (east) | Playground, Feel Lab, Tightrope, Slalom, Timing Gates, Dodge Gauntlet, Camera Bench, Verticality Tower |\n104\t| Physics Lab (north) | Stacking & Toppling, Springs & Soft Bodies, Chains & Rope Bridges, Conveyors, Bounce & Friction Gallery, Destructible Floors, Stress Test |\n105\t| Animation Lab (west) | Walk Cycles, Jointed Limbs, Impact & Recoil, Squash & Stretch, Secondary Motion, Character Style Bench |\n106\t| Visual Effects (south) | Lights & Shadows, Particle Garden, Bloom & Glow, Heat & Shockwaves, Global Illumination, Light Shafts, Wind & Water |\n107\t| Styles & Filters (east) | Pure Styles, Style vs Filters, Filter Stack Bench, Mix & Match, Paint & Print, Screen Transitions |\n108\t| Genre Wing (north) | Bullet Hell, Grid Stealth, Drift Circuit, Helicopter Run |\n109\t| Workshop (south) | Sandbox (spawn, drag, delete and save objects) |\n110\t\n111\tRooms are TOML files in [`rooms/`](rooms/) ([`_template.toml`](rooms/_template.toml) documents\n112\tevery field). They are built into the executable and hot-reload from disk while the game runs.\n113\t\n114\t## Building\n115\t```sh\n116\tscripts/setup-linux.sh        # once: packages, lavapipe (software Vulkan), mingw, Wine\n117\tcargo run -p pav_app          # the game on Linux\n118\tcargo test                    # tests\n119\tscripts/build-windows.sh      # Windows .exe (cross-compiled)\n120\tscripts/build-web.sh          # browser build -> target/web (WebAssembly + WebGPU)\n121\tpython3 -m http.server -d target/web 8000   # then open http://localhost:8000\n122\t```\n123\t\n124\t## Browser build\n125\t\n126\tThere is no hosted site. For playing and testing, build one self-contained page:\n127\t\n128\t```sh\n129\tscripts/build-web.sh --single-file   # -> target/web/shardfall.html, runs from file://\n130\t```\n131\t\n132\tOr build the multi-file version and serve it:\n133\t\n134\t```sh\n135\tscripts/build-web.sh\n136\tpython3 -m http.server -d target/web 8000\n137\t```\n138\t\n139\tUse recent Chrome or Edge with WebGPU and hardware acceleration enabled. Add `?scene=world` to\n140\tthe address for the tech demo or `?room=drift` for a particular room. Saves stay in each browser's local\n141\tstorage; visiting a different site address uses a separate save.\n142\t\n143\t## Agent tools\n144\t`pav` runs the engine headless with one tool registry (load rooms, step, drive the player, tune\n145\tparameters, rewind, record and replay, capture screenshots, filmstrips and audio, benchmark):\n146\t```sh\n147\tcargo run -q -p pav_tools --bin pav -- help\n148\tprintf 'load scene=world\\ngoto room=drift\\ncapture out=out/drift.png\\n' | pav repl\n149\t```\n150\tThe same tools are an MCP server (`pav mcp`, registered in `.mcp.json`), and a live bridge into\n151\tthe running game: start it with `--bridge`, then use `pav live` (REPL) or `pav mcp --live`.\n152\tObject packs and their licences are credited in `assets/props/CREDITS.md`.\n153\t\n154\tFor building game content there are tools that make and judge it: grow a creature from a seed\n155\t(`genome`), author one as JSON and see it from every side with its measurements (`turntable\n156\tdef={...}`), roll loot tables (`loot_roll`), map any level with its mechanics and what the AI\n157\tthinks is walkable (`levelmap nav=true`), describe any depth (`level depth=40`), take a\n158\tscreenshot with numbered marks and a legend of what each mark is (`see`), and let a bot play\n159\tthe whole descent and report every level (`campaign`):\n160\t```sh\n161\tpav levelmap depth=7 nav=true out=out/level7.png\n162\tprintf 'load scene=town\\ncampaign from=1 to=12 wall=300\\n' | pav repl\n163\t```\n164\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"bd5df3df-af8f-423f-91bd-f13324a8d97a","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:14.366Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/README.md","content":"# Shardfall\n\nA procedural hack-and-slash adventure built in Rust and WebGPU, with a playable Pavilion\ntech demo. Explore Emberwatch and the endless Depths, or visit the pavilion's rooms to try\nmovement, physics, procedural animation, visual effects, rendering styles, and genre demos.\nEverything is generated from code, every parameter can be tuned live, and agents can drive\nthe game through the same tools a person uses.\n\nPlay Shardfall in the browser from one self-contained file: `scripts/build-web.sh\n--single-file` writes `target/web/shardfall.html`, which runs from disk (add `?scene=world` to\nits address for the Pavilion tech demo). Use recent Chrome or Edge with WebGPU and hardware\nacceleration enabled. Native builds: `scripts/build-windows.sh` (Windows `.exe`) and\n`cargo build --profile dist -p pav_app` (Linux, `target/dist/shardfall`). Phones and tablets play by touch (Chrome\non Android, Safari on iOS 26 or later): drag anywhere to move, attacks are automatic, and the\nbuttons in the corner dodge, cast and drink.\n\n- Make a game on the engine: [`docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`](docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md), an agent's guide\n  that starts from the starter game (`load scene=starter`, `crates/pav_core/src/games/`).\n- Agent handoff: [`START_HERE.md`](START_HERE.md) (routes and prompts). Run\n  `scripts/package-agent.sh` to create a standalone source archive and checksum in\n  `out/agent/`; `scripts/check-handoff.sh` checks it the way a receiving agent uses it.\n- [`pavilion-lite/`](pavilion-lite/): an older compact engine with a CPU renderer. Frozen: a\n  finished experiment, outside the doctrine because its renderer isn't WebGPU, and left out\n  of the handoff archive.\n- Design: [`docs/DESIGN.md`](docs/DESIGN.md). Current state: [`docs/PROGRESS.md`](docs/PROGRESS.md);\n  history and decisions: [`docs/HISTORY.md`](docs/HISTORY.md). Principles:\n  [`docs/DOCTRINE.md`](docs/DOCTRINE.md). Rules for agents working here: [`AGENTS.md`](AGENTS.md).\n\n## Live animation editing with an LLM\n\nRun `scripts/animation-studio.sh` or `shardfall.exe --animation-studio` to open Animation Studio.\nConnect your LLM with the MCP configuration in `.mcp.animation-studio.json`.\nThe LLM can create clips, copy existing animations, change key poses, and see the results through captured images.\nYou see each accepted edit in the game window, without a rebuild.\n\nThe studio includes playback, time and speed controls, source credits, and 32 levels of undo and redo.\nEdits save to readable JSON files. The preview uses the game's renderer and leaves the current game state intact.\nSee [`docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`](docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md) for setup, example prompts, and tool arguments.\n\n## The adventure\nA fast hack-and-slash built on the engine ([`docs/GAME.md`](docs/GAME.md)). `shardfall.exe`\nstarts in **Emberwatch**, the town: trade with Hilda the smith, gamble with Odo, buy stronger\npotions from Mother Wren, keep loot in the stash, and take the portal down. Twelve designed\nlevels each introduce one mechanic (shrines, powder kegs, spike plates, rift gates, wind,\ntotems, lava, ice, crumbling floors, darkness, cursed chests, time bubbles) and mix in the\nones before; after level 12 the Depths generate new combinations forever. The portal also\nleads to the Proving Grounds (wave arena) and the Menagerie (generated creatures). Your hero\nis saved next to the executable on every trip and on quit. The pavilion of engine rooms is\nstill there: Esc menu → Load scene → `world` (or `shardfall.exe --scene world`).\n\n| Key | Action |\n|---|---|\n| W A S D · mouse | move · aim |\n| Left / right mouse, Q E R F | skills (hold to repeat) · Shift: attack in place |\n| Space | dodge roll |\n| 1 | potion |\n| G | use: trade, stash, portal, gamble, brew, the way down, cursed chests |\n| I (Tab) · P · C · K · M | inventory · passive tree · character · skill bar · map |\n| T | town portal |\n| Esc | menu (difficulty sliders for play-testing, new hero, Look & filters) |\n\nItems drop as you fight (walk over magic+ items or click their names); hover an item to compare\nit with what you wear. Gamepad: X Y B RB LB RT skills, A dodge, D-pad up potion, D-pad right use,\nD-pad left map, D-pad down the hero's panels (LB / RB switch between inventory, character,\nskills and passive tree). With a window open, the stick moves a cursor: A clicks, X\nright-clicks, hold Y for shift (take a whole path in the tree), the right stick scrolls and\nzooms, B closes. Start opens the menu, which works the same way.\n\n## Playing\nWindows: run `shardfall.exe`. It writes `shardfall.toml` next to itself on first start (Vulkan by\ndefault; `backend = \"dx12\"` switches). `shardfall.exe --room drift` starts in a room of the\npavilion (`--scene world` for the pavilion itself).\nBrowser: serve the web build (below) and open it in a recent Chrome or Edge; add `?room=drift`\nto the address to start in a room.\n\n| Key | Action |\n|---|---|\n| W A S D | move (Shift: walk slowly) |\n| Space | jump (hold for higher) |\n| C / Ctrl | crouch (hold) · dodge roll while running |\n| Z | crawl (toggle) |\n| F / left click | throw a bomb, or fire the blaster in shooting rooms |\n| E | get in / out of a vehicle |\n| Backspace (hold) | rewind time |\n| Right-drag · wheel · 1-8 | rotate · zoom · camera presets |\n| Esc | menu (Look & filters: pixel art, cel shading, outlines, oil paint, halftone, ASCII, pencil, palettes, grading, haze... on the whole scene, the characters & objects or the environment) |\n| H | how the room works (the station guide); outside rooms the field guide |\n| F1 | tuning panel (every parameter, presets) |\n| F2 | jump to any room |\n| F4 / F5 | leave the room / reset it |\n| F3 · F12 | boot diagnostics · screenshot |\n\nGamepads work too. Each room shows an info card on entry (what it demonstrates, what to try) and\nits own controls. Every room is also a lesson: H (or the card's **How it works**) opens its\nstation guide with what you are seeing, how the engine does it step by step, live sliders for\nthe settings involved, where games use it, phrases to ask for it, what it costs, the engine's\nown code and the words to know; stepping on a pad shows what that pad changed and why. The\n**Field guide** (Esc menu) collects every word and every room's \"ask for it\" phrases.\n\n## Rooms\n| Wing (corridor) | Rooms |\n|---|---|\n| Movement & Feel Lab (east) | Playground, Feel Lab, Tightrope, Slalom, Timing Gates, Dodge Gauntlet, Camera Bench, Verticality Tower |\n| Physics Lab (north) | Stacking & Toppling, Springs & Soft Bodies, Chains & Rope Bridges, Conveyors, Bounce & Friction Gallery, Destructible Floors, Stress Test |\n| Animation Lab (west) | Walk Cycles, Jointed Limbs, Impact & Recoil, Squash & Stretch, Secondary Motion, Character Style Bench |\n| Visual Effects (south) | Lights & Shadows, Particle Garden, Bloom & Glow, Heat & Shockwaves, Global Illumination, Light Shafts, Wind & Water |\n| Styles & Filters (east) | Pure Styles, Style vs Filters, Filter Stack Bench, Mix & Match, Paint & Print, Screen Transitions |\n| Genre Wing (north) | Bullet Hell, Grid Stealth, Drift Circuit, Helicopter Run |\n| Workshop (south) | Sandbox (spawn, drag, delete and save objects) |\n\nRooms are TOML files in [`rooms/`](rooms/) ([`_template.toml`](rooms/_template.toml) documents\nevery field). They are built into the executable and hot-reload from disk while the game runs.\n\n## Building\n```sh\nscripts/setup-linux.sh        # once: packages, lavapipe (software Vulkan), mingw, Wine\ncargo run -p pav_app          # the game on Linux\ncargo test                    # tests\nscripts/build-windows.sh      # Windows .exe (cross-compiled)\nscripts/build-web.sh          # browser build -> target/web (WebAssembly + WebGPU)\npython3 -m http.server -d target/web 8000   # then open http://localhost:8000\n```\n\n## Browser build\n\nThere is no hosted site. For playing and testing, build one self-contained page:\n\n```sh\nscripts/build-web.sh --single-file   # -> target/web/shardfall.html, runs from file://\n```\n\nOr build the multi-file version and serve it:\n\n```sh\nscripts/build-web.sh\npython3 -m http.server -d target/web 8000\n```\n\nUse recent Chrome or Edge with WebGPU and hardware acceleration enabled. Add `?scene=world` to\nthe address for the tech demo or `?room=drift` for a particular room. Saves stay in each browser's local\nstorage; visiting a different site address uses a separate save.\n\n## Agent tools\n`pav` runs the engine headless with one tool registry (load rooms, step, drive the player, tune\nparameters, rewind, record and replay, capture screenshots, filmstrips and audio, benchmark):\n```sh\ncargo run -q -p pav_tools --bin pav -- help\nprintf 'load scene=world\\ngoto room=drift\\ncapture out=out/drift.png\\n' | pav repl\n```\nThe same tools are an MCP server (`pav mcp`, registered in `.mcp.json`), and a live bridge into\nthe running game: start it with `--bridge`, then use `pav live` (REPL) or `pav mcp --live`.\nObject packs and their licences are credited in `assets/props/CREDITS.md`.\n\nFor building game content there are tools that make and judge it: grow a creature from a seed\n(`genome`), author one as JSON and see it from every side with its measurements (`turntable\ndef={...}`), roll loot tables (`loot_roll`), map any level with its mechanics and what the AI\nthinks is walkable (`levelmap nav=true`), describe any depth (`level depth=40`), take a\nscreenshot with numbered marks and a legend of what each mark is (`see`), and let a bot play\nthe whole descent and report every level (`campaign`):\n```sh\npav levelmap depth=7 nav=true out=out/level7.png\nprintf 'load scene=town\\ncampaign from=1 to=12 wall=300\\n' | pav repl\n```\n","numLines":164,"startLine":1,"totalLines":164}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01PyoKtTb4ozVzNtaWrBwZ3q","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9GFM4j18NpZLXhakJJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Q5ayovTXunXSuHKW9H1fwD","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/AGENTS.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4853,"cache_read_input_tokens":25536,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4853},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"46ede8bf-5ab9-409a-8024-4c17ddaa6356","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:15.044Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9GF6g2Rhhjvmqb5q6u","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Q5ayovTXunXSuHKW9H1fwD":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/AGENTS.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q5ayovTXunXSuHKW9H1fwD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AGENTS.md — rulebook for coding agents\n2\t\n3\tMaking a new game on the engine? Read `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`: the \"Key rules\" below apply to\n4\tyour game, the maintainers' workflow (HISTORY, PROGRESS, PRs, escalations) doesn't.\n5\t\n6\tShardfall is a procedural hack-and-slash game built on the Pavilion engine, written in Rust. The\n7\tprinciples are in `docs/DOCTRINE.md`, which outranks everything else here. The current state is\n8\t`docs/PROGRESS.md` (one page). Read both before starting; Claude Code loads them, with this file,\n9\tthrough `CLAUDE.md`. The design is in `docs/DESIGN.md` (engine) and `docs/GAME.md` (Shardfall):\n10\tread them when your work touches the design. `docs/REFERENCE.md` holds the full code map, every\n11\ttool family with examples, the controls and what each test suite covers: open the section you\n12\tneed. The journal and past decisions are in `docs/HISTORY.md`, newest first: search it, don't\n13\tread it whole. For a standalone Linux source handoff, `START_HERE.md` covers setup and headless\n14\tCLI examples.\n15\t\n16\t## How the doctrine applies here\n17\tAdopted 2026-10-09, partway through development. Code from before then isn't rewritten to fit;\n18\tfix a mismatch when it gets in the way.\n19\t- **Platform (5, 10):** the development platform is Linux, where agents build and test. The\n20\t  user's Windows `.exe` and the browser build count as part of the target too: a problem the user\n21\t  can see gets fixed now. Replays are promised to match only on the machine that recorded them; to\n22\t  chase something the user saw, start from a snapshot taken just before it.\n23\t- **Renderer (6):** wgpu is the WebGPU implementation: Vulkan on Linux and Windows (DX12 as a\n24\t  Windows option), the browser's own WebGPU on the web, lavapipe where there's no GPU. GPU compute\n25\t  stays cosmetic. The game UI is egui.\n26\t- **API and internals (7, 8):** the game (`pav_core/src/arpg/`) still lives inside the engine\n27\t  crate, so the compiler doesn't enforce the line. The engine modules the game uses are its API:\n28\t  `sim`, `entity`, `frame`, `rng`, `input`, `params`, `statics`, `nav`, `zones`, `character`,\n29\t  `projectile`, `puppet`, `parts`, `moves`, `clips`, `shape`, `color`, `fxdef`, and `games` (the\n30\t  hook a new game implements: `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`). Everything else\n31\t  (renderer, physics wiring, synth, importers) is internals. If game work means reading\n32\t  internals, fix the API or the error message.\n33\t- **Versions (9):** current pins stay (Rust 1.98.1, wgpu 30, rapier 0.36, egui 0.36, glam 0.33,\n34\t  cpal 0.18). The rule applies to new dependencies and to upgrades.\n35\t- **Assets (4):** no image, model, texture or audio files; fonts are allowed. Animation and\n36\t  objects are readable text: key poses (`anim/*.json`, the format `clips.rs` documents, made with\n37\t  `clip_import`) and props made of named primitive parts (`assets/props`, `docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`).\n38\t  Raw downloads (.glb, .gltf, .obj, .fbx, .bvh, .amc) go to `.cache/` (git-ignored); never commit\n39\t  them, or images. Keep each source's credit and licence. Libraries whose licences forbid sharing\n40\t  what is made from them (Bandai Namco, LaFAN1, Mixamo) are translated only into the git-ignored\n41\t  local tiers (`anim/local`, `assets/props/local`).\n42\t- **Escalation:** ask in chat and log it in `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`. If you can schedule a check-in\n43\t  (e.g. `send_later`), set one for 15 minutes and carry on with other work; if not, make the call,\n44\t  log it and continue.\n45\t- **Pavilion Lite** (`pavilion-lite/`) is frozen: its renderer runs on the CPU, not WebGPU (6),\n46\t  so it isn't developed further (`docs/ESCALATIONS.md`).\n47\t\n48\t## Working rules\n49\tUnder the doctrine, in priority order:\n50\t1. **Gameplay first, then robustness.**\n51\t2. **Use tools.** If a tool does a job faster, more accurately or cheaper over time, use or build one.\n52\t3. **It must still run well on a modern PC** (the user's RTX 3060 Ti and 4070 Super). Other\n53\t   optimization and compatibility wait (10).\n54\t4. **Approximations are fine if they feel right.** Feel beats physical accuracy.\n55\t5. **Check selectively.** Verify what often breaks or is expensive to debug; skip routine checks\n56\t   of things that almost never break. \"If it's broken, the user will say so\" is valid.\n57\t6. **Cost over speed.** Prefer the CLI/capture tools over reading lots of code.\n58\t\n59\t## Testing budget (prototype stage)\n60\tEvery engine here is a prototype with one user: spend on finding what clearly helps, not on\n61\tmeasuring exactly how much. Completion comes first: a test decides how something is built and is\n62\trecorded; it never stops, skips or reverts planned work.\n63\t- **Test only to change a decision.** Before a test, name the choice it decides and its cost in\n64\t  US$ as Claude Code bills it, subagents included. If no choice rides on it, or a wrong choice\n65\t  would cost less than the test, skip it. Afterwards, report what it cost.\n66\t- **Size the test to the gap.** Agent runs (benchmarks, judges, eval agents) start small: 3-5 per\n67\t  arm. A clear gap decides it: 2:1 or better in wins, or 25% or more in cost, model calls or\n68\t  errors.\n69\t- **Near 50/50, make the call.** If the first batch is within 60/40 (or 10%), stop: the options\n70\t  are about equally good, so the choice matters little (Fredkin's paradox). Take the simpler,\n71\t  cheaper or more reversible option, log the call and why, and move on. Between clear and close:\n72\t  at most one more small batch, then decide.\n73\t- **Close calls belong to tool tests.** Checks with no model calls (cargo tests, `pav` checks and\n74\t  captures, scripted counts) cost almost nothing per run and repeat exactly, so they may scale up\n75\t  to settle narrow margins. Even then, don't chase gains under 5%; timing differences under 10%\n76\t  are noise.\n77\t- Looks and feel are the expensive, noisy kind: prefer objective proxies; use agent judges only for\n78\t  large, obvious differences, few of them, several items per call; leave fine visual judgement to\n79\t  the user on a quick comparison page.\n80\t- Explore broadly: test distinct hypotheses, not variations of one. Don't add or keep code without\n81\t  a verified benefit.\n82\t- Count cost end to end, in US$, not one tool's traffic: every model call re-reads the agent's\n83\t  whole context.\n84\t- Assets made through the documented loop (a brief, `check` to zero findings, a 4-view sheet at\n85\t  the game camera, at most two fix rounds, `publish` with a review) need no human review: the user\n86\t  accepted 28 of 28 (2026-10-12). Show the user only edge cases, or complex ones you are unsure of.\n87\t- Per PR (from 2026-10-16):\n88\t  - Run the full checks (tests of the touched crates, clippy, fmt, the wasm check when the app\n89\t    builds the change) once, on the head you merge.\n90\t  - After a rebase or a small fix, run only the tests of what changed. A docs-only PR needs none.\n91\t  - Merge as soon as the checks pass; don't wait for a review (Codex has posted none since #45 on\n92\t    2026-10-12). A review that arrives later: fix its real findings in a follow-up PR.\n93\t  - Nothing deploys: there is no hosted site. After a merge that can change the game, build the\n94\t    single-file HTML and send it to the user (`CLAUDE.md`, \"Builds for playing and testing\").\n95\t\n96\t## Architecture\n97\tCrates (the full map, module by module: `docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Code map\"):\n98\t- `pav_core`: the simulation, with no window, GPU or audio dependencies: `Sim`, entities, physics\n99\t  (rapier3d), the world, rooms and terrain, characters and puppets, moves and clips, AI and nav,\n100\t  props and materials; the game lives in `src/arpg/`.\n101\t- `pav_render`: the wgpu renderer: shadows, bloom and post effects, the filter stack, procedural\n102\t  meshes and materials, offscreen capture to PNG.\n103\t- `pav_view`: sim frame -> render scene: the camera rig, looks and filters, water, `emit_asset`.\n104\t- `pav_audio`: synthesized sound: cpal output, offline .wav rendering.\n105\t- `pav_tools`: the agent layer: the tool registry, the `pav` CLI and REPL, the MCP server, the\n106\t  live bridge client, and the motion and asset importers.\n107\t- `pav_ui`: Shardfall's egui UI (HUD, inventory, passive tree, touch controls), drawn by the\n108\t  window and, headless, by the tools (`ui`, captures).\n109\t- `pav_app`: the game (`shardfall` binary): window, input, sim thread, live bridge server, the\n110\t  pavilion's egui UI; native and wasm32.\n111\t\n112\tData: `anim/` (moves and motion clips), `rooms/` (room files, embedded in the exe and hot-reloaded;\n113\t`_template.toml` documents every field), `game/` (Shardfall's data), `assets/` (props, materials,\n114\tmodel catalogs), `store/` (the asset store's tables).\n115\tData flow: `Sim::step(InputFrame)` (fixed tick, own thread) -> `Sim::frame()` -> `RenderFrame`\n116\t-> `pav_view::ViewBuilder::build` (interpolates prev/curr) -> `pav_render::Scene` -> `Renderer`.\n117\t\n118\tKey rules:\n119\t- `pav_core` must never depend on graphics, windowing or audio crates.\n120\t- Everything that changes during play lives in `SimState` (cloning it = snapshot). Keep it `Clone`.\n121\t- Same inputs and seed, same state (doctrine 5): randomness comes from `rng`, seeded (the sim's\n122\t  `Rng`, or its hash for seed-derived content), never from the clock; don't let\n123\t  `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order reach game state (use a `BTreeMap` or sort).\n124\t- Gameplay code reads plain struct fields; expose tunables via `impl Tunable` (see `params.rs`).\n125\t  Tunables are automatically in the tuning panel, the `params`/`set` tools and preset files.\n126\t- Colors in data are sRGB hex (`Color::hex(\"#e8704a\")`); internally linear.\n127\t- Physics precision switch: in `crates/pav_core/Cargo.toml` change `package = \"rapier3d\"` to\n128\t  `\"rapier3d-f64\"`.\n129\t\n130\t## Build, run, test\n131\t```sh\n132\tscripts/setup-linux.sh             # once per machine (apt packages, lavapipe, mingw, wine)\n133\tcargo build                        # everything (Linux)\n134\tcargo test                         # every crate's tests (`-p pav_core` for one crate)\n135\tcargo fmt --all --check            # formatting\n136\tcargo clippy -p pav_core --all-targets -- -D warnings  # lints, for each crate you touched\n137\tcargo run -p pav_app               # the game (needs a display; Xvfb works with lavapipe)\n138\tscripts/build-windows.sh           # -> target/x86_64-pc-windows-gnu/dist/shardfall.exe\n139\tscripts/smoke-windows.sh 25        # run the .exe under Wine+lavapipe, screenshot + log in out/smoke/\n140\tscripts/smoke-linux.sh 10          # same for the native Linux build\n141\tscripts/build-web.sh               # browser build -> target/web/ (serve over HTTP; Chrome/Edge)\n142\tscripts/build-web.sh --single-file # -> target/web/shardfall.html: one ~10 MB page, runs from file://\n143\tscripts/check-wgsl.sh              # after any WGSL change: Chromium's Tint compiles every module\n144\t```\n145\t`--single-file` packs web/index.html, the JS glue and the gzipped wasm into one page with no\n146\tother loads (`scripts/pack_single_html.py`; `--title --description --theme-color --scene NAME --out PATH`).\n147\tBrowser build: `cfg(target_arch = \"wasm32\")` code lives in `pav_app` (no threads, async GPU\n148\tsetup); other crates only skip disk access there (the browser has no files) or pick its GPU\n149\tbackend. Check it with `cargo clippy -p pav_app --target wasm32-unknown-unknown`. Headless\n150\tChromium runs it only with the right flags (WebGPU on SwiftShader); those and phone emulation are\n151\tin `docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Browser and phones\".\n152\tDisk: cloud containers have a fixed allowance. Dev builds use line-table debug info and no\n153\tincremental cache; if the disk fills up anyway, delete stale binaries in `target/debug/deps`\n154\t(or run `cargo clean`).\n155\t\n156\t## Agent CLI (`pav`) and MCP\n157\tEvery tool works headless (captures use lavapipe when there is no GPU). The same tools are an\n158\tMCP server: `.mcp.json` registers `pav mcp` (stdio), so Claude Code agents in this repo get\n159\tthem as `mcp__shardfall__*` tools (captures come back as images). `pav help` lists every tool\n160\tand its arguments; `docs/REFERENCE.md` (\"Tools\") shows each family with examples.\n161\t```sh\n162\tcargo run -q -p pav_tools --bin pav -- help\n163\tpav bench ticks=1200                          # ticks/sec\n164\tpav capture scene=test ticks=300 out=out/a.png\n165\tprintf 'step ticks=200\\ncamera preset=top\\ncapture out=out/b.png\\n' | pav repl\n166\tprintf 'input move=[1,0] ticks=30\\ninput press=jump move=[0,1] ticks=40\\nplayer\\n' | pav repl\n167\t```\n168\t- One-shot calls accept `scene=`, `seed=` and `ticks=` to set up the session first. `pav repl`\n169\t  keeps one session across lines (`tool key=value ...` or JSON lines\n170\t  `{\"tool\":\"name\",\"args\":{...}}`; `#` = comment).\n171\t- `input` drives the player: `move=[x,z]` world direction (x = east, z = south), `hold=`/`press=`\n172\t  buttons (jump, crouch, crawl, use, focus, interact, primary), `aim=[x,y,z]`, `ticks=N`.\n173\t- Look at captures with your image-reading tool; prefer small sizes (e.g. `width=640 height=360`).\n174\t  `filmstrip` tiles N frames into one PNG: the cheapest way to check motion and animation.\n175\t- `record_save` + `replay` reproduce a session exactly (same machine/build) and check the hash:\n176\t  use them for bug reports.\n177\t- Live bridge: start the game with `--bridge` (or `bridge = \"127.0.0.1:7878\"` in shardfall.toml),\n178\t  then `pav live` is a REPL into the running game and `pav mcp --live` an MCP server for it. Same\n179\t  tools; they act on what is on screen (captures render the game's camera on a second device).\n180\t- Studios edit on the live bridge, each with its own doc: Animation (`shardfall\n181\t  --animation-studio`, `.mcp.animation-studio.json`, `docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`), Object\n182\t  (`--asset-studio`, `.mcp.studio.json`, `docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md` and `docs/ASSET_STUDIO_LIVE.md`;\n183\t  materials `docs/MATERIALS.md`) and Creature (`--creature-studio`, `.mcp.studio.json`,\n184\t  `docs/CREATURE_STUDIO.md`); `scripts/animation-studio.sh`, `asset-studio.sh` and\n185\t  `creature-studio.sh` build and open each. Pass the last reply's `revision` as `if_revision`,\n186\t  and copy a source clip or asset before changing it.\n187\t- Game data and rooms may name only assets every build carries: embedded (BUILTIN, LIB/core) or a\n188\t  shipped pack's (`props::store::SHIPPED`: the KayKit figures). Game code draws library assets\n189\t  from its own state with `pav_view::assets::emit_asset` (`docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`, \"Library\n190\t  assets in game code\").\n191\t- `tools/creature-compiler` vendors the exact SpawnForge pin: keep the pin and source hashes\n192\t  intact; don't resolve against a moving upstream checkout.\n193\t- Shardfall content: game data lives in `game/*.toml` (`game_reload` re-reads it live). Check a\n194\t  change with the tools: `see` (a screenshot with numbered marks and a legend), `levelmap`,\n195\t  `turntable`, `animsheet`, and `campaign from=1 to=12 wall=300` (the bot plays down through the\n196\t  levels and reports each; run it after data changes).\n197\t\n198\tAsset store (`docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md`): before making a prop or clip, `assets shelf=prop` (or `shelf=clip`) lists the best entries and `assets find=words` (`kind=clip` for motion) searches; a miss gives `none` and a route.\n199\tIn the Workshop wing, `library_hall` shows every standard object (generated: after publishing to core, rebuild and run `pav assets reindex=store` and `pav assets reindex=hall`), `limits` what the checks catch and miss, `catalogue` pages through every store row, `store_showcase` shows the materials; the animation wing's `clip_browser` plays every clip.\n200\t\n201\tTools: `scenes load step status entities npcs params set camera capture bench gpu player input\n202\tspawn despawn teleport rewind snapshot_save snapshot_load record_save replay rooms room goto\n203\troom_reset room_check room_reload stream signal filmstrip camera_bench course feel audio_capture`;\n204\tlooks `look`; teaching `guide`; games on the hook `play`; Shardfall `game hero monster autoplay skills game_reload\n205\tloot_roll give inventory game_cmd ui tree tree_map genome bestiary boss turntable animsheet level\n206\tlevelmap go goto_feature see campaign theme_swatch`; animation `clips clip_import mocap\n207\tanim_reload anim_edit anim_preview`; objects `assets asset_edit asset_preview asset_spawn\n208\tasset_import studio_status`; creatures `creature_catalog creature_edit creature_preview\n209\tcreature_status`.\n210\t\n211\t### Adding a tool\n212\t1. Write `fn t_name(s: &mut Session, a: &Args) -> Result<Output>` in the tool family's module\n213\t   (`asset_tools.rs`, `game_tools.rs`, ...); it rejects unknown arguments, naming the nearest.\n214\t2. Add a `Tool { .. }` entry to `TOOLS` in `tools.rs` (help ≤ 50 tokens, each argument ≤ 12\n215\t   words). It is then in the CLI, REPL and MCP.\n216\t3. Put enums and op shapes in `schema()`; a new asset kind is a `KIND_ROUTES` row, not a new tool.\n217\t4. Add actions that change what the live studio shows to `live_feedback::changes_studio`.\n218\t5. Add a clause to the `mcp.rs` instructions if agents should find the tool first.\n219\t6. Test it on `Session::new(\"empty\", 1)`.\n220\t\n221\t### Adding a room (preferred: data only, no rebuild)\n222\tCopy `rooms/_template.toml` to `rooms/<key>.toml` and edit; the template documents every field\n223\t(`rooms/feel_lab.toml` and `crates/pav_core/tests/physics_room.toml` are full examples). The room\n224\tis placed on its wing's corridor automatically. Every room is a station of the world demo and\n225\tteaches: give it a `[learn]` block and a `note` on every pad that changes something\n226\t(`pav_view/tests/learn.rs` checks them; `rooms/bloom.toml` is the model). Check it with\n227\t`pav room_check path=rooms/<key>.toml`; look at it with `pav capture scene=<key>` (the room alone,\n228\tfast) or `printf 'load scene=world\\ngoto room=<key>\\ncapture\\n' | pav repl`. The running game\n229\thot-reloads room files. New *mechanics* are Rust (character.rs / sim.rs behaviours / entity\n230\tfields); keep rooms as data wherever possible.\n231\t\n232\t### Scenes\n233\t`world` (pavilion + rooms + streaming wilderness), `world/<room>` (start in a room),\n234\t`<room key>` (that room alone, no terrain), `test`, `empty`, and Shardfall's `town` (the game\n235\tstarts here by default; the saved hero is loaded into any game scene), `arena`, `lab` (the\n236\tMenagerie of creatures) and `level/<n>` (depth n: 1-12 designed, then the endless Depths).\n237\tGames built on the hook (`pav_core::games::GAMES`) are scenes by their key: `starter` today.\n238\tCode scenes live in `scenes.rs` (Shardfall places in `arpg/scene.rs`).\n239\t\n240\t## Tests\n241\t`cargo test` runs every suite; `cargo test -p <crate> --test <suite>` runs one. Suites live in\n242\t`crates/*/tests/`, named for what they cover (`docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Tests\", describes each).\n243\t`pav_core/tests/gameplay.rs` and `movement.rs` are the cheapest way to catch feel regressions:\n244\textend them when you add movement features. `pav_view/tests/material_parity.rs` needs a GPU (or\n245\tlavapipe); `pav_tools/tests/import_khronos.rs` is opt-in (`-- --ignored`).\n246\t\n247\t## Conventions\n248\t- Rust stable pinned in `rust-toolchain.toml`; `Cargo.lock` committed; edition 2024.\n249\t- Finished work gets a dated entry at the top of `docs/HISTORY.md` (what changed, how it works,\n250\t  the decisions); then update `docs/PROGRESS.md`, which stays one page of current state.\n251\t- Decide anything that isn't genuinely the user's call; record the decision with its entry in\n252\t  `docs/HISTORY.md`. Escalate what the doctrine says to escalate, and log it in\n253\t  `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`.\n254\t- Finishing a task or milestone: commit, push, open a PR into `main`, merge it and delete the\n255\t  branch. This is standard procedure (the user's standing instruction, which counts as asking for\n256\t  the PR); don't ask first.\n257\t- The user plays the Windows build; the log file `shardfall.log` and `shardfall.toml` (startup\n258\t  settings, e.g. `backend = \"dx12\"`) sit next to the `.exe`.\n259\t- Keep this file short: under 32 KiB (Codex reads no further; `docs_limits.rs` checks it) and\n260\t  about 250 lines. Detail goes to `docs/REFERENCE.md` or a topic doc, with a pointer here.\n261\t\n262\t## Code Review Rules\n263\tFor reviewers (Codex when it runs, or `/code-review`). Flag a change that:\n264\t- breaks one of the \"Key rules\" under Architecture: a graphics, windowing or audio dependency in\n265\t  `pav_core`, play state outside a `Clone` `SimState`, results that can differ between runs on one\n266\t  machine (unseeded randomness, the clock, `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order);\n267\t- commits a binary asset other than a font, or content a licence keeps in the local tiers;\n268\t- makes threads, blocking calls or file access reachable on `wasm32`.\n269\tLeave style, wording and test coverage of routine code alone: this is a one-user prototype\n270\t(\"Working rules\" 5, \"Testing budget\").\n271\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"79e0747b-a9f2-41a5-84f5-ce8287750244","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:15.103Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/AGENTS.md","content":"# AGENTS.md — rulebook for coding agents\n\nMaking a new game on the engine? Read `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`: the \"Key rules\" below apply to\nyour game, the maintainers' workflow (HISTORY, PROGRESS, PRs, escalations) doesn't.\n\nShardfall is a procedural hack-and-slash game built on the Pavilion engine, written in Rust. The\nprinciples are in `docs/DOCTRINE.md`, which outranks everything else here. The current state is\n`docs/PROGRESS.md` (one page). Read both before starting; Claude Code loads them, with this file,\nthrough `CLAUDE.md`. The design is in `docs/DESIGN.md` (engine) and `docs/GAME.md` (Shardfall):\nread them when your work touches the design. `docs/REFERENCE.md` holds the full code map, every\ntool family with examples, the controls and what each test suite covers: open the section you\nneed. The journal and past decisions are in `docs/HISTORY.md`, newest first: search it, don't\nread it whole. For a standalone Linux source handoff, `START_HERE.md` covers setup and headless\nCLI examples.\n\n## How the doctrine applies here\nAdopted 2026-10-09, partway through development. Code from before then isn't rewritten to fit;\nfix a mismatch when it gets in the way.\n- **Platform (5, 10):** the development platform is Linux, where agents build and test. The\n  user's Windows `.exe` and the browser build count as part of the target too: a problem the user\n  can see gets fixed now. Replays are promised to match only on the machine that recorded them; to\n  chase something the user saw, start from a snapshot taken just before it.\n- **Renderer (6):** wgpu is the WebGPU implementation: Vulkan on Linux and Windows (DX12 as a\n  Windows option), the browser's own WebGPU on the web, lavapipe where there's no GPU. GPU compute\n  stays cosmetic. The game UI is egui.\n- **API and internals (7, 8):** the game (`pav_core/src/arpg/`) still lives inside the engine\n  crate, so the compiler doesn't enforce the line. The engine modules the game uses are its API:\n  `sim`, `entity`, `frame`, `rng`, `input`, `params`, `statics`, `nav`, `zones`, `character`,\n  `projectile`, `puppet`, `parts`, `moves`, `clips`, `shape`, `color`, `fxdef`, and `games` (the\n  hook a new game implements: `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`). Everything else\n  (renderer, physics wiring, synth, importers) is internals. If game work means reading\n  internals, fix the API or the error message.\n- **Versions (9):** current pins stay (Rust 1.98.1, wgpu 30, rapier 0.36, egui 0.36, glam 0.33,\n  cpal 0.18). The rule applies to new dependencies and to upgrades.\n- **Assets (4):** no image, model, texture or audio files; fonts are allowed. Animation and\n  objects are readable text: key poses (`anim/*.json`, the format `clips.rs` documents, made with\n  `clip_import`) and props made of named primitive parts (`assets/props`, `docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`).\n  Raw downloads (.glb, .gltf, .obj, .fbx, .bvh, .amc) go to `.cache/` (git-ignored); never commit\n  them, or images. Keep each source's credit and licence. Libraries whose licences forbid sharing\n  what is made from them (Bandai Namco, LaFAN1, Mixamo) are translated only into the git-ignored\n  local tiers (`anim/local`, `assets/props/local`).\n- **Escalation:** ask in chat and log it in `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`. If you can schedule a check-in\n  (e.g. `send_later`), set one for 15 minutes and carry on with other work; if not, make the call,\n  log it and continue.\n- **Pavilion Lite** (`pavilion-lite/`) is frozen: its renderer runs on the CPU, not WebGPU (6),\n  so it isn't developed further (`docs/ESCALATIONS.md`).\n\n## Working rules\nUnder the doctrine, in priority order:\n1. **Gameplay first, then robustness.**\n2. **Use tools.** If a tool does a job faster, more accurately or cheaper over time, use or build one.\n3. **It must still run well on a modern PC** (the user's RTX 3060 Ti and 4070 Super). Other\n   optimization and compatibility wait (10).\n4. **Approximations are fine if they feel right.** Feel beats physical accuracy.\n5. **Check selectively.** Verify what often breaks or is expensive to debug; skip routine checks\n   of things that almost never break. \"If it's broken, the user will say so\" is valid.\n6. **Cost over speed.** Prefer the CLI/capture tools over reading lots of code.\n\n## Testing budget (prototype stage)\nEvery engine here is a prototype with one user: spend on finding what clearly helps, not on\nmeasuring exactly how much. Completion comes first: a test decides how something is built and is\nrecorded; it never stops, skips or reverts planned work.\n- **Test only to change a decision.** Before a test, name the choice it decides and its cost in\n  US$ as Claude Code bills it, subagents included. If no choice rides on it, or a wrong choice\n  would cost less than the test, skip it. Afterwards, report what it cost.\n- **Size the test to the gap.** Agent runs (benchmarks, judges, eval agents) start small: 3-5 per\n  arm. A clear gap decides it: 2:1 or better in wins, or 25% or more in cost, model calls or\n  errors.\n- **Near 50/50, make the call.** If the first batch is within 60/40 (or 10%), stop: the options\n  are about equally good, so the choice matters little (Fredkin's paradox). Take the simpler,\n  cheaper or more reversible option, log the call and why, and move on. Between clear and close:\n  at most one more small batch, then decide.\n- **Close calls belong to tool tests.** Checks with no model calls (cargo tests, `pav` checks and\n  captures, scripted counts) cost almost nothing per run and repeat exactly, so they may scale up\n  to settle narrow margins. Even then, don't chase gains under 5%; timing differences under 10%\n  are noise.\n- Looks and feel are the expensive, noisy kind: prefer objective proxies; use agent judges only for\n  large, obvious differences, few of them, several items per call; leave fine visual judgement to\n  the user on a quick comparison page.\n- Explore broadly: test distinct hypotheses, not variations of one. Don't add or keep code without\n  a verified benefit.\n- Count cost end to end, in US$, not one tool's traffic: every model call re-reads the agent's\n  whole context.\n- Assets made through the documented loop (a brief, `check` to zero findings, a 4-view sheet at\n  the game camera, at most two fix rounds, `publish` with a review) need no human review: the user\n  accepted 28 of 28 (2026-10-12). Show the user only edge cases, or complex ones you are unsure of.\n- Per PR (from 2026-10-16):\n  - Run the full checks (tests of the touched crates, clippy, fmt, the wasm check when the app\n    builds the change) once, on the head you merge.\n  - After a rebase or a small fix, run only the tests of what changed. A docs-only PR needs none.\n  - Merge as soon as the checks pass; don't wait for a review (Codex has posted none since #45 on\n    2026-10-12). A review that arrives later: fix its real findings in a follow-up PR.\n  - Nothing deploys: there is no hosted site. After a merge that can change the game, build the\n    single-file HTML and send it to the user (`CLAUDE.md`, \"Builds for playing and testing\").\n\n## Architecture\nCrates (the full map, module by module: `docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Code map\"):\n- `pav_core`: the simulation, with no window, GPU or audio dependencies: `Sim`, entities, physics\n  (rapier3d), the world, rooms and terrain, characters and puppets, moves and clips, AI and nav,\n  props and materials; the game lives in `src/arpg/`.\n- `pav_render`: the wgpu renderer: shadows, bloom and post effects, the filter stack, procedural\n  meshes and materials, offscreen capture to PNG.\n- `pav_view`: sim frame -> render scene: the camera rig, looks and filters, water, `emit_asset`.\n- `pav_audio`: synthesized sound: cpal output, offline .wav rendering.\n- `pav_tools`: the agent layer: the tool registry, the `pav` CLI and REPL, the MCP server, the\n  live bridge client, and the motion and asset importers.\n- `pav_ui`: Shardfall's egui UI (HUD, inventory, passive tree, touch controls), drawn by the\n  window and, headless, by the tools (`ui`, captures).\n- `pav_app`: the game (`shardfall` binary): window, input, sim thread, live bridge server, the\n  pavilion's egui UI; native and wasm32.\n\nData: `anim/` (moves and motion clips), `rooms/` (room files, embedded in the exe and hot-reloaded;\n`_template.toml` documents every field), `game/` (Shardfall's data), `assets/` (props, materials,\nmodel catalogs), `store/` (the asset store's tables).\nData flow: `Sim::step(InputFrame)` (fixed tick, own thread) -> `Sim::frame()` -> `RenderFrame`\n-> `pav_view::ViewBuilder::build` (interpolates prev/curr) -> `pav_render::Scene` -> `Renderer`.\n\nKey rules:\n- `pav_core` must never depend on graphics, windowing or audio crates.\n- Everything that changes during play lives in `SimState` (cloning it = snapshot). Keep it `Clone`.\n- Same inputs and seed, same state (doctrine 5): randomness comes from `rng`, seeded (the sim's\n  `Rng`, or its hash for seed-derived content), never from the clock; don't let\n  `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order reach game state (use a `BTreeMap` or sort).\n- Gameplay code reads plain struct fields; expose tunables via `impl Tunable` (see `params.rs`).\n  Tunables are automatically in the tuning panel, the `params`/`set` tools and preset files.\n- Colors in data are sRGB hex (`Color::hex(\"#e8704a\")`); internally linear.\n- Physics precision switch: in `crates/pav_core/Cargo.toml` change `package = \"rapier3d\"` to\n  `\"rapier3d-f64\"`.\n\n## Build, run, test\n```sh\nscripts/setup-linux.sh             # once per machine (apt packages, lavapipe, mingw, wine)\ncargo build                        # everything (Linux)\ncargo test                         # every crate's tests (`-p pav_core` for one crate)\ncargo fmt --all --check            # formatting\ncargo clippy -p pav_core --all-targets -- -D warnings  # lints, for each crate you touched\ncargo run -p pav_app               # the game (needs a display; Xvfb works with lavapipe)\nscripts/build-windows.sh           # -> target/x86_64-pc-windows-gnu/dist/shardfall.exe\nscripts/smoke-windows.sh 25        # run the .exe under Wine+lavapipe, screenshot + log in out/smoke/\nscripts/smoke-linux.sh 10          # same for the native Linux build\nscripts/build-web.sh               # browser build -> target/web/ (serve over HTTP; Chrome/Edge)\nscripts/build-web.sh --single-file # -> target/web/shardfall.html: one ~10 MB page, runs from file://\nscripts/check-wgsl.sh              # after any WGSL change: Chromium's Tint compiles every module\n```\n`--single-file` packs web/index.html, the JS glue and the gzipped wasm into one page with no\nother loads (`scripts/pack_single_html.py`; `--title --description --theme-color --scene NAME --out PATH`).\nBrowser build: `cfg(target_arch = \"wasm32\")` code lives in `pav_app` (no threads, async GPU\nsetup); other crates only skip disk access there (the browser has no files) or pick its GPU\nbackend. Check it with `cargo clippy -p pav_app --target wasm32-unknown-unknown`. Headless\nChromium runs it only with the right flags (WebGPU on SwiftShader); those and phone emulation are\nin `docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Browser and phones\".\nDisk: cloud containers have a fixed allowance. Dev builds use line-table debug info and no\nincremental cache; if the disk fills up anyway, delete stale binaries in `target/debug/deps`\n(or run `cargo clean`).\n\n## Agent CLI (`pav`) and MCP\nEvery tool works headless (captures use lavapipe when there is no GPU). The same tools are an\nMCP server: `.mcp.json` registers `pav mcp` (stdio), so Claude Code agents in this repo get\nthem as `mcp__shardfall__*` tools (captures come back as images). `pav help` lists every tool\nand its arguments; `docs/REFERENCE.md` (\"Tools\") shows each family with examples.\n```sh\ncargo run -q -p pav_tools --bin pav -- help\npav bench ticks=1200                          # ticks/sec\npav capture scene=test ticks=300 out=out/a.png\nprintf 'step ticks=200\\ncamera preset=top\\ncapture out=out/b.png\\n' | pav repl\nprintf 'input move=[1,0] ticks=30\\ninput press=jump move=[0,1] ticks=40\\nplayer\\n' | pav repl\n```\n- One-shot calls accept `scene=`, `seed=` and `ticks=` to set up the session first. `pav repl`\n  keeps one session across lines (`tool key=value ...` or JSON lines\n  `{\"tool\":\"name\",\"args\":{...}}`; `#` = comment).\n- `input` drives the player: `move=[x,z]` world direction (x = east, z = south), `hold=`/`press=`\n  buttons (jump, crouch, crawl, use, focus, interact, primary), `aim=[x,y,z]`, `ticks=N`.\n- Look at captures with your image-reading tool; prefer small sizes (e.g. `width=640 height=360`).\n  `filmstrip` tiles N frames into one PNG: the cheapest way to check motion and animation.\n- `record_save` + `replay` reproduce a session exactly (same machine/build) and check the hash:\n  use them for bug reports.\n- Live bridge: start the game with `--bridge` (or `bridge = \"127.0.0.1:7878\"` in shardfall.toml),\n  then `pav live` is a REPL into the running game and `pav mcp --live` an MCP server for it. Same\n  tools; they act on what is on screen (captures render the game's camera on a second device).\n- Studios edit on the live bridge, each with its own doc: Animation (`shardfall\n  --animation-studio`, `.mcp.animation-studio.json`, `docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`), Object\n  (`--asset-studio`, `.mcp.studio.json`, `docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md` and `docs/ASSET_STUDIO_LIVE.md`;\n  materials `docs/MATERIALS.md`) and Creature (`--creature-studio`, `.mcp.studio.json`,\n  `docs/CREATURE_STUDIO.md`); `scripts/animation-studio.sh`, `asset-studio.sh` and\n  `creature-studio.sh` build and open each. Pass the last reply's `revision` as `if_revision`,\n  and copy a source clip or asset before changing it.\n- Game data and rooms may name only assets every build carries: embedded (BUILTIN, LIB/core) or a\n  shipped pack's (`props::store::SHIPPED`: the KayKit figures). Game code draws library assets\n  from its own state with `pav_view::assets::emit_asset` (`docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`, \"Library\n  assets in game code\").\n- `tools/creature-compiler` vendors the exact SpawnForge pin: keep the pin and source hashes\n  intact; don't resolve against a moving upstream checkout.\n- Shardfall content: game data lives in `game/*.toml` (`game_reload` re-reads it live). Check a\n  change with the tools: `see` (a screenshot with numbered marks and a legend), `levelmap`,\n  `turntable`, `animsheet`, and `campaign from=1 to=12 wall=300` (the bot plays down through the\n  levels and reports each; run it after data changes).\n\nAsset store (`docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md`): before making a prop or clip, `assets shelf=prop` (or `shelf=clip`) lists the best entries and `assets find=words` (`kind=clip` for motion) searches; a miss gives `none` and a route.\nIn the Workshop wing, `library_hall` shows every standard object (generated: after publishing to core, rebuild and run `pav assets reindex=store` and `pav assets reindex=hall`), `limits` what the checks catch and miss, `catalogue` pages through every store row, `store_showcase` shows the materials; the animation wing's `clip_browser` plays every clip.\n\nTools: `scenes load step status entities npcs params set camera capture bench gpu player input\nspawn despawn teleport rewind snapshot_save snapshot_load record_save replay rooms room goto\nroom_reset room_check room_reload stream signal filmstrip camera_bench course feel audio_capture`;\nlooks `look`; teaching `guide`; games on the hook `play`; Shardfall `game hero monster autoplay skills game_reload\nloot_roll give inventory game_cmd ui tree tree_map genome bestiary boss turntable animsheet level\nlevelmap go goto_feature see campaign theme_swatch`; animation `clips clip_import mocap\nanim_reload anim_edit anim_preview`; objects `assets asset_edit asset_preview asset_spawn\nasset_import studio_status`; creatures `creature_catalog creature_edit creature_preview\ncreature_status`.\n\n### Adding a tool\n1. Write `fn t_name(s: &mut Session, a: &Args) -> Result<Output>` in the tool family's module\n   (`asset_tools.rs`, `game_tools.rs`, ...); it rejects unknown arguments, naming the nearest.\n2. Add a `Tool { .. }` entry to `TOOLS` in `tools.rs` (help ≤ 50 tokens, each argument ≤ 12\n   words). It is then in the CLI, REPL and MCP.\n3. Put enums and op shapes in `schema()`; a new asset kind is a `KIND_ROUTES` row, not a new tool.\n4. Add actions that change what the live studio shows to `live_feedback::changes_studio`.\n5. Add a clause to the `mcp.rs` instructions if agents should find the tool first.\n6. Test it on `Session::new(\"empty\", 1)`.\n\n### Adding a room (preferred: data only, no rebuild)\nCopy `rooms/_template.toml` to `rooms/<key>.toml` and edit; the template documents every field\n(`rooms/feel_lab.toml` and `crates/pav_core/tests/physics_room.toml` are full examples). The room\nis placed on its wing's corridor automatically. Every room is a station of the world demo and\nteaches: give it a `[learn]` block and a `note` on every pad that changes something\n(`pav_view/tests/learn.rs` checks them; `rooms/bloom.toml` is the model). Check it with\n`pav room_check path=rooms/<key>.toml`; look at it with `pav capture scene=<key>` (the room alone,\nfast) or `printf 'load scene=world\\ngoto room=<key>\\ncapture\\n' | pav repl`. The running game\nhot-reloads room files. New *mechanics* are Rust (character.rs / sim.rs behaviours / entity\nfields); keep rooms as data wherever possible.\n\n### Scenes\n`world` (pavilion + rooms + streaming wilderness), `world/<room>` (start in a room),\n`<room key>` (that room alone, no terrain), `test`, `empty`, and Shardfall's `town` (the game\nstarts here by default; the saved hero is loaded into any game scene), `arena`, `lab` (the\nMenagerie of creatures) and `level/<n>` (depth n: 1-12 designed, then the endless Depths).\nGames built on the hook (`pav_core::games::GAMES`) are scenes by their key: `starter` today.\nCode scenes live in `scenes.rs` (Shardfall places in `arpg/scene.rs`).\n\n## Tests\n`cargo test` runs every suite; `cargo test -p <crate> --test <suite>` runs one. Suites live in\n`crates/*/tests/`, named for what they cover (`docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Tests\", describes each).\n`pav_core/tests/gameplay.rs` and `movement.rs` are the cheapest way to catch feel regressions:\nextend them when you add movement features. `pav_view/tests/material_parity.rs` needs a GPU (or\nlavapipe); `pav_tools/tests/import_khronos.rs` is opt-in (`-- --ignored`).\n\n## Conventions\n- Rust stable pinned in `rust-toolchain.toml`; `Cargo.lock` committed; edition 2024.\n- Finished work gets a dated entry at the top of `docs/HISTORY.md` (what changed, how it works,\n  the decisions); then update `docs/PROGRESS.md`, which stays one page of current state.\n- Decide anything that isn't genuinely the user's call; record the decision with its entry in\n  `docs/HISTORY.md`. Escalate what the doctrine says to escalate, and log it in\n  `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`.\n- Finishing a task or milestone: commit, push, open a PR into `main`, merge it and delete the\n  branch. This is standard procedure (the user's standing instruction, which counts as asking for\n  the PR); don't ask first.\n- The user plays the Windows build; the log file `shardfall.log` and `shardfall.toml` (startup\n  settings, e.g. `backend = \"dx12\"`) sit next to the `.exe`.\n- Keep this file short: under 32 KiB (Codex reads no further; `docs_limits.rs` checks it) and\n  about 250 lines. Detail goes to `docs/REFERENCE.md` or a topic doc, with a pointer here.\n\n## Code Review Rules\nFor reviewers (Codex when it runs, or `/code-review`). Flag a change that:\n- breaks one of the \"Key rules\" under Architecture: a graphics, windowing or audio dependency in\n  `pav_core`, play state outside a `Clone` `SimState`, results that can differ between runs on one\n  machine (unseeded randomness, the clock, `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order);\n- commits a binary asset other than a font, or content a licence keeps in the local tiers;\n- makes threads, blocking calls or file access reachable on `wasm32`.\nLeave style, wording and test coverage of routine code alone: this is a one-user prototype\n(\"Working rules\" 5, \"Testing budget\").\n","numLines":271,"startLine":1,"totalLines":271}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Q5ayovTXunXSuHKW9H1fwD","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
