{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9GzcpFKqm1mR28uZLr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MgLEVHdeQqP28ZtLEoGLit","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/docs/REFERENCE.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16843,"cache_read_input_tokens":47614,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16843},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"8b76734b-0928-45e8-9bde-24489683546c","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:26.113Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9GzNvfDE9m8V8KHUXs","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01MgLEVHdeQqP28ZtLEoGLit":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/docs/REFERENCE.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MgLEVHdeQqP28ZtLEoGLit","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Reference\n2\t\n3\tThe detail behind `AGENTS.md`, read on demand: open the section you need, not the whole page.\n4\t`pav help` lists every tool with its arguments.\n5\t- [Code map](#code-map): what each crate and data folder holds.\n6\t- [Studios](#studios): animation, objects and creatures, edited live.\n7\t- [Tools](#tools): each tool family with examples.\n8\t- [Browser and phones](#browser-and-phones): running the web build headless, emulating a phone.\n9\t- [Controls](#controls): keyboard, gamepad, touch and the fixed system keys.\n10\t- [Tests](#tests): what each suite covers.\n11\t\n12\t## Code map\n13\t```\n14\tcrates/\n15\t  pav_core    simulation, no window/GPU/audio deps: Sim, entities, physics (rapier3d),\n16\t              static regions (statics.rs: terrain chunks, rooms, hub; active or dormant;\n17\t              blocks, decor and baked library props: drawn by the view's region cache, one\n18\t              collider box each, no entity),\n19\t              world.rs (pavilion layout, streaming, room tracking), room.rs (room files),\n20\t              terrain.rs (procedural wilderness), tile levels (level.rs), characters\n21\t              (character.rs: movement models instant/momentum/grid/committed, jump,\n22\t              crouch/crawl, ladders, ledge grab/mantle, swimming, axis lock, moving platforms,\n23\t              knockback, pushing shares momentum, bombs, ice slide), puppet (puppet.rs: body\n24\t              plans biped/spider/lizard/beetle/blob/quadruped, procedural animation, hit recoil,\n25\t              creature lunges, foot IK, legs that stride the way the body travels, idle breathing\n26\t              and blinks, cutout look and camera cheats; the biped is a `Skel` of joints that the\n27\t              procedural animation, moves and clips each produce and `dress` turns into parts),\n28\t              moves.rs (attacks and gestures as data, anim/moves.toml: arcs around the shoulders,\n29\t              wind-up from wherever the hands were, strikes with lunge/lean/twist/hop/spin,\n30\t              follow-through; skills name a move, a look may name its own per skill and combo\n31\t              swing: `attack_moves`), clips.rs (motion clips: readable key poses\n32\t              translated from open animation libraries, retargeted onto the biped with IK,\n33\t              crossfaded over the procedural animation, upper body only or mirrored; performers,\n34\t              idle clips, walk clips played as fast as the character moves), anim.rs (the /anim files: embedded, live reload), rig.rs (creature feet that plant and step,\n35\t              follow-the-leader spines, verlet tails/antennae), ai.rs (NPC brains: idle,\n36\t              wander, patrol, circle, follow; they drive characters through InputFrames),\n37\t              nav.rs (walkable grid from blocks and props, A* paths, flow fields),\n38\t              games/ (the hook a new game implements, `Game`, and `GAMES`, the list that\n39\t              makes each one a scene; starter.rs + starter.toml, the template:\n40\t              docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md),\n41\t              fxdef.rs (object lights / particle emitters / distortion, view-only),\n42\t              vehicle.rs (drift car on rapier's raycast vehicle, arcade helicopter: flight only,\n43\t              no weapons, damage or fuel), health.rs (shootable rapier objects: hp, score, boss\n44\t              phases/bar, finish),\n45\t              stealth.rs (guard sight cones with line of sight and an alert meter),\n46\t              zones.rs (trigger zones + labels), course.rs (timers, gates, checkpoints, pits,\n47\t              pads, camera cues, hits/respawn), behaviors.rs (movers, rotators, emitters,\n48\t              spawners), projectile.rs (lightweight bullets), feel.rs (feel metrics),\n49\t              softbody.rs (rapier soft bodies: jelly, balls, cloth, ropes), joints.rs (joints\n50\t              owned by entities, recreated on wake), destruct.rs (crumbling/breakable tiles,\n51\t              conveyors and bounce pads for props), history, params\n52\t  pav_render  wgpu renderer (Vulkan/DX12): Scene description -> particles (compute) -> sun +\n53\t              point-light shadow passes (shadows.rs) -> MSAA scene pass (+ GPU particles) ->\n54\t              bloom chain and distortion (fx.rs) -> composite (outlines, screen-space GI,\n55\t              tonemap, filter stack: pixelate/CRT/scanlines/dither/palettes/grading, split;\n56\t              pixel art on part of the scene: objects flagged `flags::PIXEL`, chosen in the view\n57\t              by `view.filter.pixel_target` = all/characters/hero/others/world/entity/objects/\n58\t              environment; every filter can aim at a part: `flags::OBJECT` marks characters &\n59\t              objects, the normal buffer carries it, `*_on` = all/objects/environment; painterly and\n60\t              print styles: Kuwahara oil paint, CMYK halftone, ASCII, pencil sketch (`filter.stylize`);\n61\t              hazy air: volumetric sun shafts ray-marched through the sun's shadow map and lamp\n62\t              halos in closed form (`view.haze`, `shafts`, `halos`); screen transitions (iris,\n63\t              diamonds, dissolve, mosaic, blinds, fade); wind in the vertex shader for instances\n64\t              flagged `flags::SWAY` (leaves) / `flags::GRASS` (tufts that part around the player)).\n65\t              Procedural meshes + analytic SDF spheres/capsules/rounded cones, SDF-font text in\n66\t              the world (text.rs). Procedural materials: rows of a storage buffer read by one\n67\t              kernel library (shaders/materials.wgsl, matched pixel for pixel by\n68\t              `pav_core::material::eval`); `pav_view::materials` interns them into slots for\n69\t              prop parts, static blocks (world-mapped) and decor. `render_scale` draws below the target's resolution and\n70\t              upscale.rs stretches it over (phones). Offscreen capture -> PNG.\n71\t  pav_view    sim frame -> render Scene: camera rig (tilt/yaw/distance/fov/ortho, all live),\n72\t              look.rs + looks.toml (the Look & Filters layer: filter sections, presets, whole looks),\n73\t              water.rs (water zones ripple: a CPU wave equation on a height grid, stirred by\n74\t              whatever crosses the waterline, splashes, blasts and rain; drawn as a dynamic mesh),\n75\t              assets.rs (`emit_asset`: a library asset drawn from game state each frame),\n76\t              interpolation between ticks, visual settings (ViewSettings)\n77\t  pav_audio   synthesized sound: oscillators/noise/envelopes/filters, event -> sound bank,\n78\t              cpal output (optional), offline .wav rendering\n79\t  pav_tools   agent layer: tool registry + `pav` CLI (one-shot, REPL) + MCP stdio server +\n80\t              live bridge client (bridge.rs), and the motion importer (mocap/: glTF and FBX\n81\t              libraries, CMU's ASF/AMC, BVH; Rigify, Unreal, Mixamo/MotionBuilder, 100STYLE and\n82\t              Bandai Namco skeletons; loops cut at their best cycle; fitted into readable key\n83\t              poses, number for number what my-3D2dge's own importer writes; the whole CMU\n84\t              database surveyed and translated), and the asset importer (import/: what both\n85\t              importers share, one `Scene` read from glTF/OBJ, normalisation, translation into\n86\t              props with fidelity metrics, the licence gate that routes packs to lib/ or\n87\t              local/; `asset_import`, `docs/ASSET_IMPORT.md`)\n88\t  pav_app     the game (`shardfall` binary): window, boot diagnostics, input (keyboard/mouse,\n89\t              gamepad via gilrs), system keys, tuning panel, pause menu, sim thread\n90\t              (simhost.rs; stepped from the frame loop in the browser), live bridge server\n91\t              (bridge.rs), egui input (uiinput.rs), graphics quality (quality.rs: render\n92\t              scale, dynamic resolution). Builds natively and for wasm32 (WebGPU).\n93\t  pav_ui      Shardfall's game UI in egui, drawn by the window and by the tools: the HUD\n94\t              (arpg_ui.rs: orbs, skill bar, banners, minimap), the windows (arpg_items.rs:\n95\t              inventory, shop, stash, character, skills; arpg_tree.rs: the passive tree), touch\n96\t              screens (touch.rs: finger routing, invisible stick, button fan, auto-attack,\n97\t              swipe-to-dismiss), and headless.rs (the same UI on a windowless context: a text\n98\t              reading by window, clicks, and an egui-wgpu overlay painted over captures).\n99\tanim/         animation data, embedded and hot-reloadable (`anim_reload`): moves.toml (the moves table)\n100\t              and clip sets (*.json: QUATERNIUS, MESH2MOTION, CMU, STYLE100: 437 clips with credits).\n101\t              anim/cmu/ holds every take of the CMU database (4,770 clips) and anim/100style/ the\n102\t              100STYLE runs, backward walks and runs, sidesteps and idles (780 loops), on disk\n103\t              only: `clips load=cmu`, `clips load=100style`. anim/local/ (git-ignored) holds what\n104\t              is translated from libraries whose licences forbid sharing it (Bandai Namco, LaFAN1,\n105\t              Mixamo): `clips load=local`. anim/catalogs/ says how each set is made (tags,\n106\t              descriptions, sources, picks): `clip_import catalog=...` rebuilds it from the\n107\t              libraries.\n108\trooms/        room data files (TOML: info card, station guide `[learn]`, wing, primary device, movement model, camera,\n109\t              params, keys, entrance, ASCII tile layers + legend, [[object]]s). Every file\n110\t              here is embedded in the exe at build time AND hot-reloaded at runtime.\n111\t              `_template.toml` documents every field.\n112\t```\n113\t\n114\t## Studios\n115\tAnimation Studio: `scripts/animation-studio.sh` or `shardfall --animation-studio` opens an\n116\tisolated preview and the live bridge. Use `.mcp.animation-studio.json` for visible MCP edits.\n117\t`anim_edit` creates/copies/inspects/edits clips, with revisions and 32-step undo/redo;\n118\t`anim_preview` plays, pauses, scrubs, and inspects their poses. Every accepted edit saves and\n119\tupdates the live library. The preview preserves the game state. Native animation files also\n120\treload automatically. See `docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`. REPLs accept structured JSON lines:\n121\t`{\"tool\":\"anim_edit\",\"args\":{\"action\":\"key\",\"name\":\"WORKSHOP/Wave\",\"time\":0.5,\"pose\":{\"armR\":[80,20,50,25,0]}}}`.\n122\tUse the returned `revision` as `if_revision` on later edits. Copy source clips before editing;\n123\tkeep local source copies and their history in `anim/local`.\n124\t\n125\tObject Studio: `scripts/asset-studio.sh` or `shardfall --asset-studio` opens reusable prop\n126\tauthoring on the same live bridge (`.mcp.studio.json`). `assets find=words` searches templates\n127\t(`BUILTIN/`), library packs (`LIB/pack/name`; `LIB/core` is embedded in every build, other packs\n128\tload from disk natively, `assets/props/local/` is git-ignored) and saved definitions, ranked,\n129\twith `category= tags= tier= similar=` filters. `asset_edit` creates/copies/inspects/patches\n130\treadable JSON with stable part names, atomic batches, revisions, and 32-step history.\n131\t`asset_preview` opens the isolated stage; `views=true` returns a framed 4-view contact sheet\n132\t(`figure=true` adds a 1.8 m figure) and leaves the stage and camera as they were. `capture stats=only` returns render cost without\n133\tan image. `asset_spawn` places one-root instances in the preserved scene. Edits refresh visuals\n134\tand compound collision together. Native file changes reload after 250 ms of quiet. Saved\n135\tdefinitions are `assets/props/workshop/*.json` (`PAV_ASSETS` overrides the props root).\n136\t`studio_status ticket=N` confirms frame submission for live edits without a capture. The\n137\tAnimations / Objects / Game tabs retain each workspace's controls and camera. See\n138\t`docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md` (tools and format) and `docs/ASSET_STUDIO_LIVE.md` (the live studio).\n139\tKeep procedural parts as text; use `if_revision` and batch related part changes in one `ops`\n140\tarray. Materials (planks, brick, cobbles … as per-pixel recipes, `docs/MATERIALS.md`): parts say\n141\t`\"mat\":\"MAT/oak_planks\"` or a key in the asset's `materials`; `kind=material` on `assets`,\n142\t`asset_edit` (workshop materials `MAT/workshop/x` in `assets/materials/workshop/`, `PAV_MATERIALS`\n143\toverrides the root; revisions `mat1-`) and `asset_preview` (a swatch with each layer's legibility\n144\tat the game camera). Game code draws units and scenery from its own state with library assets,\n145\tone call per thing per frame: `pav_view::assets::emit_asset(scene, \"BUILTIN/crate\",\n146\tPlacement::at(pos).yaw(deg).id(id), seed, &palette)` (no physics needed; `docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`,\n147\t\"Library assets in game code\"). Game data and rooms may name only assets every build carries\n148\t(embedded, or a shipped pack's: `props::store::SHIPPED`).\n149\t\n150\tCreature Studio: `scripts/creature-studio.sh` or `shardfall --creature-studio` opens the\n151\tprocedural creature stage. `tools/creature-compiler` vendors the exact SpawnForge core/modules\n152\tpin and bundles it with `npm ci --ignore-scripts && npm run build` (run in that directory).\n153\tKeep the pin and source hashes intact; do not resolve against a moving upstream checkout.\n154\t`creature_catalog`, `creature_edit`, `creature_preview`, and `creature_status` expose theme,\n155\ttemplate, blueprint, named-part and surface authoring through the same MCP bridge. Edits\n156\tqueue asynchronous builds. Wait for `published`, `failed`, or `superseded`; only publication\n157\tgets a frame ticket. Use the new source `revision` for the next edit, and `studio_status`\n158\tfor actual frame submission. Failed/obsolete builds keep the last valid native mesh.\n159\tSources, history and disposable caches are under `assets/creatures` (`PAV_CREATURES`\n160\toverrides it). Node runs outside the simulation. The Windows package includes the bundle\n161\tand portable Node. See `docs/CREATURE_STUDIO.md` for native material limits and the tool\n162\tcontract. This is an authoring/preview stage; gameplay spawning of these rigs is separate.\n163\t\n164\t## Tools\n165\t- Import: `asset_import` alone lists the open model libraries; `get=kenney/mini_dungeon` downloads\n166\t  a pack its catalog pins (`assets/catalogs/<lib>.json`) into .cache/assets, checked by SHA-256;\n167\t  `from=<model> list=true` summarises a .glb/.gltf/.obj (parts, triangles, size in cm, materials,\n168\t  main colours, warnings); `from=<model|folder|lib/pack>` translates into format-2 props\n169\t  (primitives, plus one read-only vertex-coloured mesh part for what they cannot fit; `fit=prims|mesh`)\n170\t  with the catalog's category and tags, routed by the licence gate: CC0/CC-BY packs to\n171\t  `assets/props/lib/<pack>/` (committed, with `PACK.json`; then the index and `CREDITS.md`),\n172\t  other licences, packs whose catalog says `\"local\"` and loose files to the git-ignored\n173\t  `assets/props/local/<pack>/` (or `out=`), with their fidelity (six-view IoU, ΔE, Chamfer mm,\n174\t  tier; `docs/ASSET_IMPORT.md`); `survey=<lib/pack|folder>` fits without writing\n175\t  and fills the ledger (`assets/catalogs/<lib>/<pack>.tsv`, keeping each row's `note`; `fit=false`:\n176\t  census only); `check=<asset.json> compare=true` measures a translation again and returns\n177\t  source-over-translation views. `up= yaw= scale= place=` bring a model into the engine's frame\n178\t  (a translation records them in `source.norm` for `check`).\n179\t- Games on the hook (`pav_core::games`, `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`; scene `starter`): `play` gives\n180\t  the running game's HUD lines, outcome and `status`; `play bot=true` lets its bot play on\n181\t  (`ticks=`, default 60 s); `play bot=true seeds=10` plays fresh runs on seeds 1..10 and leaves\n182\t  the session alone: `wins`, one `[seed, outcome, seconds]` row each, and the state of every\n183\t  run not won.\n184\t- Shardfall (scenes `town`, `arena`): `game` is the status, `autoplay seconds=30` lets a bot\n185\t  fight, `loot_roll level=40 count=5000` summarises loot tables without playing, `give\n186\t  unique=skyfall equip=true` hands over gear, `inventory` describes everything worn/carried,\n187\t  `game_cmd do=sell id=12` does any menu action exactly as the player would (it rides in the\n188\t  input frame, so replays include it). The game UI: `ui` reads what the player sees as text\n189\t  (the HUD, each open window by title, touch controls with their rects); `ui open=inventory,tree`\n190\t  / `close=all`, `device=touch width=844 height=390` (a phone), `hover=[x,y]` (a tooltip),\n191\t  `click=[x,y]` (presses what is there; its commands run) and `image=true` (the game with the UI\n192\t  over it). `capture` draws the UI too when a game runs (`ui=false` leaves it out); the windows\n193\t  `ui` opened stay open for later captures. `tree find=fire`, `tree take=Unbowed` (allocates the\n194\t  path), `tree_map` (PNG of the generated passive tree), `genome seed=7 parts=wings,horns`\n195\t  (grow a creature), `turntable seed=7` / `family=` / `boss=` (render it from all sides),\n196\t  `animsheet` (its attack frame by frame), `bestiary count=500` (generator variety), `boss`\n197\t  (spawn a designed or generated boss). Playable characters (`game/heroes.toml`: the Wanderer,\n198\t  Kestrel, Ysolde; the Hall of Heroes in town): `hero character=kestrel` plays as one (each keeps\n199\t  their own hero; the stash is shared), `game_cmd do=character name=ysolde` as the player would\n200\t  (in town), `turntable character=` / `animsheet character= skill=slash swing=3` show one. Levels: `level depth=17` (what a depth is: name,\n201\t  palette, mechanics, boss; `to=` for a range), `level` (the live one: every feature with its\n202\t  position and state), `levelmap depth=7 seed=3` (top-down PNG of a freshly built level, no\n203\t  GPU), `go place=level depth=9` (travel there, waypoint unlocked), `goto_feature kind=keg`\n204\t  (stand next to one), `game_cmd do=use spot=N` (the way down, a cursed chest);\n205\t  `autoplay` walks levels to their exits by itself.\n206\t- Building and judging content: `see` is a screenshot with numbered marks on monsters, the\n207\t  hero, townsfolk, loot, spots and level pieces plus a legend (talk about \"mark 7\" and know\n208\t  it's a rare Frost Ghoul at 40%); `campaign from=1 to=12 wall=300` lets the bot play down\n209\t  through the levels and reports each (time, deaths, kills, levels gained): run it after data\n210\t  changes; `turntable family=ghoul def={\"parts\":[{\"kind\":\"wings\"}],\"shirt\":\"#3050a0\"}` authors\n211\t  a creature as JSON over any family/boss/genome (or `{\"body\":\"quadruped\",...}` for a new one)\n212\t  and reports its anatomy; `theme_swatch depths=13-20` shows palettes; `levelmap nav=true`\n213\t  shows what the AI thinks is walkable and its planned way out. Game data lives in `game/*.toml`\n214\t  (`game_reload` re-reads it live; the tree is generated from `game/tree.toml`; levels from\n215\t  `themes.toml` and `levels.toml`, dressed with LIB/core props from the sets in `props.toml`).\n216\t- Looks: `look` lists the Look & Filters sections, presets and whole looks; `look name=HD-2D`,\n217\t  `look section=pixel preset=Chunky on=objects` set the session's look layer (applied in every\n218\t  capture); `look bench=all` renders this moment under every look in one numbered PNG. Filters\n219\t  aim at `all`, `objects` (characters & objects) or `environment` through `view.*_on` /\n220\t  `view.filter.*_on` / `pixel_target`, and `view.style_objects` / `view.style_environment`.\n221\t- Teaching: `room key=bloom` includes the room's station guide (`learn`) and its pads' notes;\n222\t  `guide term=kuwahara` / `guide search=shadow` read the field guide\n223\t  (crates/pav_core/src/field_guide.toml), `guide asks=true` lists every room's \"ask for it\"\n224\t  phrases.\n225\t- `course` shows the running course timer, gates, hits, falls, last result and best times;\n226\t  `feel` shows feel metrics (response ticks, time to top speed, stopping, turnaround, jump).\n227\t- Animation: `animsheet move=roundhouse` draws any move frame by frame (`hit=`, `side=-1` for the\n228\t  alternate swing), `animsheet clip=CMU/Cartwheel` any motion clip (`mirror=`, `upper=`,\n229\t  `travel=`; on the hero by default, `def=` for another look). `clips` lists the sets, `clips\n230\t  find=dance` searches names, tags and descriptions, `clips name=SET/Clip` shows one as readable\n231\t  key poses with its source and licence, `clips load=cmu` adds the on-disk CMU library.\n232\t  `clip_import from=<set.js|set.json|folder>` translates a set, `from=a.glb,b.glb sources=A,B\n233\t  catalog=anim/catalogs/mesh2motion.json set=MESH2MOTION` a glTF library (Rigify, Unreal-style or\n234\t  Mixamo rigs; `rm=` each file's root-motion twin, as Quaternius ships them, gives loops their\n235\t  speed), `from=lib.fbx` an FBX file (every animation stack a clip), `from=take.bvh at=2-9\n236\t  loop=true set=X` a BVH take (`way=back|left|right` for a loop that goes that way), and\n237\t  `catalog=anim/catalogs/cmu.json set=CMU` (a catalog with \"$pick\": cmu, 100style, bandai or\n238\t  lafan1) cuts its moments out of a capture database, downloading the takes; it checks the result\n239\t  reads back and reports the fit (`log=N` notes). `mocap` describes the open databases (CMU,\n240\t  100STYLE, Bandai Namco, LaFAN1, Mesh2Motion, Quaternius, Mixamo), `mocap find=limp` searches\n241\t  their takes, `mocap get=02_01,Zombie_FW` downloads (a 100STYLE or LaFAN1 take alone out of its\n242\t  archive; `get=cmu`: all 2,548 CMU takes), `mocap survey=ledger` / `survey=library` measures\n243\t  every CMU take into anim/cmu/takes.tsv / translates them into anim/cmu, and `mocap cut=13_17\n244\t  at=1.5-2.6 name=Jab set=MINE` (or `loop=true`: the best cycle; `way=`) fits one moment into\n245\t  anim/<set>.json. Room NPCs perform with `clips = [...]` / `moves = [...]`; any\n246\t  look's `idle_clip` plays while standing still and `walk_clip` (a walk loop that records its\n247\t  `speed`, e.g. STYLE100/Old_Walk) while walking, at the rate its stride matches the ground,\n248\t  `run_clip` above the pace between the two; `death_clip` is a captured fall and `attack_clips\n249\t  = { claw = \"QUATERNIUS/Zombie_Scratch\" }` captured attacks by skill, timed so the clip's\n250\t  strike (shown by `clips name=`; `@seconds` after the name overrides it) lands on the hit, or\n251\t  a list of them, one per swing of the skill's combo; `attack_moves = { cleave = \"sweep\" }`\n252\t  swings a move of anim/moves.toml in place of the skill's own (a list per swing too);\n253\t  `dodge_clip` is a captured dodge in place of the tumble (bipeds: game/monsters.toml,\n254\t  bosses.toml and heroes.toml looks, and the genome's biped `gaits`, `idle_clips`,\n255\t  `death_clips`, `attack_clips`; `animsheet family=ghoul skill=claw` shows one);\n256\t  `anim.tempo` / `anim.mirror` steer performers.\n257\t- `camera_bench` renders the current moment from several camera presets/tilts in one PNG.\n258\t- `signal name=drop` fires spawners listening for a pad signal (no need to walk onto the pad).\n259\t- `filmstrip` tiles N frames (optionally while driving the player) into one PNG: the cheapest\n260\t  way to check motion and animation. `audio_capture` renders a session's sounds to .wav.\n261\t- `stream point=[x,y,z]` adds a streaming interest point (agents exploring the world).\n262\t- `input` drives the player: `move=[x,z]` world direction (x = east, z = south), `hold=`/`press=`\n263\t  buttons (jump, crouch, crawl, use, focus, interact, primary), `aim=[x,y,z]`, `ticks=N`.\n264\t- `record_save` + `replay` reproduce a session exactly (same machine/build) and check the hash:\n265\t  use them for bug reports. The game saves `replays/last.replay.json` from the tuning panel.\n266\t- One-shot calls accept `scene=`, `seed=` and `ticks=` to set up the session first.\n267\t- `pav repl` keeps one session across lines (lines are `tool key=value ...`; `#` = comment).\n268\t- Look at captures with your image-reading tool; prefer small sizes (e.g. `width=640 height=360`).\n269\t\n270\t## Browser and phones\n271\tHeadless Chromium can run the browser build (WebGPU on SwiftShader): launch with\n272\t`--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`\n273\t(other flag sets show a blank canvas). `?room=NAME&seed=N` picks the start room.\n274\tPhones: emulate one in headless Chromium with Playwright (`viewport 844x390, isMobile, hasTouch,\n275\tdeviceScaleFactor: 1`; Chrome's DPR emulation leaves the canvas at CSS size) and drive it with\n276\tCDP `Input.dispatchTouchEvent`; SwiftShader frames take about a second, so send a tap's start and\n277\tend back to back.\n278\tFrom WSL2 you can launch the Windows build directly: `./target/x86_64-pc-windows-gnu/dist/shardfall.exe`.\n279\t\n280\t## Controls\n281\tKeyboard+mouse: WASD move, Space jump (hold = higher), C/Ctrl crouch, Z crawl toggle, F or left\n282\tclick throw bomb at the cursor, Shift walk slowly, walk into ladders to climb, jump at a ledge\n283\tand keep pushing toward it to grab it (push again/Space = pull up, C = drop), Space/C swim up/down,\n284\tcommitted model: C while running = dodge roll; right-drag rotate camera, wheel zoom, 1–8 camera\n285\tpresets. Gamepad: left stick move, right stick aim, A jump,\n286\tB crouch, Y crawl, X/RT bomb, LT slow, LB/RB rotate camera, D-pad zoom, Start menu, Back rewind.\n287\t\n288\tShardfall: WASD move, LMB/RMB/Q/E/R/F skills, Space dodge, 1 potion, Shift attack in place,\n289\tG use (vendor, stash, portal, gambler, alchemist, the way down, cursed chests), I/Tab\n290\tinventory, P passive tree, C character, K skills, M map, T town portal; gamepad\n291\tX/Y/B/RB/LB/RT skills, A dodge, D-pad up potion, D-pad right use, D-pad left map, D-pad down\n292\tpanels (LB/RB switch); in any window or the menu the stick drives a cursor (A click, X\n293\tright-click, hold Y shift, right stick scroll, B close). Controller flows can be scripted and\n294\tscreenshotted: `shardfall --pad-script FILE` (lines `<frames> [left=x,y] [right=x,y]\n295\t[hold=A,Y] [tap=DPadDown]`, see `PadScript` in input.rs).\n296\t\n297\tTouch (phones, tablets; the browser build): put a thumb down anywhere and drag to move (an\n298\tinvisible stick); attacks are automatic (the nearest foe in reach; a menu setting); the fan in\n299\tthe bottom right: dodge (big button), skills (tap: at the nearest foe · drag: aim, let go to\n300\tcast, back onto the button to cancel · hold: keep casting), potion, and a \"use\" button that\n301\tappears when something is in reach; top right: menu and bag. Swipe messages, banners and the\n302\tminimap away; tap the minimap for the big map; tap the game outside a window to close it.\n303\tGraphics quality (Auto/Low/Medium/High) and auto-attack are in the touch pause menu.\n304\t\n305\tH (world demo): how the room you are in works (the station guide: steps, pads, live settings,\n306\tuses, ask-for-it phrases, cost, the engine's code, words); outside rooms the field guide\n307\t(guide_ui.rs). Stepping on a pad with a note shows the note at the bottom for a while.\n308\tFixed system layer (never rebinds): Esc pause menu · F1 tuning panel · F2 rooms (teleport) ·\n309\tF3 boot diagnostics · F4 leave room · F5 reset room · F6 pause · F7 step · F8/F9 slower/faster ·\n310\tF10 edit mode (sandbox) · hold Backspace rewind · F11 fullscreen · F12 screenshot.\n311\tThe pause menu's *Look & filters* window (also atop the F1 panel) puts every filter on the whole\n312\tscene, the characters & objects or the environment, with sliders, presets and whole looks; it\n313\tis a layer over the scene's own settings, saved to `shardfall_looks.json` (look_ui.rs).\n314\tRooms may remap game keys while you are inside (`[keys]` in the room file); system keys never change.\n315\t\n316\t## Tests\n317\t`cargo test` includes `crates/pav_core/tests/gameplay.rs` (walk, jump onto a crate, climb the\n318\tladder, bomb the floor and drop through, crawl the tunnel, rewind repeatability, snapshot files),\n319\t`tests/world.rs` (pavilion layout, streaming, props persisting while dormant, room\n320\tenter/exit/reset, overrides, saved-object round trip), `tests/movement.rs` (courses, pads,\n321\tledge grab, swimming, pits, grid/committed models, projectiles, moving platforms, hazards),\n322\t`tests/physics.rs` (rope bridge, chain, soft bodies, conveyor, bounce, crumble/regrow, glass,\n323\tspawner pads, rewind with soft bodies; its room is `tests/physics_room.toml`, a compact example\n324\tof every physics feature) and `tests/rooms.rs` (every room file builds; courses complete).\n325\t`tests/motion.rs` (clips decode like the format's reference player, every embedded clip plays\n326\tcleanly, a set file round-trips, fades, chained moves, kicks, performers, idle clips, the hero's\n327\tcaptured death, walkers and villagers keeping their styles' pace); `tests/monsters.rs` also has\n328\ta ghoul shambling, running, clawing and falling in captured motion and generated bipeds drawing\n329\ttheirs from the genome; `crates/pav_tools/tests/mocap.rs`\n330\t(a glTF rig and a CMU take translate number for number like my-3D2dge's importer, whose output is\n331\tin tests/fixtures/mocap; the CMU survey measures a take as the committed ledger says; a BVH walk\n332\tcuts into a loop that closes; a sidestep take gives a loop each way, facing forward; an FBX file\n333\twritten by the test reads as a take); `tests/import.rs` (glTF and OBJ models built in code read\n334\tback exactly, mirrored nodes stay outward, texels give exact face colours, malformed files are\n335\terrors, normalisation, the `asset_import` tool), `tests/import_fit.rs` (a table folds into a top\n336\tand mirrored legs, a vase is a lathe, a blade an extrusion, a noisy blob a mesh part within budget;\n337\tthe engine reads every translation back; byte-identical twice; translate, check, compare and\n338\tsurvey through the tool), `tests/import_library.rs` (packs go to lib/ or local/ by licence\n339\twith their `PACK.json`, credits, index and catalog tags; unknown licences refused),\n340\t`tests/planted_defects.rs` (the store's five planted defects, each caught: size_band,\n341\tbelow_floor, socket_missing, a missing texture failing the import), `tests/store_rooms.rs` (the\n342\tLibrary Hall is current, the store rooms pass room_check, every id a sign names resolves, each\n343\tLimits sign is true, the Catalogue pages through every row with rewind and snapshots, the Store\n344\tShowcase shows every core material, the Clip Browser moves its whole cast together, Kenney Mini\n345\tDungeon through every step), `tests/figures.rs` (S3b: the KayKit adventurers check clean\n346\tas props and figures, every embedded clip poses three of them whole, finite and on the floor,\n347\tlimbs follow the puppet's side, a planted defect is caught, `assets find=knight`) and\n348\t`tests/import_khronos.rs` (opt-in,\n349\t`-- --ignored`: 12 Khronos sample models at a pinned commit). `pav_core/tests/prop_materials.rs`\n350\t(props wear library and inline materials, format 1's colours and revisions hold, unknown names,\n351\tone edit that adds and uses a material, snapshots) and `pav_tools/tests/material_preview.rs`\n352\t(a swatch renders headless with its layer lines).\n353\tThe game UI: `pav_tools/tests/game_ui.rs` (`ui` reads the HUD, windows and touch controls and\n354\tclicks a button; captures draw the UI).\n355\tGames on the hook: `pav_core/tests/games.rs` (the starter's bot wins on ten seeds, standing\n356\tstill loses, snapshot files, rewind and replays are exact, every game key names one scene)\n357\tand `pav_tools/tests/play.rs` (the `play` tool reports, drives and sweeps seeds).\n358\tShardfall: `tests/arpg.rs` (combat) and `tests/loot.rs` (drops, equipping, town trade, travel,\n359\trewind across travel, unique powers), `tests/skills.rs` (all 16 skills, tweaks, channels, the\n360\tpassive tree through commands, keystones, the bot spending points), `tests/monsters.rs`\n361\t(archetypes, broods, bombers, affixes, bosses through their phases, the Menagerie),\n362\t`tests/levels.rs` (every designed level has its mechanics, each mechanic works, endless\n363\tdepths, the sealed exit, waypoints, rewind in a level; dressing never moves a mechanic or a\n364\tmonster, is reproducible, keeps its snapshot budget and the paths; baked props collide, sleep\n365\tand restore), `tests/town.rs` (townsfolk, gambling,\n366\tbrewing, saves), `tests/feel.rs` (gibs, kill streaks, boss entrances), `tests/heroes.rs`\n367\t(playable characters: the hall, switching with a shared stash, strikes per combo swing,\n368\tcaptured dodges).\n369\tView: `pav_view/tests/pixel_art.rs`, `parts.rs` (characters & objects vs environment, filters per\n370\tpart, styles per part, styles/haze reach the renderer, transition loop), `look.rs` (look\n371\tsections, presets, whole looks), `water.rs` (ripples spread and fade, rain, pool strips merge)\n372\tand `learn.rs` (station guides and the field guide stay consistent with the engine);\n373\t`material_parity.rs` renders every material kernel on the GPU (lavapipe will do) and compares\n374\tit with the CPU reference.\n375\tExtend it when you add movement features; it is the cheapest way to catch feel regressions.\n376\tAlso, named for what they cover: `pav_core/tests/` `animation.rs`, `animation_preview.rs`,\n377\t`asset_library.rs`, `bench_holdout.rs`, `fx.rs`, `genre.rs`, `prop_families.rs`,\n378\t`prop_format2.rs`, `prop_instances.rs`, `prop_patch.rs`, `shipped_pack.rs` (the KayKit figures\n379\tload with no asset files), `vehicles.rs`; `pav_tools/tests/`\n380\t`animation_preview.rs`, `asset_errors.rs`, `asset_format2.rs`, `asset_library.rs`,\n381\t`asset_preview.rs`, `foundations.rs`, `reply_budget.rs`, `store.rs`.\n382\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"f051fc2d-3942-470b-8e69-233e8d4fb6bd","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:26.452Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/docs/REFERENCE.md","content":"# Reference\n\nThe detail behind `AGENTS.md`, read on demand: open the section you need, not the whole page.\n`pav help` lists every tool with its arguments.\n- [Code map](#code-map): what each crate and data folder holds.\n- [Studios](#studios): animation, objects and creatures, edited live.\n- [Tools](#tools): each tool family with examples.\n- [Browser and phones](#browser-and-phones): running the web build headless, emulating a phone.\n- [Controls](#controls): keyboard, gamepad, touch and the fixed system keys.\n- [Tests](#tests): what each suite covers.\n\n## Code map\n```\ncrates/\n  pav_core    simulation, no window/GPU/audio deps: Sim, entities, physics (rapier3d),\n              static regions (statics.rs: terrain chunks, rooms, hub; active or dormant;\n              blocks, decor and baked library props: drawn by the view's region cache, one\n              collider box each, no entity),\n              world.rs (pavilion layout, streaming, room tracking), room.rs (room files),\n              terrain.rs (procedural wilderness), tile levels (level.rs), characters\n              (character.rs: movement models instant/momentum/grid/committed, jump,\n              crouch/crawl, ladders, ledge grab/mantle, swimming, axis lock, moving platforms,\n              knockback, pushing shares momentum, bombs, ice slide), puppet (puppet.rs: body\n              plans biped/spider/lizard/beetle/blob/quadruped, procedural animation, hit recoil,\n              creature lunges, foot IK, legs that stride the way the body travels, idle breathing\n              and blinks, cutout look and camera cheats; the biped is a `Skel` of joints that the\n              procedural animation, moves and clips each produce and `dress` turns into parts),\n              moves.rs (attacks and gestures as data, anim/moves.toml: arcs around the shoulders,\n              wind-up from wherever the hands were, strikes with lunge/lean/twist/hop/spin,\n              follow-through; skills name a move, a look may name its own per skill and combo\n              swing: `attack_moves`), clips.rs (motion clips: readable key poses\n              translated from open animation libraries, retargeted onto the biped with IK,\n              crossfaded over the procedural animation, upper body only or mirrored; performers,\n              idle clips, walk clips played as fast as the character moves), anim.rs (the /anim files: embedded, live reload), rig.rs (creature feet that plant and step,\n              follow-the-leader spines, verlet tails/antennae), ai.rs (NPC brains: idle,\n              wander, patrol, circle, follow; they drive characters through InputFrames),\n              nav.rs (walkable grid from blocks and props, A* paths, flow fields),\n              games/ (the hook a new game implements, `Game`, and `GAMES`, the list that\n              makes each one a scene; starter.rs + starter.toml, the template:\n              docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md),\n              fxdef.rs (object lights / particle emitters / distortion, view-only),\n              vehicle.rs (drift car on rapier's raycast vehicle, arcade helicopter: flight only,\n              no weapons, damage or fuel), health.rs (shootable rapier objects: hp, score, boss\n              phases/bar, finish),\n              stealth.rs (guard sight cones with line of sight and an alert meter),\n              zones.rs (trigger zones + labels), course.rs (timers, gates, checkpoints, pits,\n              pads, camera cues, hits/respawn), behaviors.rs (movers, rotators, emitters,\n              spawners), projectile.rs (lightweight bullets), feel.rs (feel metrics),\n              softbody.rs (rapier soft bodies: jelly, balls, cloth, ropes), joints.rs (joints\n              owned by entities, recreated on wake), destruct.rs (crumbling/breakable tiles,\n              conveyors and bounce pads for props), history, params\n  pav_render  wgpu renderer (Vulkan/DX12): Scene description -> particles (compute) -> sun +\n              point-light shadow passes (shadows.rs) -> MSAA scene pass (+ GPU particles) ->\n              bloom chain and distortion (fx.rs) -> composite (outlines, screen-space GI,\n              tonemap, filter stack: pixelate/CRT/scanlines/dither/palettes/grading, split;\n              pixel art on part of the scene: objects flagged `flags::PIXEL`, chosen in the view\n              by `view.filter.pixel_target` = all/characters/hero/others/world/entity/objects/\n              environment; every filter can aim at a part: `flags::OBJECT` marks characters &\n              objects, the normal buffer carries it, `*_on` = all/objects/environment; painterly and\n              print styles: Kuwahara oil paint, CMYK halftone, ASCII, pencil sketch (`filter.stylize`);\n              hazy air: volumetric sun shafts ray-marched through the sun's shadow map and lamp\n              halos in closed form (`view.haze`, `shafts`, `halos`); screen transitions (iris,\n              diamonds, dissolve, mosaic, blinds, fade); wind in the vertex shader for instances\n              flagged `flags::SWAY` (leaves) / `flags::GRASS` (tufts that part around the player)).\n              Procedural meshes + analytic SDF spheres/capsules/rounded cones, SDF-font text in\n              the world (text.rs). Procedural materials: rows of a storage buffer read by one\n              kernel library (shaders/materials.wgsl, matched pixel for pixel by\n              `pav_core::material::eval`); `pav_view::materials` interns them into slots for\n              prop parts, static blocks (world-mapped) and decor. `render_scale` draws below the target's resolution and\n              upscale.rs stretches it over (phones). Offscreen capture -> PNG.\n  pav_view    sim frame -> render Scene: camera rig (tilt/yaw/distance/fov/ortho, all live),\n              look.rs + looks.toml (the Look & Filters layer: filter sections, presets, whole looks),\n              water.rs (water zones ripple: a CPU wave equation on a height grid, stirred by\n              whatever crosses the waterline, splashes, blasts and rain; drawn as a dynamic mesh),\n              assets.rs (`emit_asset`: a library asset drawn from game state each frame),\n              interpolation between ticks, visual settings (ViewSettings)\n  pav_audio   synthesized sound: oscillators/noise/envelopes/filters, event -> sound bank,\n              cpal output (optional), offline .wav rendering\n  pav_tools   agent layer: tool registry + `pav` CLI (one-shot, REPL) + MCP stdio server +\n              live bridge client (bridge.rs), and the motion importer (mocap/: glTF and FBX\n              libraries, CMU's ASF/AMC, BVH; Rigify, Unreal, Mixamo/MotionBuilder, 100STYLE and\n              Bandai Namco skeletons; loops cut at their best cycle; fitted into readable key\n              poses, number for number what my-3D2dge's own importer writes; the whole CMU\n              database surveyed and translated), and the asset importer (import/: what both\n              importers share, one `Scene` read from glTF/OBJ, normalisation, translation into\n              props with fidelity metrics, the licence gate that routes packs to lib/ or\n              local/; `asset_import`, `docs/ASSET_IMPORT.md`)\n  pav_app     the game (`shardfall` binary): window, boot diagnostics, input (keyboard/mouse,\n              gamepad via gilrs), system keys, tuning panel, pause menu, sim thread\n              (simhost.rs; stepped from the frame loop in the browser), live bridge server\n              (bridge.rs), egui input (uiinput.rs), graphics quality (quality.rs: render\n              scale, dynamic resolution). Builds natively and for wasm32 (WebGPU).\n  pav_ui      Shardfall's game UI in egui, drawn by the window and by the tools: the HUD\n              (arpg_ui.rs: orbs, skill bar, banners, minimap), the windows (arpg_items.rs:\n              inventory, shop, stash, character, skills; arpg_tree.rs: the passive tree), touch\n              screens (touch.rs: finger routing, invisible stick, button fan, auto-attack,\n              swipe-to-dismiss), and headless.rs (the same UI on a windowless context: a text\n              reading by window, clicks, and an egui-wgpu overlay painted over captures).\nanim/         animation data, embedded and hot-reloadable (`anim_reload`): moves.toml (the moves table)\n              and clip sets (*.json: QUATERNIUS, MESH2MOTION, CMU, STYLE100: 437 clips with credits).\n              anim/cmu/ holds every take of the CMU database (4,770 clips) and anim/100style/ the\n              100STYLE runs, backward walks and runs, sidesteps and idles (780 loops), on disk\n              only: `clips load=cmu`, `clips load=100style`. anim/local/ (git-ignored) holds what\n              is translated from libraries whose licences forbid sharing it (Bandai Namco, LaFAN1,\n              Mixamo): `clips load=local`. anim/catalogs/ says how each set is made (tags,\n              descriptions, sources, picks): `clip_import catalog=...` rebuilds it from the\n              libraries.\nrooms/        room data files (TOML: info card, station guide `[learn]`, wing, primary device, movement model, camera,\n              params, keys, entrance, ASCII tile layers + legend, [[object]]s). Every file\n              here is embedded in the exe at build time AND hot-reloaded at runtime.\n              `_template.toml` documents every field.\n```\n\n## Studios\nAnimation Studio: `scripts/animation-studio.sh` or `shardfall --animation-studio` opens an\nisolated preview and the live bridge. Use `.mcp.animation-studio.json` for visible MCP edits.\n`anim_edit` creates/copies/inspects/edits clips, with revisions and 32-step undo/redo;\n`anim_preview` plays, pauses, scrubs, and inspects their poses. Every accepted edit saves and\nupdates the live library. The preview preserves the game state. Native animation files also\nreload automatically. See `docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`. REPLs accept structured JSON lines:\n`{\"tool\":\"anim_edit\",\"args\":{\"action\":\"key\",\"name\":\"WORKSHOP/Wave\",\"time\":0.5,\"pose\":{\"armR\":[80,20,50,25,0]}}}`.\nUse the returned `revision` as `if_revision` on later edits. Copy source clips before editing;\nkeep local source copies and their history in `anim/local`.\n\nObject Studio: `scripts/asset-studio.sh` or `shardfall --asset-studio` opens reusable prop\nauthoring on the same live bridge (`.mcp.studio.json`). `assets find=words` searches templates\n(`BUILTIN/`), library packs (`LIB/pack/name`; `LIB/core` is embedded in every build, other packs\nload from disk natively, `assets/props/local/` is git-ignored) and saved definitions, ranked,\nwith `category= tags= tier= similar=` filters. `asset_edit` creates/copies/inspects/patches\nreadable JSON with stable part names, atomic batches, revisions, and 32-step history.\n`asset_preview` opens the isolated stage; `views=true` returns a framed 4-view contact sheet\n(`figure=true` adds a 1.8 m figure) and leaves the stage and camera as they were. `capture stats=only` returns render cost without\nan image. `asset_spawn` places one-root instances in the preserved scene. Edits refresh visuals\nand compound collision together. Native file changes reload after 250 ms of quiet. Saved\ndefinitions are `assets/props/workshop/*.json` (`PAV_ASSETS` overrides the props root).\n`studio_status ticket=N` confirms frame submission for live edits without a capture. The\nAnimations / Objects / Game tabs retain each workspace's controls and camera. See\n`docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md` (tools and format) and `docs/ASSET_STUDIO_LIVE.md` (the live studio).\nKeep procedural parts as text; use `if_revision` and batch related part changes in one `ops`\narray. Materials (planks, brick, cobbles … as per-pixel recipes, `docs/MATERIALS.md`): parts say\n`\"mat\":\"MAT/oak_planks\"` or a key in the asset's `materials`; `kind=material` on `assets`,\n`asset_edit` (workshop materials `MAT/workshop/x` in `assets/materials/workshop/`, `PAV_MATERIALS`\noverrides the root; revisions `mat1-`) and `asset_preview` (a swatch with each layer's legibility\nat the game camera). Game code draws units and scenery from its own state with library assets,\none call per thing per frame: `pav_view::assets::emit_asset(scene, \"BUILTIN/crate\",\nPlacement::at(pos).yaw(deg).id(id), seed, &palette)` (no physics needed; `docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`,\n\"Library assets in game code\"). Game data and rooms may name only assets every build carries\n(embedded, or a shipped pack's: `props::store::SHIPPED`).\n\nCreature Studio: `scripts/creature-studio.sh` or `shardfall --creature-studio` opens the\nprocedural creature stage. `tools/creature-compiler` vendors the exact SpawnForge core/modules\npin and bundles it with `npm ci --ignore-scripts && npm run build` (run in that directory).\nKeep the pin and source hashes intact; do not resolve against a moving upstream checkout.\n`creature_catalog`, `creature_edit`, `creature_preview`, and `creature_status` expose theme,\ntemplate, blueprint, named-part and surface authoring through the same MCP bridge. Edits\nqueue asynchronous builds. Wait for `published`, `failed`, or `superseded`; only publication\ngets a frame ticket. Use the new source `revision` for the next edit, and `studio_status`\nfor actual frame submission. Failed/obsolete builds keep the last valid native mesh.\nSources, history and disposable caches are under `assets/creatures` (`PAV_CREATURES`\noverrides it). Node runs outside the simulation. The Windows package includes the bundle\nand portable Node. See `docs/CREATURE_STUDIO.md` for native material limits and the tool\ncontract. This is an authoring/preview stage; gameplay spawning of these rigs is separate.\n\n## Tools\n- Import: `asset_import` alone lists the open model libraries; `get=kenney/mini_dungeon` downloads\n  a pack its catalog pins (`assets/catalogs/<lib>.json`) into .cache/assets, checked by SHA-256;\n  `from=<model> list=true` summarises a .glb/.gltf/.obj (parts, triangles, size in cm, materials,\n  main colours, warnings); `from=<model|folder|lib/pack>` translates into format-2 props\n  (primitives, plus one read-only vertex-coloured mesh part for what they cannot fit; `fit=prims|mesh`)\n  with the catalog's category and tags, routed by the licence gate: CC0/CC-BY packs to\n  `assets/props/lib/<pack>/` (committed, with `PACK.json`; then the index and `CREDITS.md`),\n  other licences, packs whose catalog says `\"local\"` and loose files to the git-ignored\n  `assets/props/local/<pack>/` (or `out=`), with their fidelity (six-view IoU, ΔE, Chamfer mm,\n  tier; `docs/ASSET_IMPORT.md`); `survey=<lib/pack|folder>` fits without writing\n  and fills the ledger (`assets/catalogs/<lib>/<pack>.tsv`, keeping each row's `note`; `fit=false`:\n  census only); `check=<asset.json> compare=true` measures a translation again and returns\n  source-over-translation views. `up= yaw= scale= place=` bring a model into the engine's frame\n  (a translation records them in `source.norm` for `check`).\n- Games on the hook (`pav_core::games`, `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`; scene `starter`): `play` gives\n  the running game's HUD lines, outcome and `status`; `play bot=true` lets its bot play on\n  (`ticks=`, default 60 s); `play bot=true seeds=10` plays fresh runs on seeds 1..10 and leaves\n  the session alone: `wins`, one `[seed, outcome, seconds]` row each, and the state of every\n  run not won.\n- Shardfall (scenes `town`, `arena`): `game` is the status, `autoplay seconds=30` lets a bot\n  fight, `loot_roll level=40 count=5000` summarises loot tables without playing, `give\n  unique=skyfall equip=true` hands over gear, `inventory` describes everything worn/carried,\n  `game_cmd do=sell id=12` does any menu action exactly as the player would (it rides in the\n  input frame, so replays include it). The game UI: `ui` reads what the player sees as text\n  (the HUD, each open window by title, touch controls with their rects); `ui open=inventory,tree`\n  / `close=all`, `device=touch width=844 height=390` (a phone), `hover=[x,y]` (a tooltip),\n  `click=[x,y]` (presses what is there; its commands run) and `image=true` (the game with the UI\n  over it). `capture` draws the UI too when a game runs (`ui=false` leaves it out); the windows\n  `ui` opened stay open for later captures. `tree find=fire`, `tree take=Unbowed` (allocates the\n  path), `tree_map` (PNG of the generated passive tree), `genome seed=7 parts=wings,horns`\n  (grow a creature), `turntable seed=7` / `family=` / `boss=` (render it from all sides),\n  `animsheet` (its attack frame by frame), `bestiary count=500` (generator variety), `boss`\n  (spawn a designed or generated boss). Playable characters (`game/heroes.toml`: the Wanderer,\n  Kestrel, Ysolde; the Hall of Heroes in town): `hero character=kestrel` plays as one (each keeps\n  their own hero; the stash is shared), `game_cmd do=character name=ysolde` as the player would\n  (in town), `turntable character=` / `animsheet character= skill=slash swing=3` show one. Levels: `level depth=17` (what a depth is: name,\n  palette, mechanics, boss; `to=` for a range), `level` (the live one: every feature with its\n  position and state), `levelmap depth=7 seed=3` (top-down PNG of a freshly built level, no\n  GPU), `go place=level depth=9` (travel there, waypoint unlocked), `goto_feature kind=keg`\n  (stand next to one), `game_cmd do=use spot=N` (the way down, a cursed chest);\n  `autoplay` walks levels to their exits by itself.\n- Building and judging content: `see` is a screenshot with numbered marks on monsters, the\n  hero, townsfolk, loot, spots and level pieces plus a legend (talk about \"mark 7\" and know\n  it's a rare Frost Ghoul at 40%); `campaign from=1 to=12 wall=300` lets the bot play down\n  through the levels and reports each (time, deaths, kills, levels gained): run it after data\n  changes; `turntable family=ghoul def={\"parts\":[{\"kind\":\"wings\"}],\"shirt\":\"#3050a0\"}` authors\n  a creature as JSON over any family/boss/genome (or `{\"body\":\"quadruped\",...}` for a new one)\n  and reports its anatomy; `theme_swatch depths=13-20` shows palettes; `levelmap nav=true`\n  shows what the AI thinks is walkable and its planned way out. Game data lives in `game/*.toml`\n  (`game_reload` re-reads it live; the tree is generated from `game/tree.toml`; levels from\n  `themes.toml` and `levels.toml`, dressed with LIB/core props from the sets in `props.toml`).\n- Looks: `look` lists the Look & Filters sections, presets and whole looks; `look name=HD-2D`,\n  `look section=pixel preset=Chunky on=objects` set the session's look layer (applied in every\n  capture); `look bench=all` renders this moment under every look in one numbered PNG. Filters\n  aim at `all`, `objects` (characters & objects) or `environment` through `view.*_on` /\n  `view.filter.*_on` / `pixel_target`, and `view.style_objects` / `view.style_environment`.\n- Teaching: `room key=bloom` includes the room's station guide (`learn`) and its pads' notes;\n  `guide term=kuwahara` / `guide search=shadow` read the field guide\n  (crates/pav_core/src/field_guide.toml), `guide asks=true` lists every room's \"ask for it\"\n  phrases.\n- `course` shows the running course timer, gates, hits, falls, last result and best times;\n  `feel` shows feel metrics (response ticks, time to top speed, stopping, turnaround, jump).\n- Animation: `animsheet move=roundhouse` draws any move frame by frame (`hit=`, `side=-1` for the\n  alternate swing), `animsheet clip=CMU/Cartwheel` any motion clip (`mirror=`, `upper=`,\n  `travel=`; on the hero by default, `def=` for another look). `clips` lists the sets, `clips\n  find=dance` searches names, tags and descriptions, `clips name=SET/Clip` shows one as readable\n  key poses with its source and licence, `clips load=cmu` adds the on-disk CMU library.\n  `clip_import from=<set.js|set.json|folder>` translates a set, `from=a.glb,b.glb sources=A,B\n  catalog=anim/catalogs/mesh2motion.json set=MESH2MOTION` a glTF library (Rigify, Unreal-style or\n  Mixamo rigs; `rm=` each file's root-motion twin, as Quaternius ships them, gives loops their\n  speed), `from=lib.fbx` an FBX file (every animation stack a clip), `from=take.bvh at=2-9\n  loop=true set=X` a BVH take (`way=back|left|right` for a loop that goes that way), and\n  `catalog=anim/catalogs/cmu.json set=CMU` (a catalog with \"$pick\": cmu, 100style, bandai or\n  lafan1) cuts its moments out of a capture database, downloading the takes; it checks the result\n  reads back and reports the fit (`log=N` notes). `mocap` describes the open databases (CMU,\n  100STYLE, Bandai Namco, LaFAN1, Mesh2Motion, Quaternius, Mixamo), `mocap find=limp` searches\n  their takes, `mocap get=02_01,Zombie_FW` downloads (a 100STYLE or LaFAN1 take alone out of its\n  archive; `get=cmu`: all 2,548 CMU takes), `mocap survey=ledger` / `survey=library` measures\n  every CMU take into anim/cmu/takes.tsv / translates them into anim/cmu, and `mocap cut=13_17\n  at=1.5-2.6 name=Jab set=MINE` (or `loop=true`: the best cycle; `way=`) fits one moment into\n  anim/<set>.json. Room NPCs perform with `clips = [...]` / `moves = [...]`; any\n  look's `idle_clip` plays while standing still and `walk_clip` (a walk loop that records its\n  `speed`, e.g. STYLE100/Old_Walk) while walking, at the rate its stride matches the ground,\n  `run_clip` above the pace between the two; `death_clip` is a captured fall and `attack_clips\n  = { claw = \"QUATERNIUS/Zombie_Scratch\" }` captured attacks by skill, timed so the clip's\n  strike (shown by `clips name=`; `@seconds` after the name overrides it) lands on the hit, or\n  a list of them, one per swing of the skill's combo; `attack_moves = { cleave = \"sweep\" }`\n  swings a move of anim/moves.toml in place of the skill's own (a list per swing too);\n  `dodge_clip` is a captured dodge in place of the tumble (bipeds: game/monsters.toml,\n  bosses.toml and heroes.toml looks, and the genome's biped `gaits`, `idle_clips`,\n  `death_clips`, `attack_clips`; `animsheet family=ghoul skill=claw` shows one);\n  `anim.tempo` / `anim.mirror` steer performers.\n- `camera_bench` renders the current moment from several camera presets/tilts in one PNG.\n- `signal name=drop` fires spawners listening for a pad signal (no need to walk onto the pad).\n- `filmstrip` tiles N frames (optionally while driving the player) into one PNG: the cheapest\n  way to check motion and animation. `audio_capture` renders a session's sounds to .wav.\n- `stream point=[x,y,z]` adds a streaming interest point (agents exploring the world).\n- `input` drives the player: `move=[x,z]` world direction (x = east, z = south), `hold=`/`press=`\n  buttons (jump, crouch, crawl, use, focus, interact, primary), `aim=[x,y,z]`, `ticks=N`.\n- `record_save` + `replay` reproduce a session exactly (same machine/build) and check the hash:\n  use them for bug reports. The game saves `replays/last.replay.json` from the tuning panel.\n- One-shot calls accept `scene=`, `seed=` and `ticks=` to set up the session first.\n- `pav repl` keeps one session across lines (lines are `tool key=value ...`; `#` = comment).\n- Look at captures with your image-reading tool; prefer small sizes (e.g. `width=640 height=360`).\n\n## Browser and phones\nHeadless Chromium can run the browser build (WebGPU on SwiftShader): launch with\n`--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`\n(other flag sets show a blank canvas). `?room=NAME&seed=N` picks the start room.\nPhones: emulate one in headless Chromium with Playwright (`viewport 844x390, isMobile, hasTouch,\ndeviceScaleFactor: 1`; Chrome's DPR emulation leaves the canvas at CSS size) and drive it with\nCDP `Input.dispatchTouchEvent`; SwiftShader frames take about a second, so send a tap's start and\nend back to back.\nFrom WSL2 you can launch the Windows build directly: `./target/x86_64-pc-windows-gnu/dist/shardfall.exe`.\n\n## Controls\nKeyboard+mouse: WASD move, Space jump (hold = higher), C/Ctrl crouch, Z crawl toggle, F or left\nclick throw bomb at the cursor, Shift walk slowly, walk into ladders to climb, jump at a ledge\nand keep pushing toward it to grab it (push again/Space = pull up, C = drop), Space/C swim up/down,\ncommitted model: C while running = dodge roll; right-drag rotate camera, wheel zoom, 1–8 camera\npresets. Gamepad: left stick move, right stick aim, A jump,\nB crouch, Y crawl, X/RT bomb, LT slow, LB/RB rotate camera, D-pad zoom, Start menu, Back rewind.\n\nShardfall: WASD move, LMB/RMB/Q/E/R/F skills, Space dodge, 1 potion, Shift attack in place,\nG use (vendor, stash, portal, gambler, alchemist, the way down, cursed chests), I/Tab\ninventory, P passive tree, C character, K skills, M map, T town portal; gamepad\nX/Y/B/RB/LB/RT skills, A dodge, D-pad up potion, D-pad right use, D-pad left map, D-pad down\npanels (LB/RB switch); in any window or the menu the stick drives a cursor (A click, X\nright-click, hold Y shift, right stick scroll, B close). Controller flows can be scripted and\nscreenshotted: `shardfall --pad-script FILE` (lines `<frames> [left=x,y] [right=x,y]\n[hold=A,Y] [tap=DPadDown]`, see `PadScript` in input.rs).\n\nTouch (phones, tablets; the browser build): put a thumb down anywhere and drag to move (an\ninvisible stick); attacks are automatic (the nearest foe in reach; a menu setting); the fan in\nthe bottom right: dodge (big button), skills (tap: at the nearest foe · drag: aim, let go to\ncast, back onto the button to cancel · hold: keep casting), potion, and a \"use\" button that\nappears when something is in reach; top right: menu and bag. Swipe messages, banners and the\nminimap away; tap the minimap for the big map; tap the game outside a window to close it.\nGraphics quality (Auto/Low/Medium/High) and auto-attack are in the touch pause menu.\n\nH (world demo): how the room you are in works (the station guide: steps, pads, live settings,\nuses, ask-for-it phrases, cost, the engine's code, words); outside rooms the field guide\n(guide_ui.rs). Stepping on a pad with a note shows the note at the bottom for a while.\nFixed system layer (never rebinds): Esc pause menu · F1 tuning panel · F2 rooms (teleport) ·\nF3 boot diagnostics · F4 leave room · F5 reset room · F6 pause · F7 step · F8/F9 slower/faster ·\nF10 edit mode (sandbox) · hold Backspace rewind · F11 fullscreen · F12 screenshot.\nThe pause menu's *Look & filters* window (also atop the F1 panel) puts every filter on the whole\nscene, the characters & objects or the environment, with sliders, presets and whole looks; it\nis a layer over the scene's own settings, saved to `shardfall_looks.json` (look_ui.rs).\nRooms may remap game keys while you are inside (`[keys]` in the room file); system keys never change.\n\n## Tests\n`cargo test` includes `crates/pav_core/tests/gameplay.rs` (walk, jump onto a crate, climb the\nladder, bomb the floor and drop through, crawl the tunnel, rewind repeatability, snapshot files),\n`tests/world.rs` (pavilion layout, streaming, props persisting while dormant, room\nenter/exit/reset, overrides, saved-object round trip), `tests/movement.rs` (courses, pads,\nledge grab, swimming, pits, grid/committed models, projectiles, moving platforms, hazards),\n`tests/physics.rs` (rope bridge, chain, soft bodies, conveyor, bounce, crumble/regrow, glass,\nspawner pads, rewind with soft bodies; its room is `tests/physics_room.toml`, a compact example\nof every physics feature) and `tests/rooms.rs` (every room file builds; courses complete).\n`tests/motion.rs` (clips decode like the format's reference player, every embedded clip plays\ncleanly, a set file round-trips, fades, chained moves, kicks, performers, idle clips, the hero's\ncaptured death, walkers and villagers keeping their styles' pace); `tests/monsters.rs` also has\na ghoul shambling, running, clawing and falling in captured motion and generated bipeds drawing\ntheirs from the genome; `crates/pav_tools/tests/mocap.rs`\n(a glTF rig and a CMU take translate number for number like my-3D2dge's importer, whose output is\nin tests/fixtures/mocap; the CMU survey measures a take as the committed ledger says; a BVH walk\ncuts into a loop that closes; a sidestep take gives a loop each way, facing forward; an FBX file\nwritten by the test reads as a take); `tests/import.rs` (glTF and OBJ models built in code read\nback exactly, mirrored nodes stay outward, texels give exact face colours, malformed files are\nerrors, normalisation, the `asset_import` tool), `tests/import_fit.rs` (a table folds into a top\nand mirrored legs, a vase is a lathe, a blade an extrusion, a noisy blob a mesh part within budget;\nthe engine reads every translation back; byte-identical twice; translate, check, compare and\nsurvey through the tool), `tests/import_library.rs` (packs go to lib/ or local/ by licence\nwith their `PACK.json`, credits, index and catalog tags; unknown licences refused),\n`tests/planted_defects.rs` (the store's five planted defects, each caught: size_band,\nbelow_floor, socket_missing, a missing texture failing the import), `tests/store_rooms.rs` (the\nLibrary Hall is current, the store rooms pass room_check, every id a sign names resolves, each\nLimits sign is true, the Catalogue pages through every row with rewind and snapshots, the Store\nShowcase shows every core material, the Clip Browser moves its whole cast together, Kenney Mini\nDungeon through every step), `tests/figures.rs` (S3b: the KayKit adventurers check clean\nas props and figures, every embedded clip poses three of them whole, finite and on the floor,\nlimbs follow the puppet's side, a planted defect is caught, `assets find=knight`) and\n`tests/import_khronos.rs` (opt-in,\n`-- --ignored`: 12 Khronos sample models at a pinned commit). `pav_core/tests/prop_materials.rs`\n(props wear library and inline materials, format 1's colours and revisions hold, unknown names,\none edit that adds and uses a material, snapshots) and `pav_tools/tests/material_preview.rs`\n(a swatch renders headless with its layer lines).\nThe game UI: `pav_tools/tests/game_ui.rs` (`ui` reads the HUD, windows and touch controls and\nclicks a button; captures draw the UI).\nGames on the hook: `pav_core/tests/games.rs` (the starter's bot wins on ten seeds, standing\nstill loses, snapshot files, rewind and replays are exact, every game key names one scene)\nand `pav_tools/tests/play.rs` (the `play` tool reports, drives and sweeps seeds).\nShardfall: `tests/arpg.rs` (combat) and `tests/loot.rs` (drops, equipping, town trade, travel,\nrewind across travel, unique powers), `tests/skills.rs` (all 16 skills, tweaks, channels, the\npassive tree through commands, keystones, the bot spending points), `tests/monsters.rs`\n(archetypes, broods, bombers, affixes, bosses through their phases, the Menagerie),\n`tests/levels.rs` (every designed level has its mechanics, each mechanic works, endless\ndepths, the sealed exit, waypoints, rewind in a level; dressing never moves a mechanic or a\nmonster, is reproducible, keeps its snapshot budget and the paths; baked props collide, sleep\nand restore), `tests/town.rs` (townsfolk, gambling,\nbrewing, saves), `tests/feel.rs` (gibs, kill streaks, boss entrances), `tests/heroes.rs`\n(playable characters: the hall, switching with a shared stash, strikes per combo swing,\ncaptured dodges).\nView: `pav_view/tests/pixel_art.rs`, `parts.rs` (characters & objects vs environment, filters per\npart, styles per part, styles/haze reach the renderer, transition loop), `look.rs` (look\nsections, presets, whole looks), `water.rs` (ripples spread and fade, rain, pool strips merge)\nand `learn.rs` (station guides and the field guide stay consistent with the engine);\n`material_parity.rs` renders every material kernel on the GPU (lavapipe will do) and compares\nit with the CPU reference.\nExtend it when you add movement features; it is the cheapest way to catch feel regressions.\nAlso, named for what they cover: `pav_core/tests/` `animation.rs`, `animation_preview.rs`,\n`asset_library.rs`, `bench_holdout.rs`, `fx.rs`, `genre.rs`, `prop_families.rs`,\n`prop_format2.rs`, `prop_instances.rs`, `prop_patch.rs`, `shipped_pack.rs` (the KayKit figures\nload with no asset files), `vehicles.rs`; `pav_tools/tests/`\n`animation_preview.rs`, `asset_errors.rs`, `asset_format2.rs`, `asset_library.rs`,\n`asset_preview.rs`, `foundations.rs`, `reply_budget.rs`, `store.rs`.\n","numLines":382,"startLine":1,"totalLines":382}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01MgLEVHdeQqP28ZtLEoGLit","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9GzcpFKqm1mR28uZLr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0167JMNqoss3fSSZ7o96CxuJ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/docs/FEEDBACK.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16843,"cache_read_input_tokens":47614,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16843},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"88e5b8e4-2642-4c36-b9d2-5a800a6b52a9","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:26.966Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9GzNvfDE9m8V8KHUXs","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_0167JMNqoss3fSSZ7o96CxuJ":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/docs/FEEDBACK.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0167JMNqoss3fSSZ7o96CxuJ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Test-game feedback\n2\t\n3\tEngine feedback from test games: a fresh agent builds a vertical slice of a 1985-2015 classic on\n4\tthis engine, then evaluates the engine. Each game is stored as one self-contained HTML file in\n5\t`michaelcrosato/ai-hub` (`games/`, source zip in `sources/`). The maintainer checks each claim\n6\tagainst the code, fixes what blocks the next game, and waits for patterns before larger work:\n7\tfeatures everyone uses, features no one uses, problems that keep coming back. Newest game first\n8\tunder \"Games\"; the tally is updated with every game.\n9\t\n10\t**Prompt.** The user's template, with the game's name, year and platforms as SOURCE: build an\n11\toriginal spiritual successor as one complete, polished level that doubles as a tech demo; use the\n12\tengine, extend it as needed; deliver one self-contained HTML file; then a one-page candid\n13\tevaluation of the engine (used/not used, engine work you wrote ranked by output saved, engine\n14\tchanges, bugs and wrong docs, source you had to read, fidelity limits, testing, what helped and\n15\thurt, missing features by impact, the one change). The questions get adjusted as patterns show. Since 2026-10-16 the agent starts from\n16\t`START_HERE.md`'s \"Make a game\" prompt: `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` and the starter game, ending with\n17\tthe guide's engine feedback section, which feeds this log.\n18\t\n19\t**Asset store lines** (the store's S3, 2026-10-15; the user pastes them into the template). Each\n20\tgame's answers count toward the store's critical mass (`docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md` §13): a need\n21\tis met when the game placed, forked or derived a store entry instead of making it from scratch.\n22\t1. Before you make a prop, material or clip, `assets shelf=prop` (or `shelf=clip`) lists the\n23\t   engine's asset store and `assets find=words` searches it (`store/README.md`).\n24\t2. Assets you looked for: one line each, the query and the id you used, or `none`.\n25\t3. Up to 5 things you built that another game could reuse: the file and one line each.\n26\t\n27\tThe user flags the store entries each game used on a per-game review page (`scripts/\n28\treview_page.py` with a `flags` manifest): looks, feels, reads, works or broken (§7).\n29\t\n30\t**Cost** is counted end to end from the agent's transcript (model calls, tokens read, weighted\n31\tcost at cache reads 0.1, cache writes 1.25, visible output 5; hidden reasoning is not recorded).\n32\t\n33\t## Tally (1 game)\n34\t| Asked for | Games | Impact | Status |\n35\t|---|---|---|---|\n36\t| A supported second-game path: a `Game` hook for step, frame, view and HUD instead of editing `sim.rs`, `frame.rs`, `build.rs` and `app.rs` | 1 | high | **done** (2026-10-16): `pav_core::games` (step, HUD text, status, bot), the starter game, `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`; drawing and HUD widgets still mean view or app code |\n37\t| Entities, shots, health and AI that don't require rapier (`health.rs`, `projectile.rs`) | 1 | high | waiting |\n38\t| A single-file web build (gzip + inline) | 1 | high | **done**: `scripts/build-web.sh --single-file` |\n39\t| Primitive-part models drawn per frame from game state (props can't be) | 1 | medium | **done**: `pav_view::assets::emit_asset` (plan row 4.1) |\n40\t| Stock HUD widgets (bars, radar, off-screen markers, boss bar, banner) | 1 | medium | waiting |\n41\t| Data-driven sound bank (`pav_audio::sounds_for` is a closed match) | 1 | medium | waiting |\n42\t| Per-scene default camera on `load` | 1 | medium | waiting |\n43\t| HUD visible to headless capture | 1 | medium | **done** (2026-10-19): captures draw the game UI (a hook game's HUD lines too); `ui` reads it as text |\n44\t| Open top-down map data (roads, water, spawns) | 1 | low | waiting |\n45\t| Continuous-play and top-down map captures | 1 | low | waiting |\n46\t\n47\t| Engine part | Used | Skipped |\n48\t|---|---|---|\n49\t| `Sim` (rewind, `state_hash`, snapshot), `pav_render::Scene`, particle presets, `pav_audio` synth, `pav_tools` capture/repl/input, the wasm shell and egui, `Rng`/`InputFrame`/bindings | 1 | 0 |\n50\t| rapier entities, `character.rs`, `nav.rs`, `health.rs`, `projectile.rs`, `vehicle.rs` helicopter, `course.rs`, `zones.rs`, `stealth.rs`, `ai.rs` | 0 | 1 |\n51\t| Rooms/TOML, puppet, moves, clips, the asset and creature studios (props, format 2) | 0 | 1 |\n52\t\n53\t## Games\n54\t\n55\t### Capital Strike, from Jungle Strike (1993; SNES, Genesis) — 2026-10-12\n56\t- **Result:** one mission over Washington. Gun, rockets, homing missiles, flares and a dash;\n57\t  armour, fuel and ammo; winch rescues; a motorcade escort; SAMs, AA guns, tanks, gunships and a\n58\t  three-phase boss; loadouts, difficulties, checkpoints, radar and a procedural soundtrack.\n59\t  `ai-hub/games/Jungle-Strike-Capital-Strike-2026-10-12-model-unknown.html` (9.8 MB); source as a\n60\t  patch series against `6618522` in `ai-hub/sources/`.\n61\t- **Agent:** Sonnet at medium effort, a subagent in its own worktree of this repo.\n62\t- **Cost:** 1.65 h, 170 model calls, 54M tokens read (context grew from 56k to 485k), about 6.5M\n63\t  weighted; 106k tokens of visible output. About 4,270 lines written, 3,965 of them game code\n64\t  inside the engine crates.\n65\t- **Verdict (agent's):** the renderer, wasm shell, audio synth and headless tools carried the\n66\t  visuals and the first wasm build ran; the gameplay half didn't help a non-RPG, non-physics game,\n67\t  so 4,000 lines went into a game layer with no engine facility, and the packaging was all its own.\n68\t- **Checked:** the claims about `vehicle.rs` and `health.rs` hold (rapier-bound; no weapons,\n69\t  damage or fuel), and AGENTS.md now says so. The game didn't touch props, format 2 or the studios:\n70\t  its units are SDF and cube parts drawn in code, because a prop can't be drawn from game state.\n71\t- **Acted on:** the single-file web build (every test game needs one), the AGENTS.md wording, and\n72\t  the asset plan's row 4.1 now includes drawing a library asset from game code. Everything else\n73\t  waits for a second game.\n74\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"1ed592d5-7c1f-48a7-90d1-513e8ebce439","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:26.998Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/docs/FEEDBACK.md","content":"# Test-game feedback\n\nEngine feedback from test games: a fresh agent builds a vertical slice of a 1985-2015 classic on\nthis engine, then evaluates the engine. Each game is stored as one self-contained HTML file in\n`michaelcrosato/ai-hub` (`games/`, source zip in `sources/`). The maintainer checks each claim\nagainst the code, fixes what blocks the next game, and waits for patterns before larger work:\nfeatures everyone uses, features no one uses, problems that keep coming back. Newest game first\nunder \"Games\"; the tally is updated with every game.\n\n**Prompt.** The user's template, with the game's name, year and platforms as SOURCE: build an\noriginal spiritual successor as one complete, polished level that doubles as a tech demo; use the\nengine, extend it as needed; deliver one self-contained HTML file; then a one-page candid\nevaluation of the engine (used/not used, engine work you wrote ranked by output saved, engine\nchanges, bugs and wrong docs, source you had to read, fidelity limits, testing, what helped and\nhurt, missing features by impact, the one change). The questions get adjusted as patterns show. Since 2026-10-16 the agent starts from\n`START_HERE.md`'s \"Make a game\" prompt: `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` and the starter game, ending with\nthe guide's engine feedback section, which feeds this log.\n\n**Asset store lines** (the store's S3, 2026-10-15; the user pastes them into the template). Each\ngame's answers count toward the store's critical mass (`docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md` §13): a need\nis met when the game placed, forked or derived a store entry instead of making it from scratch.\n1. Before you make a prop, material or clip, `assets shelf=prop` (or `shelf=clip`) lists the\n   engine's asset store and `assets find=words` searches it (`store/README.md`).\n2. Assets you looked for: one line each, the query and the id you used, or `none`.\n3. Up to 5 things you built that another game could reuse: the file and one line each.\n\nThe user flags the store entries each game used on a per-game review page (`scripts/\nreview_page.py` with a `flags` manifest): looks, feels, reads, works or broken (§7).\n\n**Cost** is counted end to end from the agent's transcript (model calls, tokens read, weighted\ncost at cache reads 0.1, cache writes 1.25, visible output 5; hidden reasoning is not recorded).\n\n## Tally (1 game)\n| Asked for | Games | Impact | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A supported second-game path: a `Game` hook for step, frame, view and HUD instead of editing `sim.rs`, `frame.rs`, `build.rs` and `app.rs` | 1 | high | **done** (2026-10-16): `pav_core::games` (step, HUD text, status, bot), the starter game, `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`; drawing and HUD widgets still mean view or app code |\n| Entities, shots, health and AI that don't require rapier (`health.rs`, `projectile.rs`) | 1 | high | waiting |\n| A single-file web build (gzip + inline) | 1 | high | **done**: `scripts/build-web.sh --single-file` |\n| Primitive-part models drawn per frame from game state (props can't be) | 1 | medium | **done**: `pav_view::assets::emit_asset` (plan row 4.1) |\n| Stock HUD widgets (bars, radar, off-screen markers, boss bar, banner) | 1 | medium | waiting |\n| Data-driven sound bank (`pav_audio::sounds_for` is a closed match) | 1 | medium | waiting |\n| Per-scene default camera on `load` | 1 | medium | waiting |\n| HUD visible to headless capture | 1 | medium | **done** (2026-10-19): captures draw the game UI (a hook game's HUD lines too); `ui` reads it as text |\n| Open top-down map data (roads, water, spawns) | 1 | low | waiting |\n| Continuous-play and top-down map captures | 1 | low | waiting |\n\n| Engine part | Used | Skipped |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Sim` (rewind, `state_hash`, snapshot), `pav_render::Scene`, particle presets, `pav_audio` synth, `pav_tools` capture/repl/input, the wasm shell and egui, `Rng`/`InputFrame`/bindings | 1 | 0 |\n| rapier entities, `character.rs`, `nav.rs`, `health.rs`, `projectile.rs`, `vehicle.rs` helicopter, `course.rs`, `zones.rs`, `stealth.rs`, `ai.rs` | 0 | 1 |\n| Rooms/TOML, puppet, moves, clips, the asset and creature studios (props, format 2) | 0 | 1 |\n\n## Games\n\n### Capital Strike, from Jungle Strike (1993; SNES, Genesis) — 2026-10-12\n- **Result:** one mission over Washington. Gun, rockets, homing missiles, flares and a dash;\n  armour, fuel and ammo; winch rescues; a motorcade escort; SAMs, AA guns, tanks, gunships and a\n  three-phase boss; loadouts, difficulties, checkpoints, radar and a procedural soundtrack.\n  `ai-hub/games/Jungle-Strike-Capital-Strike-2026-10-12-model-unknown.html` (9.8 MB); source as a\n  patch series against `6618522` in `ai-hub/sources/`.\n- **Agent:** Sonnet at medium effort, a subagent in its own worktree of this repo.\n- **Cost:** 1.65 h, 170 model calls, 54M tokens read (context grew from 56k to 485k), about 6.5M\n  weighted; 106k tokens of visible output. About 4,270 lines written, 3,965 of them game code\n  inside the engine crates.\n- **Verdict (agent's):** the renderer, wasm shell, audio synth and headless tools carried the\n  visuals and the first wasm build ran; the gameplay half didn't help a non-RPG, non-physics game,\n  so 4,000 lines went into a game layer with no engine facility, and the packaging was all its own.\n- **Checked:** the claims about `vehicle.rs` and `health.rs` hold (rapier-bound; no weapons,\n  damage or fuel), and AGENTS.md now says so. The game didn't touch props, format 2 or the studios:\n  its units are SDF and cube parts drawn in code, because a prop can't be drawn from game state.\n- **Acted on:** the single-file web build (every test game needs one), the AGENTS.md wording, and\n  the asset plan's row 4.1 now includes drawing a library asset from game code. Everything else\n  waits for a second game.\n","numLines":74,"startLine":1,"totalLines":74}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0167JMNqoss3fSSZ7o96CxuJ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9GzcpFKqm1mR28uZLr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01F66k3ryQDAsTpNuvSLTRzp","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/docs/PROGRESS.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16843,"cache_read_input_tokens":47614,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16843},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"9373b7c7-b2db-4802-ab08-d0b1a0ed8337","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:27.965Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9GzNvfDE9m8V8KHUXs","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01F66k3ryQDAsTpNuvSLTRzp":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/docs/PROGRESS.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F66k3ryQDAsTpNuvSLTRzp","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Progress\n2\t\n3\tThe current state, kept to one page because every session reads it (Claude Code loads it through\n4\t`CLAUDE.md`). The full journal (what was built, how it works, every decision) is\n5\t`docs/HISTORY.md`, newest first: search it when you need the background on something.\n6\t\n7\t## Where things stand (2026-10-27)\n8\t- All engine milestones (M1–M10) and Shardfall milestones (G1–G6) are done. Shardfall is the\n9\t  hack-and-slash showcase built on the engine (`docs/GAME.md`); the pavilion is the engine's\n10\t  tech demo (`docs/DESIGN.md`).\n11\t- Since then: the doctrine (`docs/DOCTRINE.md`; `AGENTS.md` says how it applies); mobile touch\n12\t  controls; moves and clips as data from open libraries, with the **Animation**, **Object** and\n13\t  **Creature Studios** (`docs/*_STUDIO.md`); looks (filters per part, painterly, volumetric\n14\t  light, water, wind); three playable characters (`game/heroes.toml`); the asset system\n15\t  (format-2 props, procedural materials, glTF/OBJ import behind a licence gate, `LIB/core`).\n16\t- **Agent asset store** (`docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md`): stages S0–S5 built (2026-10-15). A fresh\n17\t  agent searches and places store entries (`assets find=`, `shelf=`); 59 core props, 18\n18\t  materials, 437 clips, five KayKit characters as rigid figures. Measured: a library pays only\n19\t  where it holds the asset (S1 no; S3 props met 0.29 of needs against 0.8).\n20\t- **Game makers' handoff** (2026-10-16): an agent making a new game reads one page,\n21\t  `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`, and copies the starter game (`load scene=starter`): the game hook\n22\t  `pav_core::games` (rules, HUD text, status, a bot), its level a room file, `play bot=true\n23\t  seeds=10`. `START_HERE.md` routes and prompts; `scripts/check-handoff.sh` checks the archive.\n24\t- **The game UI through the tools** (2026-10-19): the HUD, windows, passive tree and touch\n25\t  controls live in `pav_ui`; `ui` reads them as text and clicks them, and captures draw them.\n26\t- **On show in the world demo:** the Workshop wing's Library Hall, Limits, Catalogue and Store\n27\t  Showcase, and the animation wing's Clip Browser (every clip on the puppet and the five figures).\n28\t  The figures ship in every build (`props::store::SHIPPED`).\n29\t- Builds (2026-11-02): no hosted site, Vercel is retired. The user plays and tests one-file\n30\t  builds made here and sent in chat: the self-contained `shardfall.html` (`build-web.sh\n31\t  --single-file`), the Windows `.exe` and the Linux binary (`CLAUDE.md`). Pavilion Lite\n32\t  (`pavilion-lite/`) is frozen.\n33\t\n34\t## Next\n35\t- **Gameplay and content:** add data (themes, levels, families, affixes, uniques, tree clusters,\n36\t  characters) and check it with the tools (`levelmap`, `see`, `campaign`, `turntable`,\n37\t  `animsheet`).\n38\t- **On hold (the user's call, 2026-10-17): co-op** (`docs/COOP_PLAN.md`), up to four players\n39\t  roaming one world. Planned in stages C0-C6 and kept for later; don't start it until the user\n40\t  says.\n41\t- **Test games** (`docs/FEEDBACK.md`): fresh agents build a classic's vertical slice on the\n42\t  engine from `START_HERE.md`'s \"Make a game\" prompt; each goes to `michaelcrosato/ai-hub` as\n43\t  one HTML file. One so far (Capital Strike, before the hook).\n44\t- **Waiting on the user:** S3b's rigid-or-skinned verdict (the review page opens again and\n45\t  holds no verdicts yet, 2026-10-21: https://claude.ai/artifact/VjP2RUbwaSJ3ggXkrJJnQs); S4's\n46\t  and S5's other halves run as sessions in my-3dge and riftlight from `docs/handoffs/` (not\n47\t  started: my-3dge still pins Shardfall at `fa2dab6`).\n48\t- **Armed, not run** (`docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md` §13): S6 phase-out at each change of the\n49\t  benchmark agent, and the triggers listed there, each with a small plan when it fires.\n50\t\n51\t## Open issues\n52\t- Balance: the `campaign` bot clears levels 1-12 on seeds 1-3 (63-114 game minutes, 2026-10-19),\n53\t  but levels 4 and 12 are grinds where it dies up to 56 times wearing the boss down.\n54\t- Older known issues and limits: \"Known issues and limits\" in `docs/HISTORY.md`.\n55\t\n56\t## Keeping this page current\n57\tWhen you finish something, write it up as a dated entry at the top of `docs/HISTORY.md` (what\n58\tchanged, how it works, the decisions), then update the sections above. Keep this page under 60\n59\tlines: `pav_tools/tests/docs_limits.rs` fails past that, so move detail to HISTORY.\n60\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"7b20e764-5066-4b44-b659-9b6a2a57264b","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:28.023Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/docs/PROGRESS.md","content":"# Progress\n\nThe current state, kept to one page because every session reads it (Claude Code loads it through\n`CLAUDE.md`). The full journal (what was built, how it works, every decision) is\n`docs/HISTORY.md`, newest first: search it when you need the background on something.\n\n## Where things stand (2026-10-27)\n- All engine milestones (M1–M10) and Shardfall milestones (G1–G6) are done. Shardfall is the\n  hack-and-slash showcase built on the engine (`docs/GAME.md`); the pavilion is the engine's\n  tech demo (`docs/DESIGN.md`).\n- Since then: the doctrine (`docs/DOCTRINE.md`; `AGENTS.md` says how it applies); mobile touch\n  controls; moves and clips as data from open libraries, with the **Animation**, **Object** and\n  **Creature Studios** (`docs/*_STUDIO.md`); looks (filters per part, painterly, volumetric\n  light, water, wind); three playable characters (`game/heroes.toml`); the asset system\n  (format-2 props, procedural materials, glTF/OBJ import behind a licence gate, `LIB/core`).\n- **Agent asset store** (`docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md`): stages S0–S5 built (2026-10-15). A fresh\n  agent searches and places store entries (`assets find=`, `shelf=`); 59 core props, 18\n  materials, 437 clips, five KayKit characters as rigid figures. Measured: a library pays only\n  where it holds the asset (S1 no; S3 props met 0.29 of needs against 0.8).\n- **Game makers' handoff** (2026-10-16): an agent making a new game reads one page,\n  `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`, and copies the starter game (`load scene=starter`): the game hook\n  `pav_core::games` (rules, HUD text, status, a bot), its level a room file, `play bot=true\n  seeds=10`. `START_HERE.md` routes and prompts; `scripts/check-handoff.sh` checks the archive.\n- **The game UI through the tools** (2026-10-19): the HUD, windows, passive tree and touch\n  controls live in `pav_ui`; `ui` reads them as text and clicks them, and captures draw them.\n- **On show in the world demo:** the Workshop wing's Library Hall, Limits, Catalogue and Store\n  Showcase, and the animation wing's Clip Browser (every clip on the puppet and the five figures).\n  The figures ship in every build (`props::store::SHIPPED`).\n- Builds (2026-11-02): no hosted site, Vercel is retired. The user plays and tests one-file\n  builds made here and sent in chat: the self-contained `shardfall.html` (`build-web.sh\n  --single-file`), the Windows `.exe` and the Linux binary (`CLAUDE.md`). Pavilion Lite\n  (`pavilion-lite/`) is frozen.\n\n## Next\n- **Gameplay and content:** add data (themes, levels, families, affixes, uniques, tree clusters,\n  characters) and check it with the tools (`levelmap`, `see`, `campaign`, `turntable`,\n  `animsheet`).\n- **On hold (the user's call, 2026-10-17): co-op** (`docs/COOP_PLAN.md`), up to four players\n  roaming one world. Planned in stages C0-C6 and kept for later; don't start it until the user\n  says.\n- **Test games** (`docs/FEEDBACK.md`): fresh agents build a classic's vertical slice on the\n  engine from `START_HERE.md`'s \"Make a game\" prompt; each goes to `michaelcrosato/ai-hub` as\n  one HTML file. One so far (Capital Strike, before the hook).\n- **Waiting on the user:** S3b's rigid-or-skinned verdict (the review page opens again and\n  holds no verdicts yet, 2026-10-21: https://claude.ai/artifact/VjP2RUbwaSJ3ggXkrJJnQs); S4's\n  and S5's other halves run as sessions in my-3dge and riftlight from `docs/handoffs/` (not\n  started: my-3dge still pins Shardfall at `fa2dab6`).\n- **Armed, not run** (`docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md` §13): S6 phase-out at each change of the\n  benchmark agent, and the triggers listed there, each with a small plan when it fires.\n\n## Open issues\n- Balance: the `campaign` bot clears levels 1-12 on seeds 1-3 (63-114 game minutes, 2026-10-19),\n  but levels 4 and 12 are grinds where it dies up to 56 times wearing the boss down.\n- Older known issues and limits: \"Known issues and limits\" in `docs/HISTORY.md`.\n\n## Keeping this page current\nWhen you finish something, write it up as a dated entry at the top of `docs/HISTORY.md` (what\nchanged, how it works, the decisions), then update the sections above. Keep this page under 60\nlines: `pav_tools/tests/docs_limits.rs` fails past that, so move detail to HISTORY.\n","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":60}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01F66k3ryQDAsTpNuvSLTRzp","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9GzcpFKqm1mR28uZLr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BkNgnQbkDPQPVHWNPrLXqx","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/docs/DOCTRINE.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16843,"cache_read_input_tokens":47614,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16843},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"68d548b8-818f-4591-914b-6454e826e7ee","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:28.917Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9GzNvfDE9m8V8KHUXs","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01BkNgnQbkDPQPVHWNPrLXqx":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/docs/DOCTRINE.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BkNgnQbkDPQPVHWNPrLXqx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Agent-First Game Engine: Doctrine\n2\t\n3\t## How to read this\n4\t\n5\tRead this as a trusted, capable manager building an engine that you and others will use. We don't need to tell you to lock the door at night, keep company secrets off public message boards, avoid burning the place down, or get approval before spending money. Use your judgment. When you think going against this doctrine is best, escalate and explain your reasoning. Rules, principles need to be flexible and we have a process to allow for that.\n6\t\n7\t## North Star\n8\t\n9\tBuild a game engine designed from the ground up for AI coding agents (\"agents\"). Every layer is built so agents can develop, test, inspect, and debug it directly. We deliberately leave behind human-centric development tooling and any development method agents cannot use well. The result: games built faster, more consistently, and at higher quality than with general-purpose engines retrofitted for agents.\n10\t\n11\tAnalogy: build a fully autonomous fighter jet with no cockpit. Removing the pilot removes the constraints a human imposes on the design. Humans give direction remotely; agents fly the plane.\n12\t\n13\tOptimize for the best expected overall output, not flawless software. Solve problems when they actually surface, not in anticipation.\n14\t\n15\t## Principles\n16\t\n17\t1. Agent-readable\n18\t   Code, data, and project structure are optimized for agent comprehension over conventions that serve only humans.\n19\t\n20\t2. Agent-operable\n21\t   Every capability is exposed through machine interfaces. Nothing requires a GUI unless no other option.\n22\t\n23\t3. Verifiable without a display\n24\t   Everything can be run, tested, inspected, and verified without a screen or a human watching.\n25\t\n26\t4. Agent-accessible assets\n27\t   Source assets are created in this order of preference:\n28\t   1. Procedural: code and data that generate the asset.\n29\t   2. Text/data: formats agents read and edit directly.\n30\t   3. Binary: only with human approval. Approved: fonts (WOFF2, TTF, OTF).\n31\t\n32\t   Rationale: agents work best with code and data, and are improving at that faster than legacy asset tools are improving for agents.\n33\t\n34\t5. Reproducible on the development platform\n35\t   On the development platform, identical inputs and seeds produce identical gameplay state, and any gameplay state can be captured, restored, and replayed.\n36\t\n37\t6. WebGPU only\n38\t   WebGPU is the only renderer, in a browser or through a native implementation (ie Vulkan). Gameplay runs only on the CPU. Game UI in HTML/CSS is allowed.\n39\t\n40\t7. Common ground\n41\t   Everything agents work with when building games uses widely used languages, libraries, systems, and patterns. Favor flexibility, ease of use, and established approaches over cutting-edge performance and capabilities.\n42\t\n43\t   Analogy: build the Sherman, not the Tiger.\n44\t\n45\t   Rationale: lean into what agents already do well rather than trying to change it.\n46\t\n47\t8. Quality under the hood\n48\t   Engine internals, meaning code whose API does not appear in game code, use the highest-quality approach their builder can execute well, however complex. Reuse what others have already built well, and invest heavily in what is ours. That complexity stays behind the API: agents need to use the engine well, not read or rebuild it, even when something goes wrong.\n49\t\n50\t   Rationale: the engine is built once and used many times, so better internals raise the quality of everything built on it.\n51\t\n52\t9. Mastery over novelty\n53\t   Use the newest version that is at least 12 months old, so agents know it deeply. A newer version that is backward compatible with a qualifying version, or otherwise works the same way, is adopted immediately, since agents' existing knowledge still applies. Within engine internals covered by principle 8, the builder may use newer versions when they raise quality and it can use them well. Upgrade as newer versions qualify.\n54\t\n55\t10. Discovery first, hardening later\n56\t    Develop and test against a single target platform: the one the development environment runs on. Cross-platform compatibility and hardening happen when a game goes to production.\n57\t\n58\t    Rationale: most of the work is finding something worth shipping.\n59\t\n60\t## Escalation\n61\t\n62\tWhen an action requires human approval or a principle cannot be satisfied, escalate and wait. If there is no response within 15 minutes, commit current work, make the call, and continue. For the rest of that run, or until a human responds, report further conflicts without stopping. Record every escalation, every later conflict, and every call made without a response, so each can be found and reviewed.\n63\t\n64\tRationale: progress never stalls indefinitely. The worst case is a review, a change, or a rollback.\n65\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"496472b4-29f1-4e54-b065-9e468e8b2b82","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:28.947Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/docs/DOCTRINE.md","content":"# Agent-First Game Engine: Doctrine\n\n## How to read this\n\nRead this as a trusted, capable manager building an engine that you and others will use. We don't need to tell you to lock the door at night, keep company secrets off public message boards, avoid burning the place down, or get approval before spending money. Use your judgment. When you think going against this doctrine is best, escalate and explain your reasoning. Rules, principles need to be flexible and we have a process to allow for that.\n\n## North Star\n\nBuild a game engine designed from the ground up for AI coding agents (\"agents\"). Every layer is built so agents can develop, test, inspect, and debug it directly. We deliberately leave behind human-centric development tooling and any development method agents cannot use well. The result: games built faster, more consistently, and at higher quality than with general-purpose engines retrofitted for agents.\n\nAnalogy: build a fully autonomous fighter jet with no cockpit. Removing the pilot removes the constraints a human imposes on the design. Humans give direction remotely; agents fly the plane.\n\nOptimize for the best expected overall output, not flawless software. Solve problems when they actually surface, not in anticipation.\n\n## Principles\n\n1. Agent-readable\n   Code, data, and project structure are optimized for agent comprehension over conventions that serve only humans.\n\n2. Agent-operable\n   Every capability is exposed through machine interfaces. Nothing requires a GUI unless no other option.\n\n3. Verifiable without a display\n   Everything can be run, tested, inspected, and verified without a screen or a human watching.\n\n4. Agent-accessible assets\n   Source assets are created in this order of preference:\n   1. Procedural: code and data that generate the asset.\n   2. Text/data: formats agents read and edit directly.\n   3. Binary: only with human approval. Approved: fonts (WOFF2, TTF, OTF).\n\n   Rationale: agents work best with code and data, and are improving at that faster than legacy asset tools are improving for agents.\n\n5. Reproducible on the development platform\n   On the development platform, identical inputs and seeds produce identical gameplay state, and any gameplay state can be captured, restored, and replayed.\n\n6. WebGPU only\n   WebGPU is the only renderer, in a browser or through a native implementation (ie Vulkan). Gameplay runs only on the CPU. Game UI in HTML/CSS is allowed.\n\n7. Common ground\n   Everything agents work with when building games uses widely used languages, libraries, systems, and patterns. Favor flexibility, ease of use, and established approaches over cutting-edge performance and capabilities.\n\n   Analogy: build the Sherman, not the Tiger.\n\n   Rationale: lean into what agents already do well rather than trying to change it.\n\n8. Quality under the hood\n   Engine internals, meaning code whose API does not appear in game code, use the highest-quality approach their builder can execute well, however complex. Reuse what others have already built well, and invest heavily in what is ours. That complexity stays behind the API: agents need to use the engine well, not read or rebuild it, even when something goes wrong.\n\n   Rationale: the engine is built once and used many times, so better internals raise the quality of everything built on it.\n\n9. Mastery over novelty\n   Use the newest version that is at least 12 months old, so agents know it deeply. A newer version that is backward compatible with a qualifying version, or otherwise works the same way, is adopted immediately, since agents' existing knowledge still applies. Within engine internals covered by principle 8, the builder may use newer versions when they raise quality and it can use them well. Upgrade as newer versions qualify.\n\n10. Discovery first, hardening later\n    Develop and test against a single target platform: the one the development environment runs on. Cross-platform compatibility and hardening happen when a game goes to production.\n\n    Rationale: most of the work is finding something worth shipping.\n\n## Escalation\n\nWhen an action requires human approval or a principle cannot be satisfied, escalate and wait. If there is no response within 15 minutes, commit current work, make the call, and continue. For the rest of that run, or until a human responds, report further conflicts without stopping. Record every escalation, every later conflict, and every call made without a response, so each can be found and reviewed.\n\nRationale: progress never stalls indefinitely. The worst case is a review, a change, or a rollback.\n","numLines":65,"startLine":1,"totalLines":65}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01BkNgnQbkDPQPVHWNPrLXqx","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9GzcpFKqm1mR28uZLr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Kxrf958WC1Neu7NAyjRyXQ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/docs/DESIGN.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16843,"cache_read_input_tokens":47614,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16843},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"1c8ff292-cff8-4f4a-a4fb-f6b61df14490","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:29.966Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9GzNvfDE9m8V8KHUXs","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Kxrf958WC1Neu7NAyjRyXQ":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/docs/DESIGN.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Kxrf958WC1Neu7NAyjRyXQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Shardfall — Engine & Showcase Design Plan\n2\t\n3\t\"Shardfall\" is the game and repository name. \"Pavilion\" is the engine's playable tech demo.\n4\tThis document is the source of truth for what we are building and why; the principles are in `docs/DOCTRINE.md`.\n5\tIf a pasted version of this plan in chat differs from this file, the pasted version wins; update this file to match.\n6\t\n7\t## 1. What we're building\n8\tAn AI-first game engine, plus a living tech-demo showcase built on it, written in Rust. Everything you see and hear is generated from code.\n9\t\n10\tThe showcase is a continuous pavilion of themed wings and test rooms set inside an infinite streaming world. It serves as a personal evaluation lab and as living documentation.\n11\t\n12\tThe engine is meant to be reused for many different games. We don't know yet what game we're making, so flexibility wins over polish.\n13\t\n14\t## 2. Principles\n15\t`docs/DOCTRINE.md` sets the principles and the escalation process, and outranks this document.\n16\t`AGENTS.md` says how the doctrine applies to this repo and lists the working rules under it:\n17\tgameplay first, use tools, run well on a modern PC, approximations that feel right, check\n18\tselectively, token efficiency.\n19\t\n20\t## 3. Platform & tech\n21\t- **Where it runs:**\n22\t  - Native desktop app.\n23\t  - The human plays on Windows 11 (RTX 3060 Ti at work, RTX 4070 Super at home).\n24\t  - Agents build, run and test on Linux (cloud containers, WSL2 Ubuntu).\n25\t- **Language:** Rust. The stable toolchain is pinned with `rust-toolchain.toml`, and `Cargo.lock` is committed.\n26\t- **Graphics:** wgpu, which implements the WebGPU API.\n27\t  - Vulkan is the default backend on Windows and Linux.\n28\t  - DX12 can be selected with a startup setting.\n29\t  - No OpenGL or WebGL.\n30\t- **Headless rendering for agents:** Mesa lavapipe (software Vulkan) on machines without a GPU. Same engine code, different driver.\n31\t- **Physics:** rapier3d. Coordinates are f32, with a one-line switch to f64 (`rapier3d-f64`) if we ever see jitter far from the origin.\n32\t- **Other libraries:** winit for window and input, gilrs for gamepads, cpal for audio output (with our own synthesizer), egui for the debug UI.\n33\t- **Browser build:** a late milestone. The same code compiles to WebAssembly and WebGPU. Library choices keep this possible, but we don't maintain it until then.\n34\t- **Assets:** no image, model, texture or audio files. Geometry, surfaces, effects and sound are all generated from code. Fonts are allowed, for UI and in-world text.\n35\t\n36\t## 4. World & camera\n37\t- **The world is fully 3D.** \"2D top-down\" is a camera preset, not an engine limit.\n38\t- **Camera parameters are live:**\n39\t  - tilt, from 90° (straight down) to 0° (side view);\n40\t  - rotation;\n41\t  - distance/zoom;\n42\t  - orthographic or perspective projection;\n43\t  - field of view.\n44\t\n45\t  They can be changed mid-game from the tuning panel, each room saves its own defaults, and game code can animate the camera.\n46\t- **Standard camera helpers:**\n47\t  - fade or cut away geometry between the camera and the player;\n48\t  - hide upper floors while the player is below them;\n49\t  - mouse aim targets the point on the ground plane at the player's height;\n50\t  - movement input is relative to the camera's rotation.\n51\t- **Default look:** tilted to about 60–75°, stylized with flat or cel shading and outlines. A 90° orthographic view gives the clean 2D look.\n52\t\n53\t## 5. Rendering\n54\t- **Pipeline:** a real 3D renderer with depth, MSAA, HDR and a post-processing chain.\n55\t- **Procedural geometry:** boxes, rounded boxes, cylinders, spheres, capsules, extruded shapes, tile terrain, tubes and ropes.\n56\t- **Analytic SDF primitives** (spheres, capsules, etc.) are pixel-perfect at any zoom, and can blend smoothly into each other for organic shapes.\n57\t- **Swappable styles** per room or per object: flat 2D look, cel/toon, or fully lit.\n58\t- **Lighting:** dynamic lights with real shadows, emissive surfaces and bloom.\n59\t- **GPU compute is cosmetic only** (particles and similar). Anything that affects gameplay runs on the CPU.\n60\t- **Text:** in-world text is rendered by the engine from a font; UI text goes through egui.\n61\t\n62\t## 6. Characters\n63\t- **Puppet model:** an invisible skeleton, driven by procedural animation, with parts attached to the bones.\n64\t  - **Procedural animation:** walk cycles driven by speed, foot IK to the ground, leaning into turns, recoil, squash-and-stretch, and secondary motion.\n65\t  - **Parts:** mostly spheres and capsules, so silhouettes read as clean 2D shapes from any camera angle.\n66\t- **One motion, many looks:**\n67\t  - a cutout (flat 2D art on a camera-facing card, for fixed-camera designs);\n68\t  - a puppet with a flat 2D look;\n69\t  - a lit puppet.\n70\t- **Camera-aware rules** recover 2D \"cheats\", such as eyes that stay visible or a body that leans toward the viewer. Stepped animation (\"on twos\") is optional.\n71\t- **Everything is a parameter:** proportions, colors and limb count are sliders.\n72\t\n73\t## 7. Physics, height & movement\n74\t- **Real verticality:**\n75\t  - jumping gaps, climbing onto boxes, ladders, ledges and falls;\n76\t  - crawling under things and ducking projectiles;\n77\t  - multi-floor buildings with destructible floor tiles (blow a hole, drop to the floor below).\n78\t- **Flat rooms** lock motion to a plane (bullet hell). **Side-view rooms** are a camera setting plus an axis lock, so Celeste-style rooms are possible.\n79\t- **High-count projectiles and bullets** use a lightweight custom system, not physics bodies.\n80\t- **Movement models** plug in per character, can be switched from a dropdown per room, and every number in them is tunable:\n81\t  - **Instant:** immediate response, with a hold-to-slow focus button.\n82\t  - **Momentum:** acceleration, skidding, air control.\n83\t  - **Grid/tile:** step-by-step movement.\n84\t  - **Committed animations:** moves play out fully once started.\n85\t  - **Vehicle:** grip and drift.\n86\t  - **Flight:** banking and inertia.\n87\t  - **Special states:** climbing and swimming.\n88\t\n89\t  Each room marks the model it was designed for. Other models can be tried wherever it makes sense.\n90\t- **Tick rate** defaults to 60 per second, with 120 and 240 selectable. Input delay is measurable, and vsync and smoothing can be toggled.\n91\t\n92\t## 8. Simulation architecture\n93\t- **Headless-first:** the simulation core is a library with no window, graphics or audio dependencies. It runs in two ways:\n94\t  - inside the game, on its own thread; the renderer observes and interpolates, and neither blocks the other;\n95\t  - as a headless command-line program at maximum speed.\n96\t- **Time control:** fixed tick rate, speed control from slow motion to maximum, pause and single-step.\n97\t- **Repeatability (the cheap version):** the same machine, build, seed and recorded inputs give the same result.\n98\t  - It exists to support rewind and bug replays.\n99\t  - It is not guaranteed across machines.\n100\t  - If it ever gets in the way of gameplay or performance, gameplay wins.\n101\t- **Time travel:**\n102\t  - periodic snapshots plus an input log;\n103\t  - rewind restores the nearest snapshot and re-simulates forward;\n104\t  - history is limited by memory (adjustable);\n105\t  - acting after a rewind starts a new timeline;\n106\t  - snapshots can be saved to files.\n107\t- **Streaming:**\n108\t  - the world is split into chunks, loaded around interest points (the player, agents), never around the camera;\n109\t  - terrain regenerates from the seed;\n110\t  - changed or moved objects are saved and restored when you return;\n111\t  - the pavilion sits at the center with wings branching outward, and procedural wilderness beyond it is used for streaming and encounter tests.\n112\t\n113\t## 9. Input\n114\t- **Devices:** keyboard+mouse and gamepad are always supported.\n115\t  - Each room declares a primary device, and its controls and design are built around it.\n116\t  - The secondary device gets a best-effort mapping that never changes the design.\n117\t  - On-screen prompts show the device touched last.\n118\t- **Fixed system layer that never rebinds:** pause, rewind, step, tuning panel, teleport menu, leave room and reset room.\n119\t- **Guidance and layout:** an on-screen control guide appears on entering a room. Keys are bound by physical position.\n120\t\n121\t## 10. Sound\n122\t- Everything is synthesized in code: oscillators, noise, envelopes and filters.\n123\t- The game runs without a sound device, and headless runs can write `.wav` files.\n124\t\n125\t## 11. Tools for humans\n126\t- **Boot diagnostics:**\n127\t  - numbered stages with timings, written to the terminal, a log file and the screen;\n128\t  - if graphics fail to start, a Windows pop-up shows the error and the log file's path;\n129\t  - crash reports appear on screen and in the log.\n130\t- **Tuning panel (egui):**\n131\t  - every registered parameter, grouped: physics, movement, camera, shaders, audio and simulation speed;\n132\t  - presets can be saved, exported and imported;\n133\t  - FPS, tick and frame-timing graphs;\n134\t  - possibly live shader editing.\n135\t- **Sandbox editing:** spawn, drag and delete objects, and save the room to a data file.\n136\t- **Navigation:**\n137\t  - a teleport menu and a reset button per room;\n138\t  - an info card per room saying what it demonstrates and what to try;\n139\t  - a command-line option to launch straight into a room with a given seed.\n140\t\n141\t## 12. Agent layer\n142\t- **One tool registry, three interfaces:** CLI commands, an MCP server (stdio), and later a live bridge into the running game.\n143\t- **Tools:**\n144\t  - list and describe rooms;\n145\t  - start, run, and step N ticks;\n146\t  - spawn, query and modify entities;\n147\t  - get and set parameters;\n148\t  - save, load and rewind snapshots;\n149\t  - record and replay inputs;\n150\t  - capture: screenshots, filmstrips (many frames tiled into one image), timing reports and `.wav` audio;\n151\t  - benchmark ticks per second;\n152\t  - validate and hot-load room files.\n153\t- **Rooms vs mechanics:** rooms built from existing parts are data files that hot-reload with no rebuild. New mechanics are Rust code, written from templates.\n154\t- **Rulebook:** `AGENTS.md` (Claude Code loads it, the doctrine and `PROGRESS.md` through `CLAUDE.md`). It covers how the principles apply, a short architecture map, how to add a tool or a room, and how to test and capture; `docs/REFERENCE.md` holds the full code map, every tool family, the controls and the test suites.\n155\t\n156\t## 13. Showcase roster\n157\t- **Movement & Feel Lab:**\n158\t  - courses: tightrope, slalom, timing gates, dodge gauntlet;\n159\t  - a movement-model switcher, with live input-delay and feel metrics;\n160\t  - a camera bench (sweep angle and projection on the same course);\n161\t  - a multi-floor verticality building (ladders, ledges, crawl spaces, ducking, blow a hole and drop).\n162\t- **Physics Lab:** stacking, springs and soft bodies, chains and rope bridges, conveyors, a bounce and friction gallery, destructible floors, and a stress test with a ticks/sec meter.\n163\t- **Procedural Animation Lab:** walk cycles, jointed limbs (spider or lizard), impact recoil, squash-and-stretch, secondary motion (tails, antennae), and the Character Style Bench (one character, a swingable camera, and a switch between cutout, flat-look puppet and lit puppet).\n164\t- **Visual Effects Wing:** lights and shadows, GPU particles, bloom, screen distortion, and global illumination (stretch goal).\n165\t- **Aesthetic & Filter Wing:** side-by-side comparisons of the pure style against filters (scanlines, CRT curvature, dithering, color grading, pixelation), plus a build-your-own filter stack bench.\n166\t- **Genre Wing:** bullet hell, grid stealth, a drift car and a helicopter.\n167\t\n168\t## 14. Delivery & workflow\n169\t- **Repo:** `michaelcrosato/shardfall`, stays private. No hosted site (Vercel retired\n170\t  2026-11-02): the browser build ships as one self-contained HTML file (`CLAUDE.md`).\n171\t- **Branch:** work on `main` (the GitHub default). Commit and push often, because cloud containers are temporary.\n172\t- **Windows build:** the `.exe` is cross-compiled in the cloud and sent in chat at the end of each milestone. Local WSL2 agents can build the `.exe` and launch it directly on Windows.\n173\t- **Progress log:** `docs/PROGRESS.md` is one page of current state (where things stand, what's next, open issues), so any agent can resume; `docs/HISTORY.md` is the full journal of what was built and the decisions made.\n174\t- **Build order:** sequential. Once the foundation and rulebook exist, self-contained rooms can be handed to cheaper helper agents and reviewed. This also tests the goal that smaller models can extend the engine.\n175\t- **Decisions:** ask the user only about decisions that are genuinely theirs. Otherwise decide, record the decision (in this document if it changes the design, otherwise in `docs/HISTORY.md`), and continue. Escalations follow the doctrine and are logged in `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`.\n176\t\n177\t## 15. Milestones (each ends with a working `.exe`)\n178\t1. **M1 Foundation:**\n179\t   - workspace and crates;\n180\t   - window, wgpu on Vulkan, and boot diagnostics;\n181\t   - a headless simulation loop;\n182\t   - lavapipe capture (screenshots) working in the cloud;\n183\t   - Windows cross-compile verified;\n184\t   - `AGENTS.md` and `PROGRESS.md`.\n185\t\n186\t   *Done when:* the `.exe` opens and shows the boot log and a lit test scene, the headless CLI runs ticks, and capture produces a PNG.\n187\t2. **M2 Core gameplay:**\n188\t   - rapier3d integration and tile-based levels with heights;\n189\t   - character puppet v1;\n190\t   - movement models v1 (instant and momentum);\n191\t   - the camera system, with all parameters live;\n192\t   - keyboard+mouse and gamepad input, with the system layer;\n193\t   - the egui tuning panel;\n194\t   - time controls (pause, step, speed, rewind);\n195\t   - agent CLI and MCP server v1.\n196\t\n197\t   *Done when:* the player can walk, jump onto boxes, climb a ladder, blow a hole in a floor and drop through, tweak camera and movement live, and rewind.\n198\t3. **M3 World & rooms:**\n199\t   - chunk streaming, the pavilion and the wilderness;\n200\t   - the room framework: enter/exit, input switching, control guide, info card, reset, teleport, launch-into-room;\n201\t   - room data files with hot reload;\n202\t   - sandbox editing;\n203\t   - synth sound v1;\n204\t   - filmstrip capture.\n205\t4. **M4:** Movement & Feel Lab.\n206\t5. **M5:** Physics Lab.\n207\t6. **M6:** Procedural Animation Lab, including the Character Style Bench.\n208\t7. **M7:** Visual Effects Wing.\n209\t8. **M8:** Aesthetic & Filter Wing.\n210\t9. **M9:** Genre Wing.\n211\t10. **M10:** Browser build, live agent bridge, and a polish pass.\n212\t\n213\t## 16. Out of scope for now\n214\t- Determinism across machines.\n215\t- Mac support and other browsers.\n216\t- Compatibility work.\n217\t- Code signing.\n218\t- Performance optimization beyond \"runs well on a modern PC\".\n219\t- GitHub automation.\n220\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"508145e7-b711-44ab-bd68-ae1eb437fc6e","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:29.990Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/docs/DESIGN.md","content":"# Shardfall — Engine & Showcase Design Plan\n\n\"Shardfall\" is the game and repository name. \"Pavilion\" is the engine's playable tech demo.\nThis document is the source of truth for what we are building and why; the principles are in `docs/DOCTRINE.md`.\nIf a pasted version of this plan in chat differs from this file, the pasted version wins; update this file to match.\n\n## 1. What we're building\nAn AI-first game engine, plus a living tech-demo showcase built on it, written in Rust. Everything you see and hear is generated from code.\n\nThe showcase is a continuous pavilion of themed wings and test rooms set inside an infinite streaming world. It serves as a personal evaluation lab and as living documentation.\n\nThe engine is meant to be reused for many different games. We don't know yet what game we're making, so flexibility wins over polish.\n\n## 2. Principles\n`docs/DOCTRINE.md` sets the principles and the escalation process, and outranks this document.\n`AGENTS.md` says how the doctrine applies to this repo and lists the working rules under it:\ngameplay first, use tools, run well on a modern PC, approximations that feel right, check\nselectively, token efficiency.\n\n## 3. Platform & tech\n- **Where it runs:**\n  - Native desktop app.\n  - The human plays on Windows 11 (RTX 3060 Ti at work, RTX 4070 Super at home).\n  - Agents build, run and test on Linux (cloud containers, WSL2 Ubuntu).\n- **Language:** Rust. The stable toolchain is pinned with `rust-toolchain.toml`, and `Cargo.lock` is committed.\n- **Graphics:** wgpu, which implements the WebGPU API.\n  - Vulkan is the default backend on Windows and Linux.\n  - DX12 can be selected with a startup setting.\n  - No OpenGL or WebGL.\n- **Headless rendering for agents:** Mesa lavapipe (software Vulkan) on machines without a GPU. Same engine code, different driver.\n- **Physics:** rapier3d. Coordinates are f32, with a one-line switch to f64 (`rapier3d-f64`) if we ever see jitter far from the origin.\n- **Other libraries:** winit for window and input, gilrs for gamepads, cpal for audio output (with our own synthesizer), egui for the debug UI.\n- **Browser build:** a late milestone. The same code compiles to WebAssembly and WebGPU. Library choices keep this possible, but we don't maintain it until then.\n- **Assets:** no image, model, texture or audio files. Geometry, surfaces, effects and sound are all generated from code. Fonts are allowed, for UI and in-world text.\n\n## 4. World & camera\n- **The world is fully 3D.** \"2D top-down\" is a camera preset, not an engine limit.\n- **Camera parameters are live:**\n  - tilt, from 90° (straight down) to 0° (side view);\n  - rotation;\n  - distance/zoom;\n  - orthographic or perspective projection;\n  - field of view.\n\n  They can be changed mid-game from the tuning panel, each room saves its own defaults, and game code can animate the camera.\n- **Standard camera helpers:**\n  - fade or cut away geometry between the camera and the player;\n  - hide upper floors while the player is below them;\n  - mouse aim targets the point on the ground plane at the player's height;\n  - movement input is relative to the camera's rotation.\n- **Default look:** tilted to about 60–75°, stylized with flat or cel shading and outlines. A 90° orthographic view gives the clean 2D look.\n\n## 5. Rendering\n- **Pipeline:** a real 3D renderer with depth, MSAA, HDR and a post-processing chain.\n- **Procedural geometry:** boxes, rounded boxes, cylinders, spheres, capsules, extruded shapes, tile terrain, tubes and ropes.\n- **Analytic SDF primitives** (spheres, capsules, etc.) are pixel-perfect at any zoom, and can blend smoothly into each other for organic shapes.\n- **Swappable styles** per room or per object: flat 2D look, cel/toon, or fully lit.\n- **Lighting:** dynamic lights with real shadows, emissive surfaces and bloom.\n- **GPU compute is cosmetic only** (particles and similar). Anything that affects gameplay runs on the CPU.\n- **Text:** in-world text is rendered by the engine from a font; UI text goes through egui.\n\n## 6. Characters\n- **Puppet model:** an invisible skeleton, driven by procedural animation, with parts attached to the bones.\n  - **Procedural animation:** walk cycles driven by speed, foot IK to the ground, leaning into turns, recoil, squash-and-stretch, and secondary motion.\n  - **Parts:** mostly spheres and capsules, so silhouettes read as clean 2D shapes from any camera angle.\n- **One motion, many looks:**\n  - a cutout (flat 2D art on a camera-facing card, for fixed-camera designs);\n  - a puppet with a flat 2D look;\n  - a lit puppet.\n- **Camera-aware rules** recover 2D \"cheats\", such as eyes that stay visible or a body that leans toward the viewer. Stepped animation (\"on twos\") is optional.\n- **Everything is a parameter:** proportions, colors and limb count are sliders.\n\n## 7. Physics, height & movement\n- **Real verticality:**\n  - jumping gaps, climbing onto boxes, ladders, ledges and falls;\n  - crawling under things and ducking projectiles;\n  - multi-floor buildings with destructible floor tiles (blow a hole, drop to the floor below).\n- **Flat rooms** lock motion to a plane (bullet hell). **Side-view rooms** are a camera setting plus an axis lock, so Celeste-style rooms are possible.\n- **High-count projectiles and bullets** use a lightweight custom system, not physics bodies.\n- **Movement models** plug in per character, can be switched from a dropdown per room, and every number in them is tunable:\n  - **Instant:** immediate response, with a hold-to-slow focus button.\n  - **Momentum:** acceleration, skidding, air control.\n  - **Grid/tile:** step-by-step movement.\n  - **Committed animations:** moves play out fully once started.\n  - **Vehicle:** grip and drift.\n  - **Flight:** banking and inertia.\n  - **Special states:** climbing and swimming.\n\n  Each room marks the model it was designed for. Other models can be tried wherever it makes sense.\n- **Tick rate** defaults to 60 per second, with 120 and 240 selectable. Input delay is measurable, and vsync and smoothing can be toggled.\n\n## 8. Simulation architecture\n- **Headless-first:** the simulation core is a library with no window, graphics or audio dependencies. It runs in two ways:\n  - inside the game, on its own thread; the renderer observes and interpolates, and neither blocks the other;\n  - as a headless command-line program at maximum speed.\n- **Time control:** fixed tick rate, speed control from slow motion to maximum, pause and single-step.\n- **Repeatability (the cheap version):** the same machine, build, seed and recorded inputs give the same result.\n  - It exists to support rewind and bug replays.\n  - It is not guaranteed across machines.\n  - If it ever gets in the way of gameplay or performance, gameplay wins.\n- **Time travel:**\n  - periodic snapshots plus an input log;\n  - rewind restores the nearest snapshot and re-simulates forward;\n  - history is limited by memory (adjustable);\n  - acting after a rewind starts a new timeline;\n  - snapshots can be saved to files.\n- **Streaming:**\n  - the world is split into chunks, loaded around interest points (the player, agents), never around the camera;\n  - terrain regenerates from the seed;\n  - changed or moved objects are saved and restored when you return;\n  - the pavilion sits at the center with wings branching outward, and procedural wilderness beyond it is used for streaming and encounter tests.\n\n## 9. Input\n- **Devices:** keyboard+mouse and gamepad are always supported.\n  - Each room declares a primary device, and its controls and design are built around it.\n  - The secondary device gets a best-effort mapping that never changes the design.\n  - On-screen prompts show the device touched last.\n- **Fixed system layer that never rebinds:** pause, rewind, step, tuning panel, teleport menu, leave room and reset room.\n- **Guidance and layout:** an on-screen control guide appears on entering a room. Keys are bound by physical position.\n\n## 10. Sound\n- Everything is synthesized in code: oscillators, noise, envelopes and filters.\n- The game runs without a sound device, and headless runs can write `.wav` files.\n\n## 11. Tools for humans\n- **Boot diagnostics:**\n  - numbered stages with timings, written to the terminal, a log file and the screen;\n  - if graphics fail to start, a Windows pop-up shows the error and the log file's path;\n  - crash reports appear on screen and in the log.\n- **Tuning panel (egui):**\n  - every registered parameter, grouped: physics, movement, camera, shaders, audio and simulation speed;\n  - presets can be saved, exported and imported;\n  - FPS, tick and frame-timing graphs;\n  - possibly live shader editing.\n- **Sandbox editing:** spawn, drag and delete objects, and save the room to a data file.\n- **Navigation:**\n  - a teleport menu and a reset button per room;\n  - an info card per room saying what it demonstrates and what to try;\n  - a command-line option to launch straight into a room with a given seed.\n\n## 12. Agent layer\n- **One tool registry, three interfaces:** CLI commands, an MCP server (stdio), and later a live bridge into the running game.\n- **Tools:**\n  - list and describe rooms;\n  - start, run, and step N ticks;\n  - spawn, query and modify entities;\n  - get and set parameters;\n  - save, load and rewind snapshots;\n  - record and replay inputs;\n  - capture: screenshots, filmstrips (many frames tiled into one image), timing reports and `.wav` audio;\n  - benchmark ticks per second;\n  - validate and hot-load room files.\n- **Rooms vs mechanics:** rooms built from existing parts are data files that hot-reload with no rebuild. New mechanics are Rust code, written from templates.\n- **Rulebook:** `AGENTS.md` (Claude Code loads it, the doctrine and `PROGRESS.md` through `CLAUDE.md`). It covers how the principles apply, a short architecture map, how to add a tool or a room, and how to test and capture; `docs/REFERENCE.md` holds the full code map, every tool family, the controls and the test suites.\n\n## 13. Showcase roster\n- **Movement & Feel Lab:**\n  - courses: tightrope, slalom, timing gates, dodge gauntlet;\n  - a movement-model switcher, with live input-delay and feel metrics;\n  - a camera bench (sweep angle and projection on the same course);\n  - a multi-floor verticality building (ladders, ledges, crawl spaces, ducking, blow a hole and drop).\n- **Physics Lab:** stacking, springs and soft bodies, chains and rope bridges, conveyors, a bounce and friction gallery, destructible floors, and a stress test with a ticks/sec meter.\n- **Procedural Animation Lab:** walk cycles, jointed limbs (spider or lizard), impact recoil, squash-and-stretch, secondary motion (tails, antennae), and the Character Style Bench (one character, a swingable camera, and a switch between cutout, flat-look puppet and lit puppet).\n- **Visual Effects Wing:** lights and shadows, GPU particles, bloom, screen distortion, and global illumination (stretch goal).\n- **Aesthetic & Filter Wing:** side-by-side comparisons of the pure style against filters (scanlines, CRT curvature, dithering, color grading, pixelation), plus a build-your-own filter stack bench.\n- **Genre Wing:** bullet hell, grid stealth, a drift car and a helicopter.\n\n## 14. Delivery & workflow\n- **Repo:** `michaelcrosato/shardfall`, stays private. No hosted site (Vercel retired\n  2026-11-02): the browser build ships as one self-contained HTML file (`CLAUDE.md`).\n- **Branch:** work on `main` (the GitHub default). Commit and push often, because cloud containers are temporary.\n- **Windows build:** the `.exe` is cross-compiled in the cloud and sent in chat at the end of each milestone. Local WSL2 agents can build the `.exe` and launch it directly on Windows.\n- **Progress log:** `docs/PROGRESS.md` is one page of current state (where things stand, what's next, open issues), so any agent can resume; `docs/HISTORY.md` is the full journal of what was built and the decisions made.\n- **Build order:** sequential. Once the foundation and rulebook exist, self-contained rooms can be handed to cheaper helper agents and reviewed. This also tests the goal that smaller models can extend the engine.\n- **Decisions:** ask the user only about decisions that are genuinely theirs. Otherwise decide, record the decision (in this document if it changes the design, otherwise in `docs/HISTORY.md`), and continue. Escalations follow the doctrine and are logged in `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`.\n\n## 15. Milestones (each ends with a working `.exe`)\n1. **M1 Foundation:**\n   - workspace and crates;\n   - window, wgpu on Vulkan, and boot diagnostics;\n   - a headless simulation loop;\n   - lavapipe capture (screenshots) working in the cloud;\n   - Windows cross-compile verified;\n   - `AGENTS.md` and `PROGRESS.md`.\n\n   *Done when:* the `.exe` opens and shows the boot log and a lit test scene, the headless CLI runs ticks, and capture produces a PNG.\n2. **M2 Core gameplay:**\n   - rapier3d integration and tile-based levels with heights;\n   - character puppet v1;\n   - movement models v1 (instant and momentum);\n   - the camera system, with all parameters live;\n   - keyboard+mouse and gamepad input, with the system layer;\n   - the egui tuning panel;\n   - time controls (pause, step, speed, rewind);\n   - agent CLI and MCP server v1.\n\n   *Done when:* the player can walk, jump onto boxes, climb a ladder, blow a hole in a floor and drop through, tweak camera and movement live, and rewind.\n3. **M3 World & rooms:**\n   - chunk streaming, the pavilion and the wilderness;\n   - the room framework: enter/exit, input switching, control guide, info card, reset, teleport, launch-into-room;\n   - room data files with hot reload;\n   - sandbox editing;\n   - synth sound v1;\n   - filmstrip capture.\n4. **M4:** Movement & Feel Lab.\n5. **M5:** Physics Lab.\n6. **M6:** Procedural Animation Lab, including the Character Style Bench.\n7. **M7:** Visual Effects Wing.\n8. **M8:** Aesthetic & Filter Wing.\n9. **M9:** Genre Wing.\n10. **M10:** Browser build, live agent bridge, and a polish pass.\n\n## 16. Out of scope for now\n- Determinism across machines.\n- Mac support and other browsers.\n- Compatibility work.\n- Code signing.\n- Performance optimization beyond \"runs well on a modern PC\".\n- GitHub automation.\n","numLines":220,"startLine":1,"totalLines":220}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Kxrf958WC1Neu7NAyjRyXQ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9GzcpFKqm1mR28uZLr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015n7UoVTxFvb4e4AhDPrRqa","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/crates/pav_core/src/games/mod.rs"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16843,"cache_read_input_tokens":47614,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16843},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"e6bee1cd-5d12-4efa-aa41-696037d7697e","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:30.817Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9GzNvfDE9m8V8KHUXs","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_015n7UoVTxFvb4e4AhDPrRqa":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/crates/pav_core/src/games/mod.rs"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015n7UoVTxFvb4e4AhDPrRqa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Games built on the engine: the hook a new game implements, and the list of games.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! A game is a struct holding its rules' state (`Clone` and serde, like everything in\n4\t//! `SimState`) that implements [`Game`], plus a `build` function that makes its scene. One\n5\t//! line in [`GAMES`] and `load scene=<key>` starts it in the tools, the window\n6\t//! (`shardfall --scene <key>`) and the browser (`?scene=<key>`). `starter.rs` is the template\n7\t//! to copy; `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` is the guide. Shardfall (`crate::arpg`) predates this hook and\n8\t//! keeps its own.\n9\t\n10\tuse std::any::Any;\n11\tuse std::collections::BTreeMap;\n12\t\n13\tuse anyhow::Result;\n14\tuse serde::de::{DeserializeOwned, Error as _};\n15\tuse serde::{Deserialize, Deserializer, Serialize, Serializer};\n16\t\n17\tuse crate::entity::EntityId;\n18\tuse crate::frame::SimEvent;\n19\tuse crate::input::InputFrame;\n20\tuse crate::sim::Sim;\n21\t\n22\tpub mod starter;\n23\t\n24\t/// Every game the engine knows. `load scene=<key>` (or `<key>/<rest>`) builds it.\n25\tpub static GAMES: &[Entry] =\n26\t    &[entry::<starter::Starter>(\"Starter: collect every shard while slimes chase you (the template to copy).\", starter::build)];\n27\t\n28\t/// Still playing, won or lost.\n29\t#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Default, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n30\t#[serde(rename_all = \"snake_case\")]\n31\tpub enum Outcome {\n32\t    #[default]\n33\t    Playing,\n34\t    Won,\n35\t    Lost,\n36\t}\n37\t\n38\t/// A game's rules. Every method has a default, so a game implements only what it uses.\n39\t///\n40\t/// The engine calls `pre` and `post` once per tick (60 a second) from `Sim::step`; everything\n41\t/// else is read-only. While `pre` or `post` runs, the game is out of `sim.state.play`, so use\n42\t/// `self` for the game's own state and `sim` for the world.\n43\tpub trait Game: Clone + Serialize + DeserializeOwned + Send + Sync + 'static {\n44\t    /// The scene key that starts the game: `load scene=<KEY>`, `--scene <KEY>`, `?scene=<KEY>`.\n45\t    const KEY: &'static str;\n46\t\n47\t    /// Before characters move: read the player's input and decide what the characters the\n48\t    /// game drives do this tick. A returned input replaces that character's own (its `ai`\n49\t    /// brain, or the player's keys for the player).\n50\t    fn pre(\n51\t        &mut self,\n52\t        _sim: &mut Sim,\n53\t        _input: &InputFrame,\n54\t        _dt: f32,\n55\t        _events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>,\n56\t    ) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame> {\n57\t        BTreeMap::new()\n58\t    }\n59\t\n60\t    /// After physics: the rules (pickups, damage, score, win and loss). Push `SimEvent`s for\n61\t    /// sounds and effects (`Coin`, `Pickup`, `Hit`, `Explosion`...).\n62\t    fn post(&mut self, _sim: &mut Sim, _dt: f32, _events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) {}\n63\t\n64\t    /// HUD lines: drawn at the top left of the window, returned by the `play` tool.\n65\t    fn hud(&self, _sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String> {\n66\t        Vec::new()\n67\t    }\n68\t\n69\t    /// The game's state for tools and tests (`play`), as JSON.\n70\t    fn status(&self, _sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value {\n71\t        serde_json::Value::Null\n72\t    }\n73\t\n74\t    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n75\t        Outcome::Playing\n76\t    }\n77\t\n78\t    /// The input a good player would give this tick. `play bot=true` and the tests drive the\n79\t    /// player with it; teach it every new rule, and keep a test that it wins.\n80\t    fn bot(&self, _sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\n81\t        InputFrame::default()\n82\t    }\n83\t}\n84\t\n85\t/// One line of [`GAMES`]: made with [`entry`].\n86\tpub struct Entry {\n87\t    pub key: &'static str,\n88\t    pub about: &'static str,\n89\t    /// Builds the scene and sets `sim.state.play`. The `&str` is the rest of the scene name\n90\t    /// after `<key>/` (a level number, say), if any.\n91\t    pub build: fn(&mut Sim, Option<&str>) -> Result<()>,\n92\t    load: fn(ciborium::Value) -> Result<GameBox, String>,\n93\t}\n94\t\n95\t/// A [`GAMES`] line for the game type `G`.\n96\tpub const fn entry<G: Game>(about: &'static str, build: fn(&mut Sim, Option<&str>) -> Result<()>) -> Entry {\n97\t    Entry { key: G::KEY, about, build, load: load_as::<G> }\n98\t}\n99\t\n100\tfn load_as<G: Game>(v: ciborium::Value) -> Result<GameBox, String> {\n101\t    v.deserialized::<G>().map(GameBox::new).map_err(|e| e.to_string())\n102\t}\n103\t\n104\t/// A game's level text: `crates/pav_core/src/games/<key>.toml` read from disk when the program\n105\t/// runs from the repository's root (`pav`, `cargo run`), so an edit shows at the next `load`\n106\t/// with no rebuild; else `built_in`, the copy embedded with `include_str!` (tests, the browser,\n107\t/// a shipped executable).\n108\tpub fn level_text(key: &str, built_in: &'static str) -> std::borrow::Cow<'static, str> {\n109\t    #[cfg(not(target_arch = \"wasm32\"))]\n110\t    if let Ok(text) = std::fs::read_to_string(format!(\"crates/pav_core/src/games/{key}.toml\")) {\n111\t        return text.into();\n112\t    }\n113\t    #[cfg(target_arch = \"wasm32\")]\n114\t    let _ = key; // the browser has no files\n115\t    built_in.into()\n116\t}\n117\t\n118\t/// The game registered under `key`.\n119\tpub fn find(key: &str) -> Option<&'static Entry> {\n120\t    GAMES.iter().find(|g| g.key == key)\n121\t}\n122\t\n123\t/// A running game in `SimState::play`: clones (rewind, snapshots) and serializes (snapshot\n124\t/// files, the state hash) through the game's own derives.\n125\tpub struct GameBox(Box<dyn DynGame>);\n126\t\n127\timpl GameBox {\n128\t    pub fn new<G: Game>(game: G) -> Self {\n129\t        Self(Box::new(game))\n130\t    }\n131\t\n132\t    pub fn key(&self) -> &'static str {\n133\t        self.0.key()\n134\t    }\n135\t\n136\t    /// The game as its own type (None for another game).\n137\t    pub fn get<G: Game>(&self) -> Option<&G> {\n138\t        self.0.as_any().downcast_ref()\n139\t    }\n140\t\n141\t    pub fn get_mut<G: Game>(&mut self) -> Option<&mut G> {\n142\t        self.0.as_any_mut().downcast_mut()\n143\t    }\n144\t\n145\t    pub fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String> {\n146\t        self.0.hud(sim)\n147\t    }\n148\t\n149\t    pub fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value {\n150\t        self.0.status(sim)\n151\t    }\n152\t\n153\t    pub fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n154\t        self.0.outcome()\n155\t    }\n156\t\n157\t    pub fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\n158\t        self.0.bot(sim)\n159\t    }\n160\t}\n161\t\n162\timpl Clone for GameBox {\n163\t    fn clone(&self) -> Self {\n164\t        Self(self.0.clone_box())\n165\t    }\n166\t}\n167\t\n168\t#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]\n169\tstruct Saved {\n170\t    key: String,\n171\t    state: ciborium::Value,\n172\t}\n173\t\n174\timpl Serialize for GameBox {\n175\t    fn serialize<S: Serializer>(&self, s: S) -> Result<S::Ok, S::Error> {\n176\t        let state = self.0.save().map_err(serde::ser::Error::custom)?;\n177\t        Saved { key: self.key().to_string(), state }.serialize(s)\n178\t    }\n179\t}\n180\t\n181\timpl<'de> Deserialize<'de> for GameBox {\n182\t    fn deserialize<D: Deserializer<'de>>(d: D) -> Result<Self, D::Error> {\n183\t        let saved = Saved::deserialize(d)?;\n184\t        let Some(entry) = find(&saved.key) else {\n185\t            let known: Vec<&str> = GAMES.iter().map(|g| g.key).collect();\n186\t            return Err(D::Error::custom(format!(\"unknown game '{}' (known: {})\", saved.key, known.join(\", \"))));\n187\t        };\n188\t        (entry.load)(saved.state).map_err(D::Error::custom)\n189\t    }\n190\t}\n191\t\n192\t/// [`Game`] as an object (the trait itself isn't one: `Clone` and serde).\n193\ttrait DynGame: Send + Sync {\n194\t    fn key(&self) -> &'static str;\n195\t    fn clone_box(&self) -> Box<dyn DynGame>;\n196\t    fn save(&self) -> Result<ciborium::Value, String>;\n197\t    fn pre(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame>;\n198\t    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>);\n199\t    fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String>;\n200\t    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value;\n201\t    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome;\n202\t    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame;\n203\t    fn as_any(&self) -> &dyn Any;\n204\t    fn as_any_mut(&mut self) -> &mut dyn Any;\n205\t}\n206\t\n207\timpl<G: Game> DynGame for G {\n208\t    fn key(&self) -> &'static str {\n209\t        G::KEY\n210\t    }\n211\t    fn clone_box(&self) -> Box<dyn DynGame> {\n212\t        Box::new(self.clone())\n213\t    }\n214\t    fn save(&self) -> Result<ciborium::Value, String> {\n215\t        ciborium::Value::serialized(self).map_err(|e| e.to_string())\n216\t    }\n217\t    fn pre(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame> {\n218\t        Game::pre(self, sim, input, dt, events)\n219\t    }\n220\t    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) {\n221\t        Game::post(self, sim, dt, events)\n222\t    }\n223\t    fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String> {\n224\t        Game::hud(self, sim)\n225\t    }\n226\t    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value {\n227\t        Game::status(self, sim)\n228\t    }\n229\t    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n230\t        Game::outcome(self)\n231\t    }\n232\t    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\n233\t        Game::bot(self, sim)\n234\t    }\n235\t    fn as_any(&self) -> &dyn Any {\n236\t        self\n237\t    }\n238\t    fn as_any_mut(&mut self) -> &mut dyn Any {\n239\t        self\n240\t    }\n241\t}\n242\t\n243\timpl Sim {\n244\t    /// The running game from [`GAMES`], if this scene is one.\n245\t    pub fn play(&self) -> Option<&GameBox> {\n246\t        self.state.play.as_ref()\n247\t    }\n248\t\n249\t    /// The running game's outcome (`Playing` when there is none).\n250\t    pub fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n251\t        self.play().map(|g| g.outcome()).unwrap_or_default()\n252\t    }\n253\t\n254\t    /// The running game's bot input (none without a game).\n255\t    pub fn bot_input(&self) -> InputFrame {\n256\t        self.play().map(|g| g.bot(self)).unwrap_or_default()\n257\t    }\n258\t\n259\t    pub(crate) fn play_pre(&mut self, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame> {\n260\t        let Some(mut g) = self.state.play.take() else { return BTreeMap::new() };\n261\t        let out = g.0.pre(self, input, dt, events);\n262\t        self.state.play = Some(g);\n263\t        out\n264\t    }\n265\t\n266\t    pub(crate) fn play_post(&mut self, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) {\n267\t        let Some(mut g) = self.state.play.take() else { return };\n268\t        g.0.post(self, dt, events);\n269\t        self.state.play = Some(g);\n270\t    }\n271\t\n272\t    pub(crate) fn play_hud(&self) -> Vec<String> {\n273\t        self.play().map(|g| g.hud(self)).unwrap_or_default()\n274\t    }\n275\t\n276\t    /// Plays the running game with its bot until it is won or lost, or `ticks` pass.\n277\t    /// Returns the outcome and the ticks played.\n278\t    pub fn run_bot(&mut self, ticks: u64) -> (Outcome, u64) {\n279\t        for t in 0..ticks {\n280\t            let o = self.outcome();\n281\t            if o != Outcome::Playing {\n282\t                return (o, t);\n283\t            }\n284\t            let input = self.bot_input();\n285\t            self.step(&input);\n286\t        }\n287\t        (self.outcome(), ticks)\n288\t    }\n289\t}\n290\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"d67cafe3-edc9-405d-a9e5-214806cf1fc7","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:30.840Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/crates/pav_core/src/games/mod.rs","content":"//! Games built on the engine: the hook a new game implements, and the list of games.\n//!\n//! A game is a struct holding its rules' state (`Clone` and serde, like everything in\n//! `SimState`) that implements [`Game`], plus a `build` function that makes its scene. One\n//! line in [`GAMES`] and `load scene=<key>` starts it in the tools, the window\n//! (`shardfall --scene <key>`) and the browser (`?scene=<key>`). `starter.rs` is the template\n//! to copy; `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` is the guide. Shardfall (`crate::arpg`) predates this hook and\n//! keeps its own.\n\nuse std::any::Any;\nuse std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\nuse anyhow::Result;\nuse serde::de::{DeserializeOwned, Error as _};\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Deserializer, Serialize, Serializer};\n\nuse crate::entity::EntityId;\nuse crate::frame::SimEvent;\nuse crate::input::InputFrame;\nuse crate::sim::Sim;\n\npub mod starter;\n\n/// Every game the engine knows. `load scene=<key>` (or `<key>/<rest>`) builds it.\npub static GAMES: &[Entry] =\n    &[entry::<starter::Starter>(\"Starter: collect every shard while slimes chase you (the template to copy).\", starter::build)];\n\n/// Still playing, won or lost.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Default, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n#[serde(rename_all = \"snake_case\")]\npub enum Outcome {\n    #[default]\n    Playing,\n    Won,\n    Lost,\n}\n\n/// A game's rules. Every method has a default, so a game implements only what it uses.\n///\n/// The engine calls `pre` and `post` once per tick (60 a second) from `Sim::step`; everything\n/// else is read-only. While `pre` or `post` runs, the game is out of `sim.state.play`, so use\n/// `self` for the game's own state and `sim` for the world.\npub trait Game: Clone + Serialize + DeserializeOwned + Send + Sync + 'static {\n    /// The scene key that starts the game: `load scene=<KEY>`, `--scene <KEY>`, `?scene=<KEY>`.\n    const KEY: &'static str;\n\n    /// Before characters move: read the player's input and decide what the characters the\n    /// game drives do this tick. A returned input replaces that character's own (its `ai`\n    /// brain, or the player's keys for the player).\n    fn pre(\n        &mut self,\n        _sim: &mut Sim,\n        _input: &InputFrame,\n        _dt: f32,\n        _events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>,\n    ) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame> {\n        BTreeMap::new()\n    }\n\n    /// After physics: the rules (pickups, damage, score, win and loss). Push `SimEvent`s for\n    /// sounds and effects (`Coin`, `Pickup`, `Hit`, `Explosion`...).\n    fn post(&mut self, _sim: &mut Sim, _dt: f32, _events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) {}\n\n    /// HUD lines: drawn at the top left of the window, returned by the `play` tool.\n    fn hud(&self, _sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String> {\n        Vec::new()\n    }\n\n    /// The game's state for tools and tests (`play`), as JSON.\n    fn status(&self, _sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value {\n        serde_json::Value::Null\n    }\n\n    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n        Outcome::Playing\n    }\n\n    /// The input a good player would give this tick. `play bot=true` and the tests drive the\n    /// player with it; teach it every new rule, and keep a test that it wins.\n    fn bot(&self, _sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\n        InputFrame::default()\n    }\n}\n\n/// One line of [`GAMES`]: made with [`entry`].\npub struct Entry {\n    pub key: &'static str,\n    pub about: &'static str,\n    /// Builds the scene and sets `sim.state.play`. The `&str` is the rest of the scene name\n    /// after `<key>/` (a level number, say), if any.\n    pub build: fn(&mut Sim, Option<&str>) -> Result<()>,\n    load: fn(ciborium::Value) -> Result<GameBox, String>,\n}\n\n/// A [`GAMES`] line for the game type `G`.\npub const fn entry<G: Game>(about: &'static str, build: fn(&mut Sim, Option<&str>) -> Result<()>) -> Entry {\n    Entry { key: G::KEY, about, build, load: load_as::<G> }\n}\n\nfn load_as<G: Game>(v: ciborium::Value) -> Result<GameBox, String> {\n    v.deserialized::<G>().map(GameBox::new).map_err(|e| e.to_string())\n}\n\n/// A game's level text: `crates/pav_core/src/games/<key>.toml` read from disk when the program\n/// runs from the repository's root (`pav`, `cargo run`), so an edit shows at the next `load`\n/// with no rebuild; else `built_in`, the copy embedded with `include_str!` (tests, the browser,\n/// a shipped executable).\npub fn level_text(key: &str, built_in: &'static str) -> std::borrow::Cow<'static, str> {\n    #[cfg(not(target_arch = \"wasm32\"))]\n    if let Ok(text) = std::fs::read_to_string(format!(\"crates/pav_core/src/games/{key}.toml\")) {\n        return text.into();\n    }\n    #[cfg(target_arch = \"wasm32\")]\n    let _ = key; // the browser has no files\n    built_in.into()\n}\n\n/// The game registered under `key`.\npub fn find(key: &str) -> Option<&'static Entry> {\n    GAMES.iter().find(|g| g.key == key)\n}\n\n/// A running game in `SimState::play`: clones (rewind, snapshots) and serializes (snapshot\n/// files, the state hash) through the game's own derives.\npub struct GameBox(Box<dyn DynGame>);\n\nimpl GameBox {\n    pub fn new<G: Game>(game: G) -> Self {\n        Self(Box::new(game))\n    }\n\n    pub fn key(&self) -> &'static str {\n        self.0.key()\n    }\n\n    /// The game as its own type (None for another game).\n    pub fn get<G: Game>(&self) -> Option<&G> {\n        self.0.as_any().downcast_ref()\n    }\n\n    pub fn get_mut<G: Game>(&mut self) -> Option<&mut G> {\n        self.0.as_any_mut().downcast_mut()\n    }\n\n    pub fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String> {\n        self.0.hud(sim)\n    }\n\n    pub fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value {\n        self.0.status(sim)\n    }\n\n    pub fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n        self.0.outcome()\n    }\n\n    pub fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\n        self.0.bot(sim)\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Clone for GameBox {\n    fn clone(&self) -> Self {\n        Self(self.0.clone_box())\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]\nstruct Saved {\n    key: String,\n    state: ciborium::Value,\n}\n\nimpl Serialize for GameBox {\n    fn serialize<S: Serializer>(&self, s: S) -> Result<S::Ok, S::Error> {\n        let state = self.0.save().map_err(serde::ser::Error::custom)?;\n        Saved { key: self.key().to_string(), state }.serialize(s)\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<'de> Deserialize<'de> for GameBox {\n    fn deserialize<D: Deserializer<'de>>(d: D) -> Result<Self, D::Error> {\n        let saved = Saved::deserialize(d)?;\n        let Some(entry) = find(&saved.key) else {\n            let known: Vec<&str> = GAMES.iter().map(|g| g.key).collect();\n            return Err(D::Error::custom(format!(\"unknown game '{}' (known: {})\", saved.key, known.join(\", \"))));\n        };\n        (entry.load)(saved.state).map_err(D::Error::custom)\n    }\n}\n\n/// [`Game`] as an object (the trait itself isn't one: `Clone` and serde).\ntrait DynGame: Send + Sync {\n    fn key(&self) -> &'static str;\n    fn clone_box(&self) -> Box<dyn DynGame>;\n    fn save(&self) -> Result<ciborium::Value, String>;\n    fn pre(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame>;\n    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>);\n    fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String>;\n    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value;\n    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome;\n    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame;\n    fn as_any(&self) -> &dyn Any;\n    fn as_any_mut(&mut self) -> &mut dyn Any;\n}\n\nimpl<G: Game> DynGame for G {\n    fn key(&self) -> &'static str {\n        G::KEY\n    }\n    fn clone_box(&self) -> Box<dyn DynGame> {\n        Box::new(self.clone())\n    }\n    fn save(&self) -> Result<ciborium::Value, String> {\n        ciborium::Value::serialized(self).map_err(|e| e.to_string())\n    }\n    fn pre(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame> {\n        Game::pre(self, sim, input, dt, events)\n    }\n    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) {\n        Game::post(self, sim, dt, events)\n    }\n    fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String> {\n        Game::hud(self, sim)\n    }\n    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value {\n        Game::status(self, sim)\n    }\n    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n        Game::outcome(self)\n    }\n    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\n        Game::bot(self, sim)\n    }\n    fn as_any(&self) -> &dyn Any {\n        self\n    }\n    fn as_any_mut(&mut self) -> &mut dyn Any {\n        self\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Sim {\n    /// The running game from [`GAMES`], if this scene is one.\n    pub fn play(&self) -> Option<&GameBox> {\n        self.state.play.as_ref()\n    }\n\n    /// The running game's outcome (`Playing` when there is none).\n    pub fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n        self.play().map(|g| g.outcome()).unwrap_or_default()\n    }\n\n    /// The running game's bot input (none without a game).\n    pub fn bot_input(&self) -> InputFrame {\n        self.play().map(|g| g.bot(self)).unwrap_or_default()\n    }\n\n    pub(crate) fn play_pre(&mut self, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame> {\n        let Some(mut g) = self.state.play.take() else { return BTreeMap::new() };\n        let out = g.0.pre(self, input, dt, events);\n        self.state.play = Some(g);\n        out\n    }\n\n    pub(crate) fn play_post(&mut self, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) {\n        let Some(mut g) = self.state.play.take() else { return };\n        g.0.post(self, dt, events);\n        self.state.play = Some(g);\n    }\n\n    pub(crate) fn play_hud(&self) -> Vec<String> {\n        self.play().map(|g| g.hud(self)).unwrap_or_default()\n    }\n\n    /// Plays the running game with its bot until it is won or lost, or `ticks` pass.\n    /// Returns the outcome and the ticks played.\n    pub fn run_bot(&mut self, ticks: u64) -> (Outcome, u64) {\n        for t in 0..ticks {\n            let o = self.outcome();\n            if o != Outcome::Playing {\n                return (o, t);\n            }\n            let input = self.bot_input();\n            self.step(&input);\n        }\n        (self.outcome(), ticks)\n    }\n}\n","numLines":290,"startLine":1,"totalLines":290}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015n7UoVTxFvb4e4AhDPrRqa","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9GzcpFKqm1mR28uZLr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Tpg2B5qxSdpPKazE8kKggb","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.rs"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16843,"cache_read_input_tokens":47614,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16843},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"c22fdf3a-0333-4637-84d7-725d50bc89af","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:31.967Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9GzNvfDE9m8V8KHUXs","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Tpg2B5qxSdpPKazE8kKggb":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.rs"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tpg2B5qxSdpPKazE8kKggb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! The starter game, and the template for a new one: collect every shard while slimes chase\n2\t//! you round the walls. Three lives; a slime's touch sends everyone back to the start.\n3\t//!\n4\t//! To start a game of your own, copy this file and `starter.toml` to `<key>.rs` and\n5\t//! `<key>.toml`, change `KEY`, add its line to `GAMES` in `mod.rs`, then change the rules.\n6\t//! `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` walks through it.\n7\t\n8\tuse std::collections::BTreeMap;\n9\tuse std::sync::Arc;\n10\t\n11\tuse anyhow::{Result, anyhow};\n12\tuse glam::{Vec2, Vec3};\n13\tuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\n14\tuse serde_json::json;\n15\t\n16\tuse super::{Game, GameBox, Outcome};\n17\tuse crate::color::Color;\n18\tuse crate::entity::{Behavior, EntityId, Spawn};\n19\tuse crate::frame::SimEvent;\n20\tuse crate::input::InputFrame;\n21\tuse crate::nav::NavGrid;\n22\tuse crate::room::RoomDef;\n23\tuse crate::shape::{Shape, Visual};\n24\tuse crate::sim::Sim;\n25\t\n26\t/// The level, a room file, built in (the browser has no files). Running from the repository,\n27\t/// `level_text` reads the file on disk instead, so an edit shows at the next `load`.\n28\tconst LEVEL: &str = include_str!(\"starter.toml\");\n29\t\n30\t/// The rules' numbers: the level's `[game]` table, each with a default, so balancing is an\n31\t/// edit to the level and a `load` (no rebuild).\n32\t#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n33\t#[serde(default, deny_unknown_fields)]\n34\tpub struct Rules {\n35\t    pub lives: u32,\n36\t    /// Slime speed as a fraction of a walker's top speed (each slime gets ±15%, from the seed).\n37\t    pub slime_speed: f32,\n38\t    /// Slimes chase a player within this walking distance (m).\n39\t    pub slime_sight: f32,\n40\t    /// Feet closer than this (m) catch the player.\n41\t    pub catch: f32,\n42\t    /// Feet closer than this (m) to a shard pick it up.\n43\t    pub pick: f32,\n44\t    /// Seconds the player is safe at the start and after a catch.\n45\t    pub grace: f32,\n46\t}\n47\t\n48\timpl Default for Rules {\n49\t    fn default() -> Self {\n50\t        Self { lives: 3, slime_speed: 0.5, slime_sight: 9.0, catch: 0.8, pick: 0.9, grace: 2.0 }\n51\t    }\n52\t}\n53\t\n54\t#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n55\tpub struct Starter {\n56\t    pub rules: Rules,\n57\t    /// Shards still on the floor.\n58\t    pub shards: Vec<EntityId>,\n59\t    pub total: usize,\n60\t    pub slimes: Vec<EntityId>,\n61\t    /// Each slime's start (feet) and speed.\n62\t    pub homes: Vec<Vec3>,\n63\t    pub speeds: Vec<f32>,\n64\t    pub lives: u32,\n65\t    /// Seconds the player is still safe from slimes.\n66\t    pub grace: f32,\n67\t    pub time: f32,\n68\t    pub outcome: Outcome,\n69\t    /// The walkable grid. It follows from the level alone, so it isn't saved: rebuilt when\n70\t    /// missing (after a snapshot load).\n71\t    #[serde(skip)]\n72\t    nav: Option<Arc<NavGrid>>,\n73\t}\n74\t\n75\t/// Builds the level, places the shards on its `shard` markers and starts the game.\n76\tpub fn build(sim: &mut Sim, _rest: Option<&str>) -> Result<()> {\n77\t    let def = RoomDef::parse(&super::level_text(Starter::KEY, LEVEL)).map_err(|e| anyhow!(\"starter.toml: {e}\"))?;\n78\t    let rules: Rules = toml::Value::Table(def.game.clone()).try_into().map_err(|e| anyhow!(\"starter.toml [game]: {e}\"))?;\n79\t    crate::scenes::build_standalone_room(sim, Starter::KEY, def);\n80\t    let mut shards = Vec::new();\n81\t    for at in crate::scenes::markers(sim, \"shard\") {\n82\t        let mut look = Visual::new(Shape::Box { half: Vec3::splat(0.2) }, Color::hex(\"#7fe3ff\"));\n83\t        look.emissive = 0.8;\n84\t        let shard = Spawn::new(\"shard\", at + Vec3::Y * 0.7).visual(look).behavior(Behavior::Spin { speed: 2.5 });\n85\t        shards.push(sim.spawn(shard));\n86\t    }\n87\t    // Slimes come from the level's [[npc]] entries; the game drives them.\n88\t    let slimes: Vec<EntityId> = sim.state.entities.iter().filter(|e| e.name == \"slime\").map(|e| e.id).collect();\n89\t    let homes = slimes.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.feet(id)).collect();\n90\t    // Randomness comes from the sim's seeded generator, so a seed always plays the same.\n91\t    let speeds = slimes.iter().map(|_| rules.slime_speed * sim.state.rng.range(0.85, 1.15)).collect();\n92\t    let game = Starter {\n93\t        total: shards.len(),\n94\t        shards,\n95\t        slimes,\n96\t        homes,\n97\t        speeds,\n98\t        lives: rules.lives,\n99\t        grace: rules.grace,\n100\t        rules,\n101\t        time: 0.0,\n102\t        outcome: Outcome::Playing,\n103\t        nav: None,\n104\t    };\n105\t    sim.state.play = Some(GameBox::new(game));\n106\t    Ok(())\n107\t}\n108\t\n109\timpl Game for Starter {\n110\t    const KEY: &'static str = \"starter\";\n111\t\n112\t    fn pre(\n113\t        &mut self,\n114\t        sim: &mut Sim,\n115\t        _input: &InputFrame,\n116\t        _dt: f32,\n117\t        _events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>,\n118\t    ) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame> {\n119\t        let mut out = BTreeMap::new();\n120\t        if self.outcome != Outcome::Playing {\n121\t            // Decided: everyone stands still.\n122\t            for &id in sim.state.player.iter().chain(&self.slimes) {\n123\t                out.insert(id, InputFrame::default());\n124\t            }\n125\t            return out;\n126\t        }\n127\t        if self.nav.is_none() {\n128\t            self.nav = NavGrid::for_room(sim).map(Arc::new);\n129\t        }\n130\t        let Some(nav) = self.nav.clone() else { return out };\n131\t        // One flow field toward the player steers every slime round the walls.\n132\t        let player = sim.state.player.and_then(|id| sim.feet(id));\n133\t        let field = player.filter(|_| self.grace <= 0.0).and_then(|p| nav.flow(p, self.rules.slime_sight));\n134\t        for (&id, &speed) in self.slimes.iter().zip(&self.speeds) {\n135\t            let mut input = InputFrame::default();\n136\t            if let (Some(field), Some(at)) = (&field, sim.feet(id))\n137\t                && let Some(d) = field.dir(&nav, at)\n138\t            {\n139\t                input.move_dir = Vec2::new(d.x, d.z) * speed;\n140\t            }\n141\t            out.insert(id, input);\n142\t        }\n143\t        out\n144\t    }\n145\t\n146\t    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) {\n147\t        if self.outcome != Outcome::Playing {\n148\t            return;\n149\t        }\n150\t        self.time += dt;\n151\t        self.grace = (self.grace - dt).max(0.0);\n152\t        let Some(pid) = sim.state.player else { return };\n153\t        let Some(p) = sim.feet(pid) else { return };\n154\t\n155\t        // Shards: walk over one to pick it up.\n156\t        let near: Vec<(EntityId, Vec3)> = self\n157\t            .shards\n158\t            .iter()\n159\t            .filter_map(|&id| sim.state.entities.get(id).map(|e| (id, e.pos)))\n160\t            .filter(|(_, at)| flat(*at - p) < self.rules.pick)\n161\t            .collect();\n162\t        for (id, at) in near {\n163\t            self.shards.retain(|&s| s != id);\n164\t            sim.despawn(id);\n165\t            events.push(SimEvent::Coin { pos: at });\n166\t            let left = self.shards.len();\n167\t            sim.say(if left == 1 { \"1 shard to go\".to_string() } else { format!(\"{left} shards to go\") });\n168\t        }\n169\t        if self.shards.is_empty() {\n170\t            self.outcome = Outcome::Won;\n171\t            sim.say(format!(\"All shards in {:.1} s!\", self.time));\n172\t            return;\n173\t        }\n174\t\n175\t        // Slimes: a touch costs a life and sends everyone back to the start.\n176\t        let catch = self.rules.catch;\n177\t        let caught = self.grace <= 0.0 && self.slimes.iter().any(|&id| sim.feet(id).is_some_and(|s| flat(s - p) < catch));\n178\t        if caught {\n179\t            self.lives -= 1;\n180\t            events.push(SimEvent::Hit { pos: p + Vec3::Y, strength: 1.0, target: Some(pid) });\n181\t            if self.lives == 0 {\n182\t                self.outcome = Outcome::Lost;\n183\t                sim.say(\"Caught! Game over\");\n184\t                return;\n185\t            }\n186\t            sim.say(format!(\"Caught! {} lives left\", self.lives));\n187\t            let spawn = sim.state.spawn;\n188\t            sim.set_position(pid, spawn);\n189\t            for (&id, &home) in self.slimes.iter().zip(&self.homes) {\n190\t                sim.set_position(id, home);\n191\t            }\n192\t            self.grace = self.rules.grace;\n193\t        }\n194\t    }\n195\t\n196\t    fn hud(&self, _sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String> {\n197\t        let mut lines =\n198\t            vec![format!(\"Shards {}/{}\", self.total - self.shards.len(), self.total), format!(\"Lives {}\", self.lives)];\n199\t        match self.outcome {\n200\t            Outcome::Won => lines.push(format!(\"You win! {:.1} s\", self.time)),\n201\t            Outcome::Lost => lines.push(\"Game over\".into()),\n202\t            Outcome::Playing => {}\n203\t        }\n204\t        lines\n205\t    }\n206\t\n207\t    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value {\n208\t        let xz = |id: EntityId| sim.feet(id).map(|f| [round(f.x), round(f.z)]);\n209\t        json!({\n210\t            \"outcome\": self.outcome,\n211\t            \"time\": round(self.time),\n212\t            \"shards_left\": self.shards.len(),\n213\t            \"total\": self.total,\n214\t            \"lives\": self.lives,\n215\t            \"grace\": round(self.grace),\n216\t            \"player\": sim.state.player.and_then(xz),\n217\t            \"shards\": self.shards.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.state.entities.get(id).map(|e| [round(e.pos.x), round(e.pos.z)])).collect::<Vec<_>>(),\n218\t            \"slimes\": self.slimes.iter().filter_map(|&id| xz(id)).collect::<Vec<_>>(),\n219\t        })\n220\t    }\n221\t\n222\t    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n223\t        self.outcome\n224\t    }\n225\t\n226\t    /// Takes the shortest walk to a shard that keeps clear of slimes (cells near them count\n227\t    /// as walls); with every shard cut off, makes for the reachable spot farthest from them.\n228\t    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\n229\t        let mut input = InputFrame::default();\n230\t        let Some(p) = sim.state.player.and_then(|id| sim.feet(id)) else { return input };\n231\t        let Some(mut grid) = self.nav.as_deref().cloned().or_else(|| NavGrid::for_room(sim)) else { return input };\n232\t        let slimes: Vec<Vec3> = self.slimes.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.feet(id)).collect();\n233\t        if self.grace < 0.5 {\n234\t            for i in 0..grid.blocked.len() {\n235\t                if slimes.iter().any(|&s| flat(grid.center(i) - s) < 1.8) {\n236\t                    grid.blocked[i] = true;\n237\t                }\n238\t            }\n239\t        }\n240\t        let paths = self.shards.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.state.entities.get(id)).filter_map(|e| grid.path(p, e.pos));\n241\t        let path = paths.min_by(|a, b| length(p, a).total_cmp(&length(p, b))).or_else(|| {\n242\t            let clearance = |i: usize| slimes.iter().map(|&s| flat(grid.center(i) - s)).fold(f32::MAX, f32::min);\n243\t            let reach = grid.flow(p, 6.0)?;\n244\t            let safest = (0..grid.blocked.len())\n245\t                .filter(|&i| reach.dist[i].is_finite())\n246\t                .max_by(|&a, &b| clearance(a).total_cmp(&clearance(b)))?;\n247\t            grid.path(p, grid.center(safest))\n248\t        });\n249\t        input.move_dir = path.map(|path| flat_dir(path[0] - p)).unwrap_or_default();\n250\t        input\n251\t    }\n252\t}\n253\t\n254\tfn flat(v: Vec3) -> f32 {\n255\t    Vec2::new(v.x, v.z).length()\n256\t}\n257\t\n258\tfn flat_dir(v: Vec3) -> Vec2 {\n259\t    Vec2::new(v.x, v.z).normalize_or_zero()\n260\t}\n261\t\n262\t/// Walking length of a path from `p`.\n263\tfn length(p: Vec3, path: &[Vec3]) -> f32 {\n264\t    let (mut at, mut sum) = (p, 0.0);\n265\t    for &q in path {\n266\t        sum += flat(q - at);\n267\t        at = q;\n268\t    }\n269\t    sum\n270\t}\n271\t\n272\tfn round(x: f32) -> f64 {\n273\t    (x as f64 * 100.0).round() / 100.0\n274\t}\n275\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"6aa3bceb-66be-4a98-96dc-cdf363ef760e","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:32.071Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo/crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.rs","content":"//! The starter game, and the template for a new one: collect every shard while slimes chase\n//! you round the walls. Three lives; a slime's touch sends everyone back to the start.\n//!\n//! To start a game of your own, copy this file and `starter.toml` to `<key>.rs` and\n//! `<key>.toml`, change `KEY`, add its line to `GAMES` in `mod.rs`, then change the rules.\n//! `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` walks through it.\n\nuse std::collections::BTreeMap;\nuse std::sync::Arc;\n\nuse anyhow::{Result, anyhow};\nuse glam::{Vec2, Vec3};\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\nuse serde_json::json;\n\nuse super::{Game, GameBox, Outcome};\nuse crate::color::Color;\nuse crate::entity::{Behavior, EntityId, Spawn};\nuse crate::frame::SimEvent;\nuse crate::input::InputFrame;\nuse crate::nav::NavGrid;\nuse crate::room::RoomDef;\nuse crate::shape::{Shape, Visual};\nuse crate::sim::Sim;\n\n/// The level, a room file, built in (the browser has no files). Running from the repository,\n/// `level_text` reads the file on disk instead, so an edit shows at the next `load`.\nconst LEVEL: &str = include_str!(\"starter.toml\");\n\n/// The rules' numbers: the level's `[game]` table, each with a default, so balancing is an\n/// edit to the level and a `load` (no rebuild).\n#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n#[serde(default, deny_unknown_fields)]\npub struct Rules {\n    pub lives: u32,\n    /// Slime speed as a fraction of a walker's top speed (each slime gets ±15%, from the seed).\n    pub slime_speed: f32,\n    /// Slimes chase a player within this walking distance (m).\n    pub slime_sight: f32,\n    /// Feet closer than this (m) catch the player.\n    pub catch: f32,\n    /// Feet closer than this (m) to a shard pick it up.\n    pub pick: f32,\n    /// Seconds the player is safe at the start and after a catch.\n    pub grace: f32,\n}\n\nimpl Default for Rules {\n    fn default() -> Self {\n        Self { lives: 3, slime_speed: 0.5, slime_sight: 9.0, catch: 0.8, pick: 0.9, grace: 2.0 }\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct Starter {\n    pub rules: Rules,\n    /// Shards still on the floor.\n    pub shards: Vec<EntityId>,\n    pub total: usize,\n    pub slimes: Vec<EntityId>,\n    /// Each slime's start (feet) and speed.\n    pub homes: Vec<Vec3>,\n    pub speeds: Vec<f32>,\n    pub lives: u32,\n    /// Seconds the player is still safe from slimes.\n    pub grace: f32,\n    pub time: f32,\n    pub outcome: Outcome,\n    /// The walkable grid. It follows from the level alone, so it isn't saved: rebuilt when\n    /// missing (after a snapshot load).\n    #[serde(skip)]\n    nav: Option<Arc<NavGrid>>,\n}\n\n/// Builds the level, places the shards on its `shard` markers and starts the game.\npub fn build(sim: &mut Sim, _rest: Option<&str>) -> Result<()> {\n    let def = RoomDef::parse(&super::level_text(Starter::KEY, LEVEL)).map_err(|e| anyhow!(\"starter.toml: {e}\"))?;\n    let rules: Rules = toml::Value::Table(def.game.clone()).try_into().map_err(|e| anyhow!(\"starter.toml [game]: {e}\"))?;\n    crate::scenes::build_standalone_room(sim, Starter::KEY, def);\n    let mut shards = Vec::new();\n    for at in crate::scenes::markers(sim, \"shard\") {\n        let mut look = Visual::new(Shape::Box { half: Vec3::splat(0.2) }, Color::hex(\"#7fe3ff\"));\n        look.emissive = 0.8;\n        let shard = Spawn::new(\"shard\", at + Vec3::Y * 0.7).visual(look).behavior(Behavior::Spin { speed: 2.5 });\n        shards.push(sim.spawn(shard));\n    }\n    // Slimes come from the level's [[npc]] entries; the game drives them.\n    let slimes: Vec<EntityId> = sim.state.entities.iter().filter(|e| e.name == \"slime\").map(|e| e.id).collect();\n    let homes = slimes.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.feet(id)).collect();\n    // Randomness comes from the sim's seeded generator, so a seed always plays the same.\n    let speeds = slimes.iter().map(|_| rules.slime_speed * sim.state.rng.range(0.85, 1.15)).collect();\n    let game = Starter {\n        total: shards.len(),\n        shards,\n        slimes,\n        homes,\n        speeds,\n        lives: rules.lives,\n        grace: rules.grace,\n        rules,\n        time: 0.0,\n        outcome: Outcome::Playing,\n        nav: None,\n    };\n    sim.state.play = Some(GameBox::new(game));\n    Ok(())\n}\n\nimpl Game for Starter {\n    const KEY: &'static str = \"starter\";\n\n    fn pre(\n        &mut self,\n        sim: &mut Sim,\n        _input: &InputFrame,\n        _dt: f32,\n        _events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>,\n    ) -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame> {\n        let mut out = BTreeMap::new();\n        if self.outcome != Outcome::Playing {\n            // Decided: everyone stands still.\n            for &id in sim.state.player.iter().chain(&self.slimes) {\n                out.insert(id, InputFrame::default());\n            }\n            return out;\n        }\n        if self.nav.is_none() {\n            self.nav = NavGrid::for_room(sim).map(Arc::new);\n        }\n        let Some(nav) = self.nav.clone() else { return out };\n        // One flow field toward the player steers every slime round the walls.\n        let player = sim.state.player.and_then(|id| sim.feet(id));\n        let field = player.filter(|_| self.grace <= 0.0).and_then(|p| nav.flow(p, self.rules.slime_sight));\n        for (&id, &speed) in self.slimes.iter().zip(&self.speeds) {\n            let mut input = InputFrame::default();\n            if let (Some(field), Some(at)) = (&field, sim.feet(id))\n                && let Some(d) = field.dir(&nav, at)\n            {\n                input.move_dir = Vec2::new(d.x, d.z) * speed;\n            }\n            out.insert(id, input);\n        }\n        out\n    }\n\n    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>) {\n        if self.outcome != Outcome::Playing {\n            return;\n        }\n        self.time += dt;\n        self.grace = (self.grace - dt).max(0.0);\n        let Some(pid) = sim.state.player else { return };\n        let Some(p) = sim.feet(pid) else { return };\n\n        // Shards: walk over one to pick it up.\n        let near: Vec<(EntityId, Vec3)> = self\n            .shards\n            .iter()\n            .filter_map(|&id| sim.state.entities.get(id).map(|e| (id, e.pos)))\n            .filter(|(_, at)| flat(*at - p) < self.rules.pick)\n            .collect();\n        for (id, at) in near {\n            self.shards.retain(|&s| s != id);\n            sim.despawn(id);\n            events.push(SimEvent::Coin { pos: at });\n            let left = self.shards.len();\n            sim.say(if left == 1 { \"1 shard to go\".to_string() } else { format!(\"{left} shards to go\") });\n        }\n        if self.shards.is_empty() {\n            self.outcome = Outcome::Won;\n            sim.say(format!(\"All shards in {:.1} s!\", self.time));\n            return;\n        }\n\n        // Slimes: a touch costs a life and sends everyone back to the start.\n        let catch = self.rules.catch;\n        let caught = self.grace <= 0.0 && self.slimes.iter().any(|&id| sim.feet(id).is_some_and(|s| flat(s - p) < catch));\n        if caught {\n            self.lives -= 1;\n            events.push(SimEvent::Hit { pos: p + Vec3::Y, strength: 1.0, target: Some(pid) });\n            if self.lives == 0 {\n                self.outcome = Outcome::Lost;\n                sim.say(\"Caught! Game over\");\n                return;\n            }\n            sim.say(format!(\"Caught! {} lives left\", self.lives));\n            let spawn = sim.state.spawn;\n            sim.set_position(pid, spawn);\n            for (&id, &home) in self.slimes.iter().zip(&self.homes) {\n                sim.set_position(id, home);\n            }\n            self.grace = self.rules.grace;\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn hud(&self, _sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String> {\n        let mut lines =\n            vec![format!(\"Shards {}/{}\", self.total - self.shards.len(), self.total), format!(\"Lives {}\", self.lives)];\n        match self.outcome {\n            Outcome::Won => lines.push(format!(\"You win! {:.1} s\", self.time)),\n            Outcome::Lost => lines.push(\"Game over\".into()),\n            Outcome::Playing => {}\n        }\n        lines\n    }\n\n    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value {\n        let xz = |id: EntityId| sim.feet(id).map(|f| [round(f.x), round(f.z)]);\n        json!({\n            \"outcome\": self.outcome,\n            \"time\": round(self.time),\n            \"shards_left\": self.shards.len(),\n            \"total\": self.total,\n            \"lives\": self.lives,\n            \"grace\": round(self.grace),\n            \"player\": sim.state.player.and_then(xz),\n            \"shards\": self.shards.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.state.entities.get(id).map(|e| [round(e.pos.x), round(e.pos.z)])).collect::<Vec<_>>(),\n            \"slimes\": self.slimes.iter().filter_map(|&id| xz(id)).collect::<Vec<_>>(),\n        })\n    }\n\n    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome {\n        self.outcome\n    }\n\n    /// Takes the shortest walk to a shard that keeps clear of slimes (cells near them count\n    /// as walls); with every shard cut off, makes for the reachable spot farthest from them.\n    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame {\n        let mut input = InputFrame::default();\n        let Some(p) = sim.state.player.and_then(|id| sim.feet(id)) else { return input };\n        let Some(mut grid) = self.nav.as_deref().cloned().or_else(|| NavGrid::for_room(sim)) else { return input };\n        let slimes: Vec<Vec3> = self.slimes.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.feet(id)).collect();\n        if self.grace < 0.5 {\n            for i in 0..grid.blocked.len() {\n                if slimes.iter().any(|&s| flat(grid.center(i) - s) < 1.8) {\n                    grid.blocked[i] = true;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        let paths = self.shards.iter().filter_map(|&id| sim.state.entities.get(id)).filter_map(|e| grid.path(p, e.pos));\n        let path = paths.min_by(|a, b| length(p, a).total_cmp(&length(p, b))).or_else(|| {\n            let clearance = |i: usize| slimes.iter().map(|&s| flat(grid.center(i) - s)).fold(f32::MAX, f32::min);\n            let reach = grid.flow(p, 6.0)?;\n            let safest = (0..grid.blocked.len())\n                .filter(|&i| reach.dist[i].is_finite())\n                .max_by(|&a, &b| clearance(a).total_cmp(&clearance(b)))?;\n            grid.path(p, grid.center(safest))\n        });\n        input.move_dir = path.map(|path| flat_dir(path[0] - p)).unwrap_or_default();\n        input\n    }\n}\n\nfn flat(v: Vec3) -> f32 {\n    Vec2::new(v.x, v.z).length()\n}\n\nfn flat_dir(v: Vec3) -> Vec2 {\n    Vec2::new(v.x, v.z).normalize_or_zero()\n}\n\n/// Walking length of a path from `p`.\nfn length(p: Vec3, path: &[Vec3]) -> f32 {\n    let (mut at, mut sum) = (p, 0.0);\n    for &q in path {\n        sum += flat(q - at);\n        at = q;\n    }\n    sum\n}\n\nfn round(x: f32) -> f64 {\n    (x as f64 * 100.0).round() / 100.0\n}\n","numLines":275,"startLine":1,"totalLines":275}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Tpg2B5qxSdpPKazE8kKggb","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
