{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9V74MGephaiECYuyr2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UwnB3nHzWagToNLYUQzEMB","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 485,530p README.md; echo ...; sed -n 1,80p docs/multiplayer-plan.md; echo ...; sed -n 640,700p lib/hub/examples/arena.html; echo; sed -n 1,50p scripts/hub-check.mjs","description":"Read hub README's session section, multiplayer plan and check script"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17878,"cache_read_input_tokens":184414,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17878},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"2efea5dd-190a-4880-a7ce-0f3ee7e643b5","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:26:09.276Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9V6phhLda7x9VP4uzL","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01UwnB3nHzWagToNLYUQzEMB":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/ai-hub/repo && sed -n 485,530p README.md; echo ...; sed -n 1,80p docs/multiplayer-plan.md; echo ...; sed -n 640,700p lib/hub/examples/arena.html; echo; sed -n 1,50p scripts/hub-check.mjs","description":"Read hub README's session section, multiplayer plan and check script"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01UwnB3nHzWagToNLYUQzEMB":{"cwd":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/ai-hub/repo"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UwnB3nHzWagToNLYUQzEMB","type":"tool_result","content":"Games tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` plays as ghost, `co-op`\nand `versus` as a shared world.\n\nIn short: the `hub:session` metas in the head, the small `session.js` client\npasted in unmodified as the first script, a round reported with its stats and\nawards, and outside a session the game plays exactly as it always would. What\nthe hub does around a round (points, awards, formats, the deal, the\nscoreboard, what travels where) is in\n[`docs/session-hub.md`](docs/session-hub.md). The plan behind all of it, and\nwhy, is [`docs/multiplayer-plan.md`](docs/multiplayer-plan.md).\n\nA game that declares it shows a *Hub session* line in its details\n(`1–4 players · Parallel · about 4 min`), and the Catalogue finds them all\nwith `has:session`. Those fields never become rails. A parallel game is\nnot a multiplayer game on the wall: each player plays alone.\n\nGames that play in sessions include **Blaze Cup** (a shared world, in teams),\n**Arwingdings**, **Merry-Go-Kart 64**, **Brine Line 64** and **Urethane\nSwitchyard** (tagged race, so played as ghost), and **Grand Theft Motto III**\n(a shared world: the whole campaign, together).\n\nTo test a game against the protocol, open the harness, which loads 1–4 copies\nnext to each other on a simulated network:\n\n```\npnpm dev\nhttp://localhost:7417/lib/hub/harness.html?game=/games/mygame.html&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5\n```\n\n`node scripts/hub-check.mjs games/mygame.html` runs the automatic conformance\nchecks with Playwright: a parallel game at 1 and 2 players, a ghost game or a\nshared world at 2, 3 and 4 on a bad network (within the seats it declares),\neach with a player leaving, then the game on its own. `--record` writes the\nverdict into `data/overrides.json`, where a failing game gets `\"session\": \"0\"`\nand is never dealt. The three examples in `lib/hub/examples/` (**Starfall**,\nparallel; **Comet Dash**, ghost; **Star Arena**, a shared world) are the hub's\nreference games and pass it; the protocol page does not link them, so no agent\ncopies them.\n\n### Bringing the library to sessions\n\nTwo scripts keep thousands of games on the protocol without editing them by\nhand:\n\n```bash\n...\n# AI Hub multiplayer: the plan\n\nVersion 3 · Oct 29, 2026. Version 2 (Oct 28) was the owner's manifesto. This\nversion is that plan after an audit against the code, a census of all 1,831\nentries in the library, the cc-matrix repository and outside facts, and with\nthe first stage built. The protocol itself, the one thing a game builder\nneeds, is [`lib/hub/README.md`](../lib/hub/README.md), served at\n<https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md>. What the hub does\naround a round is [`session-hub.md`](session-hub.md).\n\n**Nov 2, 2026: three modes, and the protocol cut to the interface.** A game\ndeclares **parallel**, **ghost** (parallel, plus the other players'\ncharacters shown while they are in the same area of the game, never touching\na world; `Math.random` seeded as in parallel) or **shared world** (one world\nfor everyone, run by the host's copy). Older tags still play: `race` as ghost,\n`co-op` and `versus` as a shared world. The protocol page now holds only the\ninterface and the hub's facts: no controller standard, settings menu, probe,\nround-length advice or example values, since an agent takes any hint as the\nway to build a game. `hub:session-minutes` is optional; a game that gives no\nlength gets the longest round. The dated log below keeps the names of its\nday.\n\n## The short version\n\nEvery game on the hub should be playable with friends, at the level of\n\"together\" it can honestly support. The cheapest level, **parallel**,\nreaches almost the whole library: everyone plays their own copy of the same\ngame at the same time, from the same start, and the hub shows who is ahead.\n**Ghost** (the other players seen in your own world) and a **shared world**\n(everyone in one world, run by the host) build on it where a game suits\nthem.\n\nThe audit kept version 2's chassis and changed its armour:\n\n- **Kept**: the hub owns everything around a round; one network; friends\n  only, no talking in games; one point for every player beaten; parallel\n  mode as the new baseline, added to the library by a tool and a cheap model; the\n  host's copy runs every shared world, each copy owning its own player.\n- **Changed**: watching stays on state, as version 2 decided, but asks no\n  game for a new ability. A watcher of a shared world is a copy\n  with no player of its own, drawn from the traffic every copy already gets;\n  in parallel mode, the live scoreboard is the view. That cuts the hardest,\n  costliest per-game ability (snapshot, restore, mirror) out of the roadmap.\n  Dedicated servers: the games are made ready now, with one rule that the\n  check tests; the server itself is built when it is wanted.\n- **Cut**: new names for things the hub already does (a list of modes per\n  game, an end-type meta, `authority` and `role`), and every step that needed\n  cc-matrix to change. cc-matrix is handed the protocol's address and nothing\n  else.\n- **Added**: what the plan was missing to work on day one: checks that fit\n  each kind of game; progress carried on the free peer-to-peer links; a fair\n  start the client enforces (saved data hidden in a session); a client that\n  never needs re-pasting for most new features, plus a script that upgrades\n  every pasted copy; a retrofit runner with spend caps that puts back every\n  game it cannot wire; a deal that plays one build of each title and only\n  games every seat can run; and a database change so parallel rounds are\n  kept.\n- **Next, first**: *Again*. Most of the library is shooters and action games\n  with lives, so in a one-run round the first player out waits for minutes.\n  The hub should let them start a fresh attempt and count their best, before\n  the first retrofitted wave is dealt.\n\nClient 1.3.0, everything under *Now* below, is built and tested in this\nchange.\n\n---\n\n## Part 1. The principles\n\n1. **Friends, not strangers.** Sessions are for people who invited each other\n   with a link. No matchmaking, no public lobbies, no rankings against\n   strangers.\n2. **No talking in the game.** No chat, voice, emotes, pings or custom names.\n   Friends talk where they already do. The hub shows only the names it\n   generates.\n3. **Every game can be played together, at its honest level.** A game with a\n   score or a finish gets parallel; a game with a hero, ghost; a game with\n   online multiplayer of its own, a shared world. A game declares the one\n   level it plays best, and nothing fakes a level it can't support.\n4. **The hub owns everything around a round; the game owns only the round.**\n...\n}\nfunction newStar() {\n  game.star = { x: 40 + game.rng() * (W - 80), y: 40 + game.rng() * (H - 80), n: game.star.n + 1 };\n}\n\n/** Your own player, from your own stick. Every copy moves its own, every frame, host or not. */\nfunction move(p, s, dt) {\n  const speed = SPEED * (s.sprint ? 1.5 : 1);\n  p.x = Math.max(R, Math.min(W - R, p.x + s.x * speed * dt));\n  p.y = Math.max(R, Math.min(H - R, p.y + s.y * speed * dt));\n}\nconst touching = (p) => Math.hypot(p.x - game.star.x, p.y - game.star.y) < R + 12;\n\n// ---- solo: the game as it plays on its own\nfunction solo() {\n  game.players = [{ id: 0, name: 'You', color: '#f5b700', x: W / 2, y: H / 2, score: 0 }];\n  game.phase = 'title';\n  game.message = 'Star Arena — press any key or tap to play';\n}\nlet soloEnds = 0;\n\n// ---- hub mode. Every other player also has tx, ty: where it last said it is\n// (and on the host, at: when). It is drawn easing toward there; your own player\n// is drawn where you put it.\nlet hub = null;\nlet lastSent = 0;\nlet lastSnap = -Infinity;    // the newest snapshot taken, by its AIHub.time()\nlet claimed = -1;            // the star this copy claimed last: one claim a star\n\nasync function connect() {\n  const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [2, 4] });\n  if (!info) return solo();\n  hub = info;\n  game.rng = AIHub.rng('stars');\n  game.players = info.players.map((p) => ({ ...p, x: 0, y: 0, score: 0, gone: false, at: -Infinity }));\n  place(game.players);\n  newStar();                 // the first star comes from the seed: the same on every copy\n  // The hub's card collects everyone's ready: ready up as soon as the arena is built.\n  game.phase = 'waiting';\n  game.message = 'Get ready…';\n  AIHub.ready();\n  AIHub.on('start', () => { game.phase = 'playing'; unlockAudio(); });\n  AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => {\n    if (hub.isHost && data.t === 'me') heard(from, data);\n    if (hub.isHost && data.t === 'claim') take(from, data.n);\n    if (from === hub.host && data.t === 's') applySnapshot(data);\n    if (from === hub.host && data.t === 'pick' && data.id !== hub.me) beep(330);   // your own cheered at the claim\n  });\n  AIHub.on('leave', ({ id }) => {\n    const p = game.players.find((q) => q.id === id);\n    if (p) p.gone = true;\n    if (hub.isHost && game.phase === 'playing' && game.players.filter((q) => !q.gone).length < 2) finish();\n  });\n  AIHub.on('time-up', () => { if (hub.isHost) finish(); });\n  AIHub.on('end', ({ reason, standings }) => {\n    game.phase = 'over';\n    game.standings = standings;\n    game.message = reason === 'finished' ? 'Round over' : reason === 'sat-out' ? 'You sat this one out' : 'Round cancelled';\n  });\n}\nconst inside = (v, max) => Math.max(R, Math.min(max - R, Number(v) || 0));\n\n#!/usr/bin/env node\n/**\n * hub-check.mjs — does a game keep the AI Hub Session Protocol?\n *\n * Runs lib/hub/harness.html?auto=1 against each game, at the player counts its\n * hub:session-players allows: hello, ready under the hub's card with no key\n * press, the start, traffic, a player leaving, time-up and a valid result. Then\n * once more with the host leaving. Then the game on its own, outside a session:\n * it opens, Enter and Space are pressed, and it must draw its screen with no\n * errors, the session client inactive and Math.random left alone. Before any\n * game script runs, every frame in session mode gets alert, confirm, prompt,\n * window.open and socket constructors wrapped, so calling them is caught.\n * Needs Playwright, which is not a dependency (see shots.mjs).\n *\n * Parallel is played at 1 and 2 players: nothing is shared, so more copies\n * prove nothing more. Its checks expect Math.random seeded the same on every\n * copy, progress from every copy, and no game traffic.\n *\n * Ghost and shared world are played at 2, 3 and 4 players on a poor network\n * (150 ± 50 ms, 5% loss). Ghost is checked like parallel, except that every\n * copy sends its player. A shared world wants traffic both ways, and is\n * played once more at 2 players with its world hosted by one more copy that\n * has no player of its own, as a dedicated server would host it (README,\n * \"Shared world\"). That run is reported like any; --record does not hold it\n * against a game, since no server runs yet.\n *\n * --retrofit is for an existing game upgraded to parallel: it is played once,\n * at 2 players with one of them leaving. With --original <path>, errors the\n * original page already had are forgiven when it is opened on its own.\n * --draws-only does only that last part, for any page: the retrofit runs it on\n * a game before spending a model on it, since a page this browser cannot run\n * cannot be checked here. A WebGPU-only game without WebGPU is caught even\n * when its title screen shows: it asked for an adapter, got none, and drew\n * nothing with WebGL or a 2D canvas instead. --browser-args passes flags to\n * Chromium; on a machine without a GPU, SwiftShader gives such games a\n * software adapter:\n *   --browser-args \"--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader\"\n *\n * Every game's pasted client is also read from disk and must be exactly a\n * released one (scripts/lib/hub-client.mjs).\n *\n * --record writes the outcome into data/overrides.json, which is how the hub\n * keeps a failing game out of sessions: a game that fails gets \"session\": \"0\"\n * (it overrides the game's own hub:session meta, so Random Mode never deals\n * it) and a sessionNote naming what failed; a game that passes loses both.\n *\n * Usage:  node scripts/hub-check.mjs [game paths…] [--players 2,3,4] [--base URL] [--json] [--no-solo]\n *                                    [--retrofit [--original <path>]] [--draws-only] [--record] [--browser-args \"<flags>\"]\n *         (games default to the three examples in lib/hub/examples/)\n */","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"328adff4-3379-4aa9-a3f4-1599b7e9c5ad","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:26:09.313Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Games tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` plays as ghost, `co-op`\nand `versus` as a shared world.\n\nIn short: the `hub:session` metas in the head, the small `session.js` client\npasted in unmodified as the first script, a round reported with its stats and\nawards, and outside a session the game plays exactly as it always would. What\nthe hub does around a round (points, awards, formats, the deal, the\nscoreboard, what travels where) is in\n[`docs/session-hub.md`](docs/session-hub.md). The plan behind all of it, and\nwhy, is [`docs/multiplayer-plan.md`](docs/multiplayer-plan.md).\n\nA game that declares it shows a *Hub session* line in its details\n(`1–4 players · Parallel · about 4 min`), and the Catalogue finds them all\nwith `has:session`. Those fields never become rails. A parallel game is\nnot a multiplayer game on the wall: each player plays alone.\n\nGames that play in sessions include **Blaze Cup** (a shared world, in teams),\n**Arwingdings**, **Merry-Go-Kart 64**, **Brine Line 64** and **Urethane\nSwitchyard** (tagged race, so played as ghost), and **Grand Theft Motto III**\n(a shared world: the whole campaign, together).\n\nTo test a game against the protocol, open the harness, which loads 1–4 copies\nnext to each other on a simulated network:\n\n```\npnpm dev\nhttp://localhost:7417/lib/hub/harness.html?game=/games/mygame.html&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5\n```\n\n`node scripts/hub-check.mjs games/mygame.html` runs the automatic conformance\nchecks with Playwright: a parallel game at 1 and 2 players, a ghost game or a\nshared world at 2, 3 and 4 on a bad network (within the seats it declares),\neach with a player leaving, then the game on its own. `--record` writes the\nverdict into `data/overrides.json`, where a failing game gets `\"session\": \"0\"`\nand is never dealt. The three examples in `lib/hub/examples/` (**Starfall**,\nparallel; **Comet Dash**, ghost; **Star Arena**, a shared world) are the hub's\nreference games and pass it; the protocol page does not link them, so no agent\ncopies them.\n\n### Bringing the library to sessions\n\nTwo scripts keep thousands of games on the protocol without editing them by\nhand:\n\n```bash\n...\n# AI Hub multiplayer: the plan\n\nVersion 3 · Oct 29, 2026. Version 2 (Oct 28) was the owner's manifesto. This\nversion is that plan after an audit against the code, a census of all 1,831\nentries in the library, the cc-matrix repository and outside facts, and with\nthe first stage built. The protocol itself, the one thing a game builder\nneeds, is [`lib/hub/README.md`](../lib/hub/README.md), served at\n<https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md>. What the hub does\naround a round is [`session-hub.md`](session-hub.md).\n\n**Nov 2, 2026: three modes, and the protocol cut to the interface.** A game\ndeclares **parallel**, **ghost** (parallel, plus the other players'\ncharacters shown while they are in the same area of the game, never touching\na world; `Math.random` seeded as in parallel) or **shared world** (one world\nfor everyone, run by the host's copy). Older tags still play: `race` as ghost,\n`co-op` and `versus` as a shared world. The protocol page now holds only the\ninterface and the hub's facts: no controller standard, settings menu, probe,\nround-length advice or example values, since an agent takes any hint as the\nway to build a game. `hub:session-minutes` is optional; a game that gives no\nlength gets the longest round. The dated log below keeps the names of its\nday.\n\n## The short version\n\nEvery game on the hub should be playable with friends, at the level of\n\"together\" it can honestly support. The cheapest level, **parallel**,\nreaches almost the whole library: everyone plays their own copy of the same\ngame at the same time, from the same start, and the hub shows who is ahead.\n**Ghost** (the other players seen in your own world) and a **shared world**\n(everyone in one world, run by the host) build on it where a game suits\nthem.\n\nThe audit kept version 2's chassis and changed its armour:\n\n- **Kept**: the hub owns everything around a round; one network; friends\n  only, no talking in games; one point for every player beaten; parallel\n  mode as the new baseline, added to the library by a tool and a cheap model; the\n  host's copy runs every shared world, each copy owning its own player.\n- **Changed**: watching stays on state, as version 2 decided, but asks no\n  game for a new ability. A watcher of a shared world is a copy\n  with no player of its own, drawn from the traffic every copy already gets;\n  in parallel mode, the live scoreboard is the view. That cuts the hardest,\n  costliest per-game ability (snapshot, restore, mirror) out of the roadmap.\n  Dedicated servers: the games are made ready now, with one rule that the\n  check tests; the server itself is built when it is wanted.\n- **Cut**: new names for things the hub already does (a list of modes per\n  game, an end-type meta, `authority` and `role`), and every step that needed\n  cc-matrix to change. cc-matrix is handed the protocol's address and nothing\n  else.\n- **Added**: what the plan was missing to work on day one: checks that fit\n  each kind of game; progress carried on the free peer-to-peer links; a fair\n  start the client enforces (saved data hidden in a session); a client that\n  never needs re-pasting for most new features, plus a script that upgrades\n  every pasted copy; a retrofit runner with spend caps that puts back every\n  game it cannot wire; a deal that plays one build of each title and only\n  games every seat can run; and a database change so parallel rounds are\n  kept.\n- **Next, first**: *Again*. Most of the library is shooters and action games\n  with lives, so in a one-run round the first player out waits for minutes.\n  The hub should let them start a fresh attempt and count their best, before\n  the first retrofitted wave is dealt.\n\nClient 1.3.0, everything under *Now* below, is built and tested in this\nchange.\n\n---\n\n## Part 1. The principles\n\n1. **Friends, not strangers.** Sessions are for people who invited each other\n   with a link. No matchmaking, no public lobbies, no rankings against\n   strangers.\n2. **No talking in the game.** No chat, voice, emotes, pings or custom names.\n   Friends talk where they already do. The hub shows only the names it\n   generates.\n3. **Every game can be played together, at its honest level.** A game with a\n   score or a finish gets parallel; a game with a hero, ghost; a game with\n   online multiplayer of its own, a shared world. A game declares the one\n   level it plays best, and nothing fakes a level it can't support.\n4. **The hub owns everything around a round; the game owns only the round.**\n...\n}\nfunction newStar() {\n  game.star = { x: 40 + game.rng() * (W - 80), y: 40 + game.rng() * (H - 80), n: game.star.n + 1 };\n}\n\n/** Your own player, from your own stick. Every copy moves its own, every frame, host or not. */\nfunction move(p, s, dt) {\n  const speed = SPEED * (s.sprint ? 1.5 : 1);\n  p.x = Math.max(R, Math.min(W - R, p.x + s.x * speed * dt));\n  p.y = Math.max(R, Math.min(H - R, p.y + s.y * speed * dt));\n}\nconst touching = (p) => Math.hypot(p.x - game.star.x, p.y - game.star.y) < R + 12;\n\n// ---- solo: the game as it plays on its own\nfunction solo() {\n  game.players = [{ id: 0, name: 'You', color: '#f5b700', x: W / 2, y: H / 2, score: 0 }];\n  game.phase = 'title';\n  game.message = 'Star Arena — press any key or tap to play';\n}\nlet soloEnds = 0;\n\n// ---- hub mode. Every other player also has tx, ty: where it last said it is\n// (and on the host, at: when). It is drawn easing toward there; your own player\n// is drawn where you put it.\nlet hub = null;\nlet lastSent = 0;\nlet lastSnap = -Infinity;    // the newest snapshot taken, by its AIHub.time()\nlet claimed = -1;            // the star this copy claimed last: one claim a star\n\nasync function connect() {\n  const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [2, 4] });\n  if (!info) return solo();\n  hub = info;\n  game.rng = AIHub.rng('stars');\n  game.players = info.players.map((p) => ({ ...p, x: 0, y: 0, score: 0, gone: false, at: -Infinity }));\n  place(game.players);\n  newStar();                 // the first star comes from the seed: the same on every copy\n  // The hub's card collects everyone's ready: ready up as soon as the arena is built.\n  game.phase = 'waiting';\n  game.message = 'Get ready…';\n  AIHub.ready();\n  AIHub.on('start', () => { game.phase = 'playing'; unlockAudio(); });\n  AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => {\n    if (hub.isHost && data.t === 'me') heard(from, data);\n    if (hub.isHost && data.t === 'claim') take(from, data.n);\n    if (from === hub.host && data.t === 's') applySnapshot(data);\n    if (from === hub.host && data.t === 'pick' && data.id !== hub.me) beep(330);   // your own cheered at the claim\n  });\n  AIHub.on('leave', ({ id }) => {\n    const p = game.players.find((q) => q.id === id);\n    if (p) p.gone = true;\n    if (hub.isHost && game.phase === 'playing' && game.players.filter((q) => !q.gone).length < 2) finish();\n  });\n  AIHub.on('time-up', () => { if (hub.isHost) finish(); });\n  AIHub.on('end', ({ reason, standings }) => {\n    game.phase = 'over';\n    game.standings = standings;\n    game.message = reason === 'finished' ? 'Round over' : reason === 'sat-out' ? 'You sat this one out' : 'Round cancelled';\n  });\n}\nconst inside = (v, max) => Math.max(R, Math.min(max - R, Number(v) || 0));\n\n#!/usr/bin/env node\n/**\n * hub-check.mjs — does a game keep the AI Hub Session Protocol?\n *\n * Runs lib/hub/harness.html?auto=1 against each game, at the player counts its\n * hub:session-players allows: hello, ready under the hub's card with no key\n * press, the start, traffic, a player leaving, time-up and a valid result. Then\n * once more with the host leaving. Then the game on its own, outside a session:\n * it opens, Enter and Space are pressed, and it must draw its screen with no\n * errors, the session client inactive and Math.random left alone. Before any\n * game script runs, every frame in session mode gets alert, confirm, prompt,\n * window.open and socket constructors wrapped, so calling them is caught.\n * Needs Playwright, which is not a dependency (see shots.mjs).\n *\n * Parallel is played at 1 and 2 players: nothing is shared, so more copies\n * prove nothing more. Its checks expect Math.random seeded the same on every\n * copy, progress from every copy, and no game traffic.\n *\n * Ghost and shared world are played at 2, 3 and 4 players on a poor network\n * (150 ± 50 ms, 5% loss). Ghost is checked like parallel, except that every\n * copy sends its player. A shared world wants traffic both ways, and is\n * played once more at 2 players with its world hosted by one more copy that\n * has no player of its own, as a dedicated server would host it (README,\n * \"Shared world\"). That run is reported like any; --record does not hold it\n * against a game, since no server runs yet.\n *\n * --retrofit is for an existing game upgraded to parallel: it is played once,\n * at 2 players with one of them leaving. With --original <path>, errors the\n * original page already had are forgiven when it is opened on its own.\n * --draws-only does only that last part, for any page: the retrofit runs it on\n * a game before spending a model on it, since a page this browser cannot run\n * cannot be checked here. A WebGPU-only game without WebGPU is caught even\n * when its title screen shows: it asked for an adapter, got none, and drew\n * nothing with WebGL or a 2D canvas instead. --browser-args passes flags to\n * Chromium; on a machine without a GPU, SwiftShader gives such games a\n * software adapter:\n *   --browser-args \"--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader\"\n *\n * Every game's pasted client is also read from disk and must be exactly a\n * released one (scripts/lib/hub-client.mjs).\n *\n * --record writes the outcome into data/overrides.json, which is how the hub\n * keeps a failing game out of sessions: a game that fails gets \"session\": \"0\"\n * (it overrides the game's own hub:session meta, so Random Mode never deals\n * it) and a sessionNote naming what failed; a game that passes loses both.\n *\n * Usage:  node scripts/hub-check.mjs [game paths…] [--players 2,3,4] [--base URL] [--json] [--no-solo]\n *                                    [--retrofit [--original <path>]] [--draws-only] [--record] [--browser-args \"<flags>\"]\n *         (games default to the three examples in lib/hub/examples/)\n */","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01UwnB3nHzWagToNLYUQzEMB","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9V74MGephaiECYuyr2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JnbabxZ6GfAr6RXcvTAzUt","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/ai-hub/repo/lib/hub && sed -n 1,120p harness.html; echo ...; grep -n \"20_000\\|20000\\|readyMs\\|READY\\|limit\\|graceMs\\|cardMs\\|timeout\\|setTimeout\" harness.html round.js | head -60","description":"Read the harness and its timing limits"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17878,"cache_read_input_tokens":184414,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17878},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"b2152c65-5256-4ccd-acf3-866516373f02","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:26:11.572Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9V6phhLda7x9VP4uzL","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01JnbabxZ6GfAr6RXcvTAzUt":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/ai-hub/repo/lib/hub && sed -n 1,120p harness.html; echo ...; grep -n \"20_000\\|20000\\|readyMs\\|READY\\|limit\\|graceMs\\|cardMs\\|timeout\\|setTimeout\" harness.html round.js | head -60","description":"Read the harness and its timing limits"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JnbabxZ6GfAr6RXcvTAzUt","type":"tool_result","content":"<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title>AI Hub Session Harness</title>\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Test a game against the AI Hub Session Protocol: 1–4 copies next to each other on a simulated network.\">\n<style>\n  :root { color-scheme: dark; --bg: #0e0c14; --panel: #191623; --line: #2c2838; --ink: #ece8f4; --dim: #9a93ab; --ok: #4ade80; --bad: #f87171; --warn: #fbbf24; }\n  * { box-sizing: border-box; }\n  html, body { margin: 0; height: 100%; background: var(--bg); color: var(--ink); font: 14px/1.4 system-ui, sans-serif; }\n  body { display: grid; grid-template-rows: auto 1fr; }\n  header { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px 14px; align-items: center; padding: 8px 12px; background: var(--panel); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }\n  header h1 { font-size: 15px; margin: 0 8px 0 0; }\n  header label { color: var(--dim); display: inline-flex; gap: 6px; align-items: center; }\n  input, select, button { font: inherit; color: var(--ink); background: #241f31; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 6px; padding: 4px 8px; }\n  input[type=number] { width: 70px; }\n  input.game { width: 300px; }\n  button { cursor: pointer; }\n  button:hover { border-color: var(--dim); }\n  main { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 340px; min-height: 0; }\n  #grid { display: grid; gap: 6px; padding: 6px; min-height: 0; }\n  .cell { position: relative; display: grid; grid-template-rows: auto 1fr; min-height: 0; border: 2px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; overflow: hidden; background: #000; }\n  .cell__bar { display: flex; gap: 8px; align-items: center; padding: 3px 8px; background: var(--panel); font-size: 12px; }\n  .cell__dot { width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 50%; }\n  .cell__state { margin-left: auto; color: var(--dim); }\n  .cell iframe { width: 100%; height: 100%; border: 0; background: #000; }\n  .cell.is-out { opacity: .35; }\n  aside { display: grid; grid-template-rows: auto auto 1fr; min-height: 0; border-left: 1px solid var(--line); background: var(--panel); }\n  aside section { padding: 8px 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }\n  aside h2 { font-size: 12px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em; color: var(--dim); margin: 0 0 6px; }\n  #checks li { list-style: none; }\n  #checks ul { margin: 0; padding: 0; }\n  .ok::before { content: '✓ '; color: var(--ok); }\n  .bad::before { content: '✗ '; color: var(--bad); }\n  .wait::before { content: '… '; color: var(--warn); }\n  #log { overflow: auto; font: 12px/1.35 ui-monospace, monospace; padding: 8px 12px; margin: 0; white-space: pre-wrap; }\n  #round { white-space: pre-wrap; font-size: 13px; }\n  .hint { color: var(--dim); }\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<header>\n  <h1>AI Hub session harness</h1>\n  <label>Game <input class=\"game\" id=\"game\" placeholder=\"mygame.html or /games/mygame/index.html\"></label>\n  <label>Players <select id=\"players\"><option>1</option><option selected>2</option><option>3</option><option>4</option></select></label>\n  <label>Latency <input type=\"number\" id=\"latency\" min=\"0\" value=\"60\"> ms</label>\n  <label>Jitter <input type=\"number\" id=\"jitter\" min=\"0\" value=\"20\"> ms</label>\n  <label>Loss <input type=\"number\" id=\"loss\" min=\"0\" max=\"50\" value=\"0\"> %</label>\n  <button id=\"restart\" type=\"button\">New round</button>\n  <button id=\"release\" type=\"button\" title=\"End the splash now: the round starts once every game has loaded\">Start now</button>\n  <button id=\"timeup\" type=\"button\">Time up</button>\n  <label>Remove <select id=\"who\"></select></label>\n  <button id=\"remove\" type=\"button\">Remove player</button>\n  <button id=\"hide\" type=\"button\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Hide host tab</button>\n</header>\n<main>\n  <div id=\"grid\"><p class=\"hint\" style=\"padding:16px\">Name a game above (a path or URL served alongside this page), then press New round. Click a player's frame to control that player.</p></div>\n  <aside>\n    <section><h2>Round</h2><div id=\"round\" class=\"hint\">No round yet.</div></section>\n    <section id=\"checks\"><h2>Conformance</h2><ul id=\"checklist\"><li class=\"hint\">Run with <code>?auto=1</code> for the automatic checks.</li></ul></section>\n    <pre id=\"log\" aria-live=\"off\"></pre>\n  </aside>\n</main>\n<script type=\"module\">\nimport { createBridge, attachFrame, postTo, createLocalNetwork } from './bridge.js';\nimport { createRound, limitFor, modeOf, seedsRandom, SELF_REPORTED, TIMINGS } from './round.js';\n\nconst NAMES = ['Red Fox', 'Blue Otter', 'Green Heron', 'Gold Lynx'];\nconst params = new URLSearchParams(location.search);\nconst $ = (id) => document.getElementById(id);\nconst auto = params.get('auto') === '1';\nconst scenario = params.get('scenario') || 'standard';\n/** ?scenario=server: the shared world runs on one more copy with no player of its own, as a dedicated server would run it. */\nconst serverMode = scenario === 'server';\nconst playMs = Number(params.get('play')) || 6000;\nconst SPLASH_MS = 10_000;\n\nfor (const [id, key] of [['game', 'game'], ['players', 'players'], ['latency', 'latency'], ['jitter', 'jitter'], ['loss', 'loss']]) {\n  if (params.has(key)) $(id).value = params.get(key);\n}\n\nconst net = createLocalNetwork({});\nlet current = null;\nlet roundNo = 0;\nconst logLines = [];\nfunction log(text) {\n  const t = (performance.now() / 1000).toFixed(2).padStart(7);\n  logLines.push(`${t}  ${text}`);\n  if (logLines.length > 400) logLines.splice(0, logLines.length - 400);\n  $('log').textContent = logLines.join('\\n');\n  $('log').scrollTop = $('log').scrollHeight;\n}\n\n/** The game's head metas, when the page can be fetched from here. */\nasync function metasOf(url) {\n  try {\n    const html = await (await fetch(url)).text();\n    const doc = new DOMParser().parseFromString(html, 'text/html');\n    const meta = (name) => doc.querySelector(`meta[name=\"${name}\"]`)?.getAttribute('content')?.trim() ?? null;\n    return {\n      session: meta('hub:session'),\n      players: meta('hub:session-players'),\n      mode: meta('hub:session-mode'),\n      minutes: meta('hub:session-minutes'),\n    };\n  } catch {\n    return null;\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * For a game tagged parallel or ghost, the seed rides in the address: the client seeds Math.random with it before the game's scripts run.\n * Only a page of this site is loaded: ?game= comes from the address bar, and a javascript: or another site's\n * address in an unsandboxed frame would run as this origin, where a signed-in player's session is kept.\n */\nconst gameUrl = (game, { tag, seed } = {}) => {\n  let url;\n  try {\n    url = new URL(game, location.href);\n...\nharness.html:67:import { createRound, limitFor, modeOf, seedsRandom, SELF_REPORTED, TIMINGS } from './round.js';\nharness.html:170:  const limitMs = params.has('limit') ? Number(params.get('limit')) * 1000 : limitFor(metas?.minutes);\nharness.html:181:  const state = { round, n, metas, mode, limitMs, seed, players, cells: [], hubEnd, status: null, record: null, hidden: false, splashUntil: performance.now() + SPLASH_MS, answers: { ready: new Set(), more: new Set() } };\nharness.html:183:  log(`round ${round}: ${n} player${n > 1 ? 's' : ''}, seed ${seed}, limit ${Math.round(limitMs / 1000)} s, mode ${mode || '(from hello)'}`);\nharness.html:192:    limitMs,\nharness.html:383:const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));\nharness.html:400:  setTimeout(() => target.dispatchEvent(new win.KeyboardEvent('keyup', init)), 120);\nharness.html:441:    const loaded = await until(() => copies().every((p) => p.ready), 20_000);\nharness.html:497:    const ended = await until(() => state.record, TIMINGS.graceMs + 2000);\nround.js:8: * injected, so `scripts/hub.test.mjs` can walk a round through every timeout\nround.js:17:  readyMs: 45_000,\nround.js:19:  loadGraceMs: 20_000,\nround.js:21:  graceMs: 5_000,\nround.js:22:  hiddenMs: 20_000,\nround.js:56:  setTimeout: (fn, ms) => setTimeout(fn, ms),\nround.js:61:export function limitFor(minutes, { maxRoundMs = TIMINGS.maxRoundMs } = {}) {\nround.js:98:/** The protocol's limits on a round's highlights (README §10). */\nround.js:171: * @param {number} o.limitMs\nround.js:172: * @param {{now:()=>number, setTimeout:Function, clearTimeout:Function}} [o.clock]\nround.js:185:  limitMs,\nround.js:231:    const handle = clock.setTimeout(() => {\nround.js:294:      limitMs,\nround.js:335:    later(t.countdownMs + limitMs, timeUp);\nround.js:342:    toPresent({ type: 'time-up', graceMs: t.graceMs });\nround.js:343:    later(t.graceMs, () => {\nround.js:486:      later(t.readyMs, sitOut((s) => !s.ready));","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"92a6d8a6-fcb4-4181-ab72-741281b87c26","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:26:11.608Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title>AI Hub Session Harness</title>\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Test a game against the AI Hub Session Protocol: 1–4 copies next to each other on a simulated network.\">\n<style>\n  :root { color-scheme: dark; --bg: #0e0c14; --panel: #191623; --line: #2c2838; --ink: #ece8f4; --dim: #9a93ab; --ok: #4ade80; --bad: #f87171; --warn: #fbbf24; }\n  * { box-sizing: border-box; }\n  html, body { margin: 0; height: 100%; background: var(--bg); color: var(--ink); font: 14px/1.4 system-ui, sans-serif; }\n  body { display: grid; grid-template-rows: auto 1fr; }\n  header { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px 14px; align-items: center; padding: 8px 12px; background: var(--panel); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }\n  header h1 { font-size: 15