{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U6ethnAeuGG2RcMCSMfvTX","type":"tool_result","content":"1002-/// `dress` turns the result into parts.\n1003-#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq)]\n1004:pub struct Skel {\n1005-    pub pelvis: Vec3,\n1006-    /// Centre of the shoulder line.\n1007-    pub chest: Vec3,\n1008-    pub neck: Vec3,\n1009-    pub head: Vec3,\n1010-    /// How the pelvis, chest and head are turned (character space): belts and hips, the back\n1011-    /// (capes, wings, pauldrons), the face (eyes, helmets).\n1012-    pub pelvis_rot: Quat,\n1013-    pub chest_rot: Quat,\n1014-    pub head_rot: Quat,\n1015-    /// The top of the head points this way (helmets and hats sit along it).\n1016-    pub crown: Vec3,\n1017-    /// `[left, right]`.\n1018-    pub shoulder: [Vec3; 2],\n1019-    pub elbow: [Vec3; 2],\n1020-    pub hand: [Vec3; 2],\n1021-    pub hip: [Vec3; 2],\n1022-    pub knee: [Vec3; 2],\n1023-    pub ankle: [Vec3; 2],\n1024-    /// Which way each shoe points (unit).\n1025-    pub toe: [Vec3; 2],\n1026-    /// Which way the held weapon points from the right hand (unit).\n1027-    pub weapon: Vec3,\n1028-    /// Eyes shut (blinks): 0..1.\n1029-    pub blink: f32,\n1030-}\n1031-\n1032-impl Skel {\n1033-    /// Every point turned by `q` about the feet and moved by `lift`; every direction turned by `q`\n1034-    /// (whole-body turns and hops).\n1035-    pub fn transform(&mut self, q: Quat, lift: Vec3) {\n1036-        let p = |v: &mut Vec3| *v = q * *v + lift;\n1037-        for v in [&mut self.pelvis, &mut self.chest, &mut self.neck, &mut self.head] {\n1038-            p(v);\n1039-        }\n1040-        for i in 0..2 {\n1041-            for v in [\n1042-                &mut self.shoulder[i],\n1043-                &mut self.elbow[i],\n1044-                &mut self.hand[i],\n15:    /// What a character holds (Shardfall weapons).\n20:        Sword => \"sword\",\n26:        Greatsword => \"greatsword\",\n34:    /// What the off hand holds.\n44:/// A held weapon's look: drawn from the hand along the arm, swinging with attacks.\n52:    /// Glow of the blade (enchanted and unique weapons).\n58:    /// A library prop drawn in place of the weapon (`LIB/core/mage_staff`), held by its `grip`\n59:    /// socket with the socket's +y along the weapon; `kind` still sets the reach and the swings.\n154:pub struct PuppetDef {\n169:    pub look: Look,\n199:    /// Held weapon and off hand.\n200:    pub weapon: WeaponLook,\n234:    /// `crate::figure`): rigid per-bone segments that follow the biped's pose at the figure's\n319:            weapon: WeaponLook::default(),\n342:    pub fn preset(plan: BodyPlan) -> Self {\n467:pub struct PuppetState {\n556:    /// The clip before, held under the new one while it fades in.\n570:/// What the character is doing this tick (input to the animator).\n693:    /// `hit`, alternate swings on `side < 0`. An action that begins while another still holds\n758:            // Under itself: what plays now stays beneath while the new start fades in.\n890:/// `pose`, plus the held weapon's span (hand, tip) in world space when there is one (for swing\n892:pub fn pose_ex(\n950:    // The sink comes after either (a body that lies where it fell, the hero's, stays at half).\n957:/// look (the side view drawn flat on a card that faces the camera, mirrored by facing).\n1015:    /// The top of the head points this way (helmets and hats sit along it).\n1026:    /// Which way the held weapon points from the right hand (unit).\n1027:    pub weapon: Vec3,\n1057:        self.weapon = q * self.weapon;\n1061:/// `st` held on its animation frame when the look animates in steps (`anim_fps`).\n1162:        weapon: Vec3::new(0.12, -0.35, 1.0).normalize(),\n1206:    // Arms (the right hand holds the weapon). A move twists the shoulders and steers the hands\n1208:    let fists = def.weapon.kind == WeaponKind::None;\n1251:    // The weapon points forward and down, or along the arc while a move swings it.\n1255:            let rest = sk.weapon;\n1256:            sk.weapon = rest.lerp(rest.lerp(m.weapon_dir(), m.wind_k), m.w).normalize_or(rest);\n1266:/// Parts for a skeleton: body, head, gear, limbs, weapon and eyes, squashed and stretched about\n1268:fn dress(def: &PuppetDef, st: &PuppetState, sk: &Skel, feet: Vec3, cam_fwd: Vec3) -> (Vec<PuppetPart>, Option<(Vec3, Vec3)>) {\n1451:    let mut held_parts = Vec::new();\n1453:    if def.weapon.kind != WeaponKind::None || def.weapon.offhand != OffhandKind::None {\n1454:        let (hand, dir) = (sk.hand[1], sk.weapon);\n1455:        weapon_parts(&def.weapon, k, hand, dir, sk.hand[0], &mut held_parts);\n1456:        for p in held_parts.iter_mut() {\n1460:        if def.weapon.kind != WeaponKind::None {\n1461:            span = Some((local(hand), local(hand + dir * weapon_length(def.weapon.kind) * def.weapon.size.max(0.3) * k)));\n1480:    parts.extend(held_parts);\n1494:/// Reach of a weapon from the hand to its tip (before size and scale).\n1495:pub fn weapon_length(k: WeaponKind) -> f32 {\n1499:        WeaponKind::Greatsword => 1.35,\n1511:/// A held weapon (and off-hand) as parts in character space: `h` is the hand, `d` the\n1512:/// direction the weapon points.\n//! Motion clips: animation captured or keyed elsewhere (motion-capture databases, animation\n//! libraries) as readable key poses, played on the biped puppet.\n//!\n//! The format is the one my-3D2dge introduced for AI models, kept as it is so clips move freely\n//! between the two engines. A clip is a short list of key poses; each names what an animator\n//! would: where the hips are, how the body, chest and head turn and lean, how the shoulders reach\n//! and shrug, which way each arm and leg points and how much the elbow or knee bends, how the\n//! feet point. Nothing in it depends on body size (limbs are a direction plus a bend, heights are\n//! shares of hip height), so the puppet plays any clip at its own proportions: each limb takes the\n//! clip's direction at its own length and the elbows and knees are solved with the same IK the\n//! walk uses. Every set file carries the legend (`legend`) and its credits.\n//!\n//! Sets are the `*.json` files in /anim (embedded at build time; `crate::anim::reload` re-reads\n//! them). A clip is named `SET/Clip` (or just `Clip` when only one set has it); animation state\n//! stores its `ClipId`, a hash of that name.\n\nuse std::collections::{BTreeMap, HashMap};\nuse std::sync::{Arc, RwLock};\n\nuse glam::{Quat, Vec3};\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Deserializer, Serialize};\n\nuse crate::puppet::{PuppetDef, PuppetState, Skel};\n\n/// Play flags (`PuppetState::clip_flags`).\n/// Only the chest, head and arms follow the clip; the legs keep walking.\npub const UPPER: u8 = 1;\n/// Left and right swapped.\npub const MIRROR: u8 = 2;\n/// The clip's own travel moves the body (otherwise it plays in place).\npub const TRAVEL: u8 = 4;\n/// Fading out (set by `PuppetState::stop_clip`).\npub const STOP: u8 = 8;\n/// Fades out by itself when the clip ends (one-shot gestures over the procedural animation).\npub const ONCE: u8 = 16;\n/// Play flag: fade in and out three times as fast (an attack's wind-up is short).\npub const QUICK: u8 = 32;\n/// Hold the first and last poses instead of wrapping. The editor uses this to inspect a\n/// loop's final key without changing how that clip plays in the game.\npub const CLAMP: u8 = 128;\n\n/// Seconds a clip takes to fade in or out.\npub const FADE: f32 = 0.25;\n\n/// Performance settings (the `anim` group): how fast performing characters play their clips\n/// and moves, and whether their clips are mirrored.\n#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n#[serde(default)]\npub struct AnimParams {\n    pub tempo: f32,\n    pub mirror: bool,\n}\n\nimpl Default for AnimParams {\n    fn default() -> Self {\n        Self { tempo: 1.0, mirror: false }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl crate::params::Tunable for AnimParams {","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"304d8d69-0d7f-4c6d-8bd4-32be0f37d9b5","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:30:03.890Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"1002-/// `dress` turns the result into parts.\n1003-#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq)]\n1004:pub struct Skel {\n1005-    pub pelvis: Vec3,\n1006-    /// Centre of the shoulder line.\n1007-    pub chest: Vec3,\n1008-    pub neck: Vec3,\n1009-    pub head: Vec3,\n1010-    /// How the pelvis, chest and head are turned (character space): belts and hips, the back\n1011-    /// (capes, wings, pauldrons), the face (eyes, helmets).\n1012-    pub pelvis_rot: Quat,\n1013-    pub chest_rot: Quat,\n1014-    pub head_rot: Quat,\n1015-    /// The top of the head points this way (helmets and hats sit along it).\n1016-    pub crown: Vec3,\n1017-    /// `[left, right]`.\n1018-    pub shoulder: [Vec3; 2],\n1019-    pub elbow: [Vec3; 2],\n1020-    pub hand: [Vec3; 2],\n1021-    pub hip: [Vec3; 2],\n1022-    pub knee: [Vec3; 2],\n1023-    pub ankle: [Vec3; 2],\n1024-    /// Which way each shoe points (unit).\n1025-    pub toe: [Vec3; 2],\n1026-    /// Which way the held weapon points from the right hand (unit).\n1027-    pub weapon: Vec3,\n1028-    /// Eyes shut (blinks): 0..1.\n1029-    pub blink: f32,\n1030-}\n1031-\n1032-impl Skel {\n1033-    /// Every point turned by `q` about the feet and moved by `lift`; every direction turned by `q`\n1034-    /// (whole-body turns and hops).\n1035-    pub fn transform(&mut self, q: Quat, lift: Vec3) {\n1036-        let p = |v: &mut Vec3| *v = q * *v + lift;\n1037-        for v in [&mut self.pelvis, &mut self.chest, &mut self.neck, &mut self.head] {\n1038-            p(v);\n1039-        }\n1040-        for i in 0..2 {\n1041-            for v in [\n1042-                &mut self.shoulder[i],\n1043-                &mut self.elbow[i],\n1044-                &mut self.hand[i],\n15:    /// What a character holds (Shardfall weapons).\n20:        Sword => \"sword\",\n26:        Greatsword => \"greatsword\",\n34:    /// What the off hand holds.\n44:/// A held weapon's look: drawn from the hand along the arm, swinging with attacks.\n52:    /// Glow of the blade (enchanted and unique weapons).\n58:    /// A library prop drawn in place of the weapon (`LIB/core/mage_staff`), held by its `grip`\n59:    /// socket with the socket's +y along the weapon; `kind` still sets the reach and the swings.\n154:pub struct PuppetDef {\n169:    pub look: Look,\n199:    /// Held weapon and off hand.\n200:    pub weapon: WeaponLook,\n234:    /// `crate::figure`): rigid per-bone segments that follow the biped's pose at the figure's\n319:            weapon: WeaponLook::default(),\n342:    pub fn preset(plan: BodyPlan) -> Self {\n467:pub struct PuppetState {\n556:    /// The clip before, held under the new one while it fades in.\n570:/// What the character is doing this tick (input to the animator).\n693:    /// `hit`, alternate swings on `side < 0`. An action that begins while another still holds\n758:            // Under itself: what plays now stays beneath while the new start fades in.\n890:/// `pose`, plus the held weapon's span (hand, tip) in world space when there is one (for swing\n892:pub fn pose_ex(\n950:    // The sink comes after either (a body that lies where it fell, the hero's, stays at half).\n957:/// look (the side view drawn flat on a card that faces the camera, mirrored by facing).\n1015:    /// The top of the head points this way (helmets and hats sit along it).\n1026:    /// Which way the held weapon points from the right hand (unit).\n1027:    pub weapon: Vec3,\n1057:        self.weapon = q * self.weapon;\n1061:/// `st` held on its animation frame when the look animates in steps (`anim_fps`).\n1162:        weapon: Vec3::new(0.12, -0.35, 1.0).normalize(),\n1206:    // Arms (the right hand holds the weapon). A move twists the shoulders and steers the hands\n1208:    let fists = def.weapon.kind == WeaponKind::None;\n1251:    // The weapon points forward and down, or along the arc while a move swings it.\n1255:            let rest = sk.weapon;\n1256:            sk.weapon = rest.lerp(rest.lerp(m.weapon_dir(), m.wind_k), m.w).normalize_or(rest);\n1266:/// Parts for a skeleton: body, head, gear, limbs, weapon and eyes, squashed and stretched about\n1268:fn dress(def: &PuppetDef, st: &PuppetState, sk: &Skel, feet: Vec3, cam_fwd: Vec3) -> (Vec<PuppetPart>, Option<(Vec3, Vec3)>) {\n1451:    let mut held_parts = Vec::new();\n1453:    if def.weapon.kind != WeaponKind::None || def.weapon.offhand != OffhandKind::None {\n1454:        let (hand, dir) = (sk.hand[1], sk.weapon);\n1455:        weapon_parts(&def.weapon, k, hand, dir, sk.hand[0], &mut held_parts);\n1456:        for p in held_parts.iter_mut() {\n1460:        if def.weapon.kind != WeaponKind::None {\n1461:            span = Some((local(hand), local(hand + dir * weapon_length(def.weapon.kind) * def.weapon.size.max(0.3) * k)));\n1480:    parts.extend(held_parts);\n1494:/// Reach of a weapon from the hand to its tip (before size and scale).\n1495:pub fn weapon_length(k: WeaponKind) -> f32 {\n1499:        WeaponKind::Greatsword => 1.35,\n1511:/// A held weapon (and off-hand) as parts in character space: `h` is the hand, `d` the\n1512:/// direction the weapon points.\n//! Motion clips: animation captured or keyed elsewhere (motion-capture databases, animation\n//! libraries) as readable key poses, played on the biped puppet.\n//!\n//! The format is the one my-3D2dge introduced for AI models, kept as it is so clips move freely\n//! between the two engines. A clip is a short list of key poses; each names what an animator\n//! would: where the hips are, how the body, chest and head turn and lean, how the shoulders reach\n//! and shrug, which way each arm and leg points and how much the elbow or knee bends, how the\n//! feet point. Nothing in it depends on body size (limbs are a direction plus a bend, heights are\n//! shares of hip height), so the puppet plays any clip at its own proportions: each limb takes the\n//! clip's direction at its own length and the elbows and knees are solved with the same IK the\n//! walk uses. Every set file carries the legend (`legend`) and its credits.\n//!\n//! Sets are the `*.json` files in /anim (embedded at build time; `crate::anim::reload` re-reads\n//! them). A clip is named `SET/Clip` (or just `Clip` when only one set has it); animation state\n//! stores its `ClipId`, a hash of that name.\n\nuse std::collections::{BTreeMap, HashMap};\nuse std::sync::{Arc, RwLock};\n\nuse glam::{Quat, Vec3};\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Deserializer, Serialize};\n\nuse crate::puppet::{PuppetDef, PuppetState, Skel};\n\n/// Play flags (`PuppetState::clip_flags`).\n/// Only the chest, head and arms follow the clip; the legs keep walking.\npub const UPPER: u8 = 1;\n/// Left and right swapped.\npub const MIRROR: u8 = 2;\n/// The clip's own travel moves the body (otherwise it plays in place).\npub const TRAVEL: u8 = 4;\n/// Fading out (set by `PuppetState::stop_clip`).\npub const STOP: u8 = 8;\n/// Fades out by itself when the clip ends (one-shot gestures over the procedural animation).\npub const ONCE: u8 = 16;\n/// Play flag: fade in and out three times as fast (an attack's wind-up is short).\npub const QUICK: u8 = 32;\n/// Hold the first and last poses instead of wrapping. The editor uses this to inspect a\n/// loop's final key without changing how that clip plays in the game.\npub const CLAMP: u8 = 128;\n\n/// Seconds a clip takes to fade in or out.\npub const FADE: f32 = 0.25;\n\n/// Performance settings (the `anim` group): how fast performing characters play their clips\n/// and moves, and whether their clips are mirrored.\n#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n#[serde(default)]\npub struct AnimParams {\n    pub tempo: f32,\n    pub mirror: bool,\n}\n\nimpl Default for AnimParams {\n    fn default() -> Self {\n        Self { tempo: 1.0, mirror: false }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl crate::params::Tunable for AnimParams {","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01U6ethnAeuGG2RcMCSMfvTX","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9X4oUNqmkbuRA7GbHv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CQmeuVBUDiiXLu7yAoF5wr","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 1,120p docs/COOP_PLAN.md","description":"Read the engine's co-op plan for known pitfalls"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13684,"cache_read_input_tokens":213762,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13684},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"24d54601-6d20-4f39-9f2b-3739645b59a4","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:30:05.098Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9X4YMJfGeNVpd7r7oD","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01CQmeuVBUDiiXLu7yAoF5wr":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo && sed -n 1,120p docs/COOP_PLAN.md","description":"Read the engine's co-op plan for known pitfalls"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01CQmeuVBUDiiXLu7yAoF5wr":{"cwd":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/shardfall/repo"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CQmeuVBUDiiXLu7yAoF5wr","type":"tool_result","content":"# Co-op: up to four players, one world\n\nThe plan for online co-op in Shardfall: up to four players, each free to go anywhere, playing\nthe same game as single player, with monsters made tougher instead of more numerous.\n\n**Status: on hold** (the user's call, 2026-10-17). The plan is kept for later, not scrapped;\nnothing is built. Don't start any stage, or run §14's `/goal`, until the user takes it off hold.\nWhen it resumes, re-check §3's facts against `main` first, since the code will have moved.\n\nFacts come from a survey of `main` at `dd5584b`; line numbers are as of then, and *unverified*\nmarks what was not checked. The agent who builds it reads this whole file, then works through\n§11's stages in order. Paths without a crate are under `crates/pav_core/src/`. Players get their\nown page, `docs/COOP.md`, in C5.\n\n## 1. What the user asked\n| The ask | Where this plan meets it |\n|---|---|\n| **Up to four players**, online co-op, \"essentially the same single player experience\" | §2 (1-5), §5.1 (the cap), §5.5, §7 |\n| **No extra monsters:** monster **life and damage scale** with the number of players | §6 |\n| **Lag-free feel:** whatever it takes on every side (host, client, server) | §2 (2-4, 8-9), §5.7, §9, with measured targets in §10 |\n| **No anti-cheat:** every client is trusted | §2 (4), §5.6: claims are never re-checked |\n| **Free roaming:** anyone goes anywhere they can reach on their own, never tied to another player, never waiting (\"a super mini-MMO\") | §2 (1-2), §5.2: one shared instance per place, with travel gated only by the hero's own progress |\n| **No voice or text chat.** Emotes with animations, pings and map pings are in | §8 |\n| A short `/goal` to build it, the effort level, and whether to `/compact` or `/goal` first | §14 |\n| Implied by `AGENTS.md`: the Windows `.exe`, Linux and the browser build play together | §9, §11 (C3) |\n\n## 2. The design in one page\n1. **One game, one place, as today.** Each player's game simulates only the place its hero is\n   in (town, a level, the arena, the lab). One `Sim` holds one place, and travel rebuilds it.\n2. **Place owners.** The first player into a place builds it as single player does and **owns**\n   it: their game runs its monsters, bosses and shared mechanics. Players who arrive later are\n   **guests** whose games mirror the owner's. One guest is the place's **standby**. When the\n   owner leaves, the standby takes over from an exact snapshot, confirmed before the owner goes;\n   if the owner drops, the standby promotes from a snapshot at most 5 s old. A player alone in a\n   place is playing single player, with zero lag.\n3. **Your hero is yours.** Every game fully simulates its own hero: movement, skills, life,\n   mana, potions, items, death. Input never waits on the network.\n4. **The attacker decides it hit; the victim decides it was hit.** Your hits resolve on your\n   screen against what you see and go to the owner as claims. A monster's attack resolves on\n   its target's screen against where that hero really is. Nothing is re-checked (§5.6).\n5. **Personal rewards, shared world.** Loot, gold, XP, boons, chest payouts, vendors, the stash\n   and waypoints are per player and never cross the network: on a kill, each player nearby\n   rolls their own drops, as in single player. Monsters, bosses, seals and the level's\n   mechanics are shared (§5.5).\n6. **Scaling:** life ×(1 + 0.75(n−1)) and damage ×(1 + 0.25(n−1)), where n is the number of\n   players in the place (ruling E2; §6).\n7. **Joining a place means receiving the owner's exact state.** The owner sends its `SimState`\n   snapshot, the CBOR that `Sim::save_state` already writes, minus its own hero. Measured\n   deflated with gzip: 41 KB for town, 81 KB for level 3, 109 KB for level 9 and 175 KB for\n   level 14. Machines therefore never have to compute identical results.\n8. **One replication pattern.** Every character simulated on another machine is a **movement\n   stream** (interpolated) plus **action events** (casts, dodges, emotes, deaths), played\n   through the engine's own animation and skills.\n9. **Transport:** WebRTC data channels through matchbox, on native and in the browser: a full\n   mesh with an unreliable channel for streams and reliable channels for control, events and\n   bulk transfers (§5.4). Tests and tools use an in-process simulated network. The session host\n   keeps only the directory of who owns which place, and hands it on under a new term if it\n   leaves (§5.1).\n10. **Online rules:** no pause, rewind or speed control. Hit-stop freezes only your hero and\n    what you hit, never the world.\n\n**Rejected:**\n- *One host simulating every place* (host-authoritative). Every guest would feel latency on\n  every monster, even when alone, and the host's game would run up to five places at once.\n- *Lockstep or rollback* (for example GGRS). Both need bit-identical simulation on Windows,\n  Linux and wasm, which isn't true here: rapier's `enhanced-determinism` is off and the code\n  uses `std` trig. Every player would also simulate every place.\n- *A WebSocket relay.* On TCP, one lost packet stalls everything behind it, and every message\n  takes two hops.\n\n## 3. What the code assumes today\n| Area | Today | Where | Co-op change |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| The player | `SimState.player`; one `InputFrame` per tick, given only to the player | `sim.rs` `spawn_player` l.412, `step_inner` l.521 (input l.553) | Stays the local hero. Remote heroes use a new remote-drive mode (C0). |\n| Shardfall's hero | `Game.hero` (the profile) and `hero_id`; live life and mana are in `Actor` | `arpg/mod.rs` l.123-126, `resume_game` l.324 | `Game` splits into a hero side and a place side (C0). |\n| Monster AI | `Game::pre` takes one hero: wake at 12 m, pack at 9 m, casts at the hero's feet; one flow field cached per hero cell | `arpg/mod.rs` `pre` l.629-729, `arpg/brain.rs` l.11-12 and `think` l.56, `arpg/mechanics.rs` `hero_flow` l.248 | Target the nearest living hero (sticky); one flow field per targeted hero. |\n| Engine checks | one `player_feet` for every engine brain; `PLAYER_CRUMBLE` tiles react only to the player | `sim.rs` l.545, `ai.rs`, `destruct.rs` l.78 | Any hero (the Crumbling Halls need it). |\n| Time | hit-stop slows the whole sim to 0.06 | `Game::time_scale`, `arpg/mod.rs` l.271; `sim.rs` l.524 | Per entity online (§7). |\n| Pause, rewind, speed | the app's `TimeControl`; the Esc menu pauses; Backspace rewinds | `pav_app/src/simhost.rs` l.20-38; `app.rs` l.764, l.1000 | Off online. |\n| Travel | rebuilds the whole `SimState` within one tick, carrying the `Game`; `resume_game` wipes actors, loot and the level; the layout seed comes from the sim's RNG, so a level is new on every visit | `arpg/mod.rs` `game_travel` l.404; `arpg/world.rs` l.513 | Kept. Co-op decides whether to build fresh (owner) or adopt the owner's snapshot (guest). |\n| Damage | `Game::hit` for everything; `targets()` filters by `Team::hostile`, so there is no friendly fire | `arpg/mod.rs` l.1077; `arpg/skills.rs` `fire_cast` l.455, `targets` l.416 | One routing check at the top of `Game::hit` (§5.6). |\n| Rewards | `kill()` gives XP to the one `g.hero`; `drop_loot` uses that hero's find stats | `arpg/mod.rs` l.1309; `arpg/loot.rs` l.50 | Each game runs its own reward on a `Killed` event. |\n| Scaling hooks | `spawn_actor` sets life (l.598) and damage (l.611); `enemy_damage` is also applied in `roll_damage`, the boss's `death_burst` and powers | `arpg/mod.rs` l.578; `arpg/skills.rs` l.331; `arpg/boss.rs` l.350; `arpg/powers.rs` l.251, l.348 | Life scales on the owner, damage on the victim (§6). |\n| Where you can go | `GameCmd::Travel` is gated by the hero's own `max_depth`; `use_exit` refuses while the level is sealed | `arpg/cmd.rs` l.296; `arpg/mechanics.rs` l.911 | Unchanged: \"anywhere they can reach on their own\". |\n| HUD and map | the map takes the hero to be the first `Team::Hero` actor | `pav_app/src/arpg_ui.rs` `level_hud` l.562 (l.641) | Use `hero_id`; draw remote heroes and pings. |\n| View | `RenderFrame.player` and `focus` are single; every entity carries its own puppet look | `sim.rs` `frame` l.941; `pav_view/src/build.rs` l.1104 | Unchanged (the local hero); four heroes already render. |\n| Saves | one hero and roster per machine or browser | `pav_app/src/save.rs` | Unchanged: no save crosses the network. |\n| Bots and tools | `autoplay` and `campaign` drive `g.hero` in one `Session` | `arpg/bot.rs`; `pav_tools/src/agent_tools.rs` l.227 | Per player, through the `coop` tool (§10). |\n| App | no title screen (boots into `town`); the pause menu is the only menu | `pav_app/src/settings.rs` l.172; `ui.rs` `MenuAction` l.25, `pause_menu` l.167 | The co-op entries go in the pause menu. |\n| Networking | none; no async runtime in use (tokio is only in `Cargo.lock`) | every `Cargo.toml` | A new crate, `pav_net` (§4). |\n| Several sims | already work in one process (travel builds a second `Sim`); content caches are process-global | `arpg/mod.rs` l.409 | Enough for the in-process test network. |\n\n## 4. Where the code goes\n- **`pav_core::net`** (new; engine API):\n  - `PeerId`, `NetId` and `Channel` (unreliable or reliable).\n  - A tick's `NetInbox` and `NetOutbox`.\n  - `InterpBuffer`: interpolates, extrapolates up to a limit, then holds.\n  - A link clock: RTT, jitter and the remote-tick estimate.\n  - `SimNet`: an in-process network whose links have latency, jitter, loss and bandwidth drawn\n    from a seed.\n\n  It is pure: no IO, no threads, safe on wasm. It is written for any game on the hook; Shardfall\n  uses it first.\n- **`pav_core::arpg::coop`** (new; the game): messages, the directory, owner and guest logic,\n  the hero and place streams, damage routing, scaling, pings and emotes. Its state is\n  `Game.net: Option<Box<CoopState>>`, inside `SimState`, so a player's own snapshots and\n  replays carry it. It has two parts:\n  - the **session part** (directory, peers, host term, link clocks) lasts the whole session;\n  - the **place part** (role, place epoch, `NetId` map, interpolation buffers, pending events\n    and claims, watermarks) belongs to one place.\n\n  Place snapshots sent to other players carry neither part. Adopting a snapshot keeps the\n  recipient's session part and builds a fresh place part from the snapshot; so does any travel\n  (§5.2).\n- **`pav_net`** (new crate; internals, doctrine 8): the WebRTC transport (matchbox, on native and\n  wasm) and the signaling server with rooms (native only; §9), both embedded in the host's game\n  and as a small `shardfall-signal` binary. It handles bytes only and knows nothing of the game,\n  so `pav_core` stays free of IO.\n- **`pav_app`**: flags and settings, the net pump, the Co-op window, party frames, ping and emote\n  input and wheels, and pad and touch bindings.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"c9fe45d7-dcbb-4b41-ac32-2fd2d139fd56","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:30:05.159Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Co-op: up to four players, one world\n\nThe plan for online co-op in Shardfall: up to four players, each free to go anywhere, playing\nthe same game as single player, with monsters made tougher instead of more numerous.\n\n**Status: on hold** (the user's call, 2026-10-17). The plan is kept for later, not scrapped;\nnothing is built. Don't start any stage, or run §14's `/goal`, until the user takes it off hold.\nWhen it resumes, re-check §3's facts against `main` first, since the code will have moved.\n\nFacts come from a survey of `main` at `dd5584b`; line numbers are as of then, and *unverified*\nmarks what was not checked. The agent who builds it reads this whole file, then works through\n§11's stages in order. Paths without a crate are under `crates/pav_core/src/`. Players get their\nown page, `docs/COOP.md`, in C5.\n\n## 1. What the user asked\n| The ask | Where this plan meets it |\n|---|---|\n| **Up to four players**, online co-op, \"essentially the same single player experience\" | §2 (1-5), §5.1 (the cap), §5.5, §7 |\n| **No extra monsters:** monster **life and damage scale** with the number of players | §6 |\n| **Lag-free feel:** whatever it takes on every side (host, client, server) | §2 (2-4, 8-9), §5.7, §9, with measured targets in §10 |\n| **No anti-cheat:** every client is trusted | §2 (4), §5.6: claims are never re-checked |\n| **Free roaming:** anyone goes anywhere they can reach on their own, never tied to another player, never waiting (\"a super mini-MMO\") | §2 (1-2), §5.2: one shared instance per place, with travel gated only by the hero's own progress |\n| **No voice or text chat.** Emotes with animations, pings and map pings are in | §8 |\n| A short `/goal` to build it, the effort level, and whether to `/compact` or `/goal` first | §14 |\n| Implied by `AGENTS.md`: the Windows `.exe`, Linux and the browser build play together | §9, §11 (C3) |\n\n## 2. The design in one page\n1. **One game, one place, as today.** Each player's game simulates only the place its hero is\n   in (town, a level, the arena, the lab). One `Sim` holds one place, and travel rebuilds it.\n2. **Place owners.** The first player into a place builds it as single player does and **owns**\n   it: their game runs its monsters, bosses and shared mechanics. Players who arrive later are\n   **guests** whose games mirror the owner's. One guest is the place's **standby**. When the\n   owner leaves, the standby takes over from an exact snapshot, confirmed before the owner goes;\n   if the owner drops, the standby promotes from a snapshot at most 5 s old. A player alone in a\n   place is playing single player, with zero lag.\n3. **Your hero is yours.** Every game fully simulates its own hero: movement, skills, life,\n   mana, potions, items, death. Input never waits on the network.\n4. **The attacker decides it hit; the victim decides it was hit.** Your hits resolve on your\n   screen against what you see and go to the owner as claims. A monster's attack resolves on\n   its target's screen against where that hero really is. Nothing is re-checked (§5.6).\n5. **Personal rewards, shared world.** Loot, gold, XP, boons, chest payouts, vendors, the stash\n   and waypoints are per player and never cross the network: on a kill, each player nearby\n   rolls their own drops, as in single player. Monsters, bosses, seals and the level's\n   mechanics are shared (§5.5).\n6. **Scaling:** life ×(1 + 0.75(n−1)) and damage ×(1 + 0.25(n−1)), where n is the number of\n   players in the place (ruling E2; §6).\n7. **Joining a place means receiving the owner's exact state.** The owner sends its `SimState`\n   snapshot, the CBOR that `Sim::save_state` already writes, minus its own hero. Measured\n   deflated with gzip: 41 KB for town, 81 KB for level 3, 109 KB for level 9 and 175 KB for\n   level 14. Machines therefore never have to compute identical results.\n8. **One replication pattern.** Every character simulated on another machine is a **movement\n   stream** (interpolated) plus **action events** (casts, dodges, emotes, deaths), played\n   through the engine's own animation and skills.\n9. **Transport:** WebRTC data channels through matchbox, on native and in the browser: a full\n   mesh with an unreliable channel for streams and reliable channels for control, events and\n   bulk transfers (§5.4). Tests and tools use an in-process simulated network. The session host\n   keeps only the directory of who owns which place, and hands it on under a new term if it\n   leaves (§5.1).\n10. **Online rules:** no pause, rewind or speed control. Hit-stop freezes only your hero and\n    what you hit, never the world.\n\n**Rejected:**\n- *One host simulating every place* (host-authoritative). Every guest would feel latency on\n  every monster, even when alone, and the host's game would run up to five places at once.\n- *Lockstep or rollback* (for example GGRS). Both need bit-identical simulation on Windows,\n  Linux and wasm, which isn't true here: rapier's `enhanced-determinism` is off and the code\n  uses `std` trig. Every player would also simulate every place.\n- *A WebSocket relay.* On TCP, one lost packet stalls everything behind it, and every message\n  takes two hops.\n\n## 3. What the code assumes today\n| Area | Today | Where | Co-op change |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| The player | `SimState.player`; one `InputFrame` per tick, given only to the player | `sim.rs` `spawn_player` l.412, `step_inner` l.521 (input l.553) | Stays the local hero. Remote heroes use a new remote-drive mode (C0). |\n| Shardfall's hero | `Game.hero` (the profile) and `hero_id`; live life and mana are in `Actor` | `arpg/mod.rs` l.123-126, `resume_game` l.324 | `Game` splits into a hero side and a place side (C0). |\n| Monster AI | `Game::pre` takes one hero: wake at 12 m, pack at 9 m, casts at the hero's feet; one flow field cached per hero cell | `arpg/mod.rs` `pre` l.629-729, `arpg/brain.rs` l.11-12 and `think` l.56, `arpg/mechanics.rs` `hero_flow` l.248 | Target the nearest living hero (sticky); one flow field per targeted hero. |\n| Engine checks | one `player_feet` for every engine brain; `PLAYER_CRUMBLE` tiles react only to the player | `sim.rs` l.545, `ai.rs`, `destruct.rs` l.78 | Any hero (the Crumbling Halls need it). |\n| Time | hit-stop slows the whole sim to 0.06 | `Game::time_scale`, `arpg/mod.rs` l.271; `sim.rs` l.524 | Per entity online (§7). |\n| Pause, rewind, speed | the app's `TimeControl`; the Esc menu pauses; Backspace rewinds | `pav_app/src/simhost.rs` l.20-38; `app.rs` l.764, l.1000 | Off online. |\n| Travel | rebuilds the whole `SimState` within one tick, carrying the `Game`; `resume_game` wipes actors, loot and the level; the layout seed comes from the sim's RNG, so a level is new on every visit | `arpg/mod.rs` `game_travel` l.404; `arpg/world.rs` l.513 | Kept. Co-op decides whether to build fresh (owner) or adopt the owner's snapshot (guest). |\n| Damage | `Game::hit` for everything; `targets()` filters by `Team::hostile`, so there is no friendly fire | `arpg/mod.rs` l.1077; `arpg/skills.rs` `fire_cast` l.455, `targets` l.416 | One routing check at the top of `Game::hit` (§5.6). |\n| Rewards | `kill()` gives XP to the one `g.hero`; `drop_loot` uses that hero's find stats | `arpg/mod.rs` l.1309; `arpg/loot.rs` l.50 | Each game runs its own reward on a `Killed` event. |\n| Scaling hooks | `spawn_actor` sets life (l.598) and damage (l.611); `enemy_damage` is also applied in `roll_damage`, the boss's `death_burst` and powers | `arpg/mod.rs` l.578; `arpg/skills.rs` l.331; `arpg/boss.rs` l.350; `arpg/powers.rs` l.251, l.348 | Life scales on the owner, damage on the victim (§6). |\n| Where you can go | `GameCmd::Travel` is gated by the hero's own `max_depth`; `use_exit` refuses while the level is sealed | `arpg/cmd.rs` l.296; `arpg/mechanics.rs` l.911 | Unchanged: \"anywhere they can reach on their own\". |\n| HUD and map | the map takes the hero to be the first `Team::Hero` actor | `pav_app/src/arpg_ui.rs` `level_hud` l.562 (l.641) | Use `hero_id`; draw remote heroes and pings. |\n| View | `RenderFrame.player` and `focus` are single; every entity carries its own puppet look | `sim.rs` `frame` l.941; `pav_view/src/build.rs` l.1104 | Unchanged (the local hero); four heroes already render. |\n| Saves | one hero and roster per machine or browser | `pav_app/src/save.rs` | Unchanged: no save crosses the network. |\n| Bots and tools | `autoplay` and `campaign` drive `g.hero` in one `Session` | `arpg/bot.rs`; `pav_tools/src/agent_tools.rs` l.227 | Per player, through the `coop` tool (§10). |\n| App | no title screen (boots into `town`); the pause menu is the only menu | `pav_app/src/settings.rs` l.172; `ui.rs` `MenuAction` l.25, `pause_menu` l.167 | The co-op entries go in the pause menu. |\n| Networking | none; no async runtime in use (tokio is only in `Cargo.lock`) | every `Cargo.toml` | A new crate, `pav_net` (§4). |\n| Several sims | already work in one process (travel builds a second `Sim`); content caches are process-global | `arpg/mod.rs` l.409 | Enough for the in-process test network. |\n\n## 4. Where the code goes\n- **`pav_core::net`** (new; engine API):\n  - `PeerId`, `NetId` and `Channel` (unreliable or reliable).\n  - A tick's `NetInbox` and `NetOutbox`.\n  - `InterpBuffer`: interpolates, extrapolates up to a limit, then holds.\n  - A link clock: RTT, jitter and the remote-tick estimate.\n  - `SimNet`: an in-process network whose links have latency, jitter, loss and bandwidth drawn\n    from a seed.\n\n  It is pure: no IO, no threads, safe on wasm. It is written for any game on the hook; Shardfall\n  uses it first.\n- **`pav_core::arpg::coop`** (new; the game): messages, the directory, owner and guest logic,\n  the hero and place streams, damage routing, scaling, pings and emotes. Its state is\n  `Game.net: Option<Box<CoopState>>`, inside `SimState`, so a player's own snapshots and\n  replays carry it. It has two parts:\n  - the **session part** (directory, peers, host term, link clocks) lasts the whole session;\n  - the **place part** (role, place epoch, `NetId` map, interpolation buffers, pending events\n    and claims, watermarks) belongs to one place.\n\n  Place snapshots sent to other players carry neither part. Adopting a snapshot keeps the\n  recipient's session part and builds a fresh place part from the snapshot; so does any travel\n  (§5.2).\n- **`pav_net`** (new crate; internals, doctrine 8): the WebRTC transport (matchbox, on native and\n  wasm) and the signaling server with rooms (native only; §9), both embedded in the host's game\n  and as a small `shardfall-signal` binary. It handles bytes only and knows nothing of the game,\n  so `pav_core` stays free of IO.\n- **`pav_app`**: flags and settings, the net pump, the Co-op window, party frames, ping and emote\n  input and wheels, and pad and touch bindings.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01CQmeuVBUDiiXLu7yAoF5wr","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9X4oUNqmkbuRA7GbHv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TPUoEWuJMfGYwkpGsDgbMS","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/ai-hub/repo/lib/hub && sed -n 425,548p harness.html; echo ---; sed -n 1,60p bridge.js | head -80; grep -n \"allow\\|sandbox\\|iframe\\|createElement('iframe')\" harness.html bridge.js ../../session/* 2>/dev/null | head -20; sed -n 1,60p round.js | sed -n 8,50p; ls ../ ; sed -n 1,60p ../../docs/session-hub.md","description":"Read harness check flow, iframe attributes and hub timings"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13684,"cache_read_input_tokens":213762,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13684},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"33763885-0015-4428-91f2-66f9b2d9072d","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:30:07.333Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9X4YMJfGeNVpd7r7oD","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01TPUoEWuJMfGYwkpGsDgbMS":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211926__x6e380e/work/.research/ai-hub/repo/lib/hub && sed -n 425,548p harness.html; echo ---; sed -n 1,60p bridge.js | head -80; grep -n \"allow\\|sandbox\\|iframe\\|createElement('iframe')\" harness.html bridge.js ../../session/* 2>/dev/null | head -20; sed -n 1,60p round.js | sed -n 8,50p; ls ../ ; sed -n 1,60p ../../docs/session-hub.md","description":"Read harness check flow, iframe attributes and hub timings"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TPUoEWuJMfGYwkpGsDgbMS","type":"tool_result","content":"    if (m?.minutes != null) check('hub:session-minutes, when given, is a number', Number(m.minutes) > 0, `hub:session-minutes=${m.minutes}`);\n\n    // Every copy: the players, and in a server round the server's copy.\n    const copies = () => [...(state.status?.players ?? []), ...(state.status?.server ? [state.status.server] : [])];\n    const helloed = await until(() => copies().every((p) => p.hello), TIMINGS.helloMs + 1000);\n    check('every copy says hello', helloed, copies().filter((p) => !p.hello).map((p) => p.key).join(', '));\n    if (!helloed) return;\n    const caps = state.coordinator.seats().map((s) => s.caps);\n    check('hello names protocol 1 and the mode', caps.every((c) => c.v === 1 && modeOf(c.mode) === state.coordinator.mode), JSON.stringify(caps[0]));\n    check('hello seats cover this player count', caps.every((c) => Array.isArray(c.seats) && c.seats[0] <= Math.max(2, n) && c.seats[1] >= n), JSON.stringify(caps[0]?.seats));\n    if (state.cells.some((c) => !c.frame.contentDocument)) {\n      check('frames reachable for synthetic input', false, 'serve the game from the same origin as this harness');\n      return;\n    }\n\n    // The card collects ready: every game readies up by itself once loaded, with no prompt and no key.\n    const loaded = await until(() => copies().every((p) => p.ready), 20_000);\n    check('ready without a key press (the card asks the players)', loaded, copies().filter((p) => !p.ready).map((p) => p.key).join(', '));\n    // A game that shows its own card takes the players' answers on it: Enter is Ready.\n    if (caps.some((c) => c?.card)) {\n      const players = state.cells.filter((c) => !c.server);\n      const sent = await until(() => players.every((c) => c.cardSent), 2000);\n      for (const cell of players) poke(cell, 'Enter');\n      const answered = await until(() => players.every((c) => state.answers.ready.has(c.key)), 3000);\n      check('its own card: Enter answers Ready', sent && answered, players.filter((c) => !state.answers.ready.has(c.key)).map((c) => `${c.key}${c.cardSent ? '' : ' (no card yet)'}`).join(', '));\n    }\n    releaseRound(state);\n    const started = await until(() => state.status?.phase !== 'loading', 15_000);\n    check('the round starts when the card ends', started && copies().every((p) => p.ready), copies().filter((p) => !p.ready).map((p) => p.key).join(', ') || state.status?.phase);\n    if (!started || state.record) return;\n\n    await until(() => state.status.phase === 'playing' || state.record, TIMINGS.countdownMs + 2000);\n    const arrows = ['ArrowLeft', 'ArrowRight', 'ArrowUp', 'ArrowDown', 'a', 'd', 'w', 's'];\n    const randomly = async (ms) => {\n      const end = performance.now() + ms;\n      while (performance.now() < end && !state.record) {\n        for (const cell of state.cells) if (!cell.gone && !cell.server) poke(cell, arrows[Math.floor(Math.random() * arrows.length)]);\n        await sleep(150);\n      }\n    };\n    await randomly(Math.min(1500, playMs / 4));\n\n    const mode = state.coordinator.mode;\n    // Parallel and ghost: every copy runs its own world from the same seeded start. A game\n    // still tagged race was built to race's contract: no seeded start, and no progress asked.\n    const own = SELF_REPORTED.includes(mode);\n    const legacyRace = own && !seedsRandom(m?.mode);\n    if (own && !legacyRace) {\n      const seeded = state.cells.every((c) => c.frame.contentWindow?.AIHub?.seeded === true);\n      check(`${mode}: Math.random is seeded, the same on every copy`, seeded, state.cells.map((c) => `${c.key}: client ${c.frame.contentWindow?.AIHub?.version ?? '?'} seeded ${c.frame.contentWindow?.AIHub?.seeded}`).join('; '));\n    }\n\n    if (scenario === 'host-leave') removeCell(state.cells[0]);\n    else if (n >= 3) removeCell(state.cells[n - 1]);\n    await randomly(playMs / 2);\n    const remaining = state.cells.filter((c) => !c.gone);\n    if (own) {\n      if (mode === 'parallel') {\n        check('parallel: no game traffic', state.cells.every((c) => c.bridge.stats.sent === 0),\n          state.cells.map((c) => `${c.key} ↑${c.bridge.stats.sent}`).join(' '));\n      } else {\n        check('ghost: every copy sends its player', remaining.every((c) => c.bridge.stats.sent > 0) || n === 1,\n          remaining.map((c) => `${c.key} ↑${c.bridge.stats.sent}`).join(' '));\n      }\n      if (!legacyRace) check(`${mode}: every copy reports its progress`, remaining.every((c) => c.progress.length > 0 || state.status?.players.find((p) => p.key === c.key)?.result),\n        remaining.map((c) => `${c.key}: ${c.progress.length} report${c.progress.length === 1 ? '' : 's'}`).join(', '));\n    } else {\n      check('game traffic flows both ways', remaining.every((c) => c.bridge.stats.sent > 0 && c.bridge.stats.received > 0) || n === 1,\n        remaining.map((c) => `${c.key} ↑${c.bridge.stats.sent} ↓${c.bridge.stats.received}`).join(' '));\n    }\n\n    if (!state.record) state.coordinator.timeUp();\n    const ended = await until(() => state.record, TIMINGS.graceMs + 2000);\n    const record = state.record;\n    if (serverMode) {\n      // The world ran on the server: the players heard it, and only the host may finish a shared world.\n      const serverCell = state.cells.find((c) => c.server);\n      check('a copy with no player of its own runs the world (as a server would)', ended && record.status === 'played' && record.hostId === serverCell.bridge.me && serverCell.bridge.stats.sent > 0,\n        record ? `${record.status} (${record.reason}), host ${record.hostId}, server sent ${serverCell.bridge.stats.sent}` : 'no end');\n    }\n    if (scenario === 'host-leave' && !SELF_REPORTED.includes(state.coordinator.mode)) {\n      check('host leaving voids the round', ended && record.status === 'void' && record.endReason === 'host-left', record ? `${record.status} (${record.reason})` : 'no end');\n    } else {\n      check('the round ends with a valid result', ended && record.status === 'played', record ? `${record.status} (${record.reason})` : 'no end within the grace period');\n    }\n    await sleep(300);\n    check('every remaining copy is told the round ended', remaining.every((c) => c.bridge.ended), remaining.filter((c) => !c.bridge.ended).map((c) => c.key).join(', '));\n    const issues = [...(state.status?.issues ?? []), ...state.cells.flatMap((c) => c.bridge.issues)];\n    check('no protocol problems', issues.length === 0, issues.map((i) => `${i.key}: ${i.problem}`).join('; '));\n    const violations = state.cells.flatMap((c) => {\n      let extra = [];\n      try {\n        extra = c.frame.contentWindow?.__hubViolations ?? [];\n      } catch {}\n      return [...c.violations, ...extra].map((v) => `${c.key}: ${v}`);\n    });\n    check('no errors, dialogs, popups or sockets', violations.length === 0, violations.slice(0, 6).join('; '));\n  } catch (error) {\n    check('harness ran', false, String(error?.stack || error));\n  } finally {\n    report.pass = checks.length > 0 && checks.every((c) => c.pass);\n    report.done = true;\n    document.body.dataset.complete = 'true';\n    log(`conformance: ${report.pass ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL'}`);\n  }\n}\n\nfunction renderChecks(checks) {\n  $('checklist').replaceChildren(\n    ...checks.map((c) => {\n      const li = document.createElement('li');\n      li.className = c.pass ? 'ok' : 'bad';\n      li.textContent = c.name + (c.detail && !c.pass ? ` — ${c.detail}` : '');\n      li.title = c.detail;\n      return li;\n    }),\n  );\n}\n\nif (auto) conformance();\nelse if ($('game').value) newRound();\n</script>\n</body>\n</html>\n---\n/**\n * bridge.js — the hub's end of one game frame in a session.\n *\n * A game speaks the AI Hub Session Protocol over postMessage (lib/hub/README.md).\n * The bridge checks every message, enforces the limits, and routes it: control\n * messages (hello, ready, result, finish, error) to whoever coordinates the\n * round (lib/hub/round.js), game traffic straight to the other players, and a\n * game's answers on its own card to the page that holds it. What\n * comes back from the network is checked again before it reaches the game,\n * and anything from another round is dropped, so a slow packet can never land\n * in the next game.\n *\n * The network is anything with `send(toKey, envelope, {reliable})`: the\n * simulated one below (harness, tests), a BroadcastChannel between tabs, or a\n * WebRTC mesh. Envelopes:\n *   {k:'c', r, m}       to the coordinator: a message from this game\n *   {k:'h', r, m}       from the coordinator: welcome, start, leave, time-up, end\n *   {k:'g', r, f, d}    game traffic from player id f\n *   {k:'p', r, f, d}    player id f's progress, {score, label}, for the scoreboard\n */\n\nexport const LIMITS = Object.freeze({\n  maxBytes: 16_384,\n  ratePerS: 20,\n  burst: 40,\n  queueMessages: 64,\n  queueBytes: 262_144,\n  /**\n   * progress() reaches the hub about once a second. Two closer than this are a\n   * report delivered late (a long task in the game) or a client misbehaving:\n   * the later is dropped, and the next report, a second on, has the newest score.\n   */\n  progressMs: 250,\n  label: 24,\n});\n\nconst FROM_GAME = new Set(['hello', 'ready', 'send', 'result', 'finish', 'error', 'progress', 'fullscreen', 'answer']);\n\n/** A progress report, checked: a finite score and an optional short label. Null when it is not one. */\nexport function cleanProgress(p) {\n  if (!p || typeof p !== 'object') return null;\n  // A number, or text that is one: never null, '', true or [], which Number() would make 0 or 1.\n  const score = typeof p.score === 'number' || (typeof p.score === 'string' && p.score.trim() !== '') ? Number(p.score) : NaN;\n  if (!Number.isFinite(score)) return null;\n  const label = typeof p.label === 'string' ? p.label.trim().slice(0, LIMITS.label) : '';\n  return label ? { score, label } : { score };\n}\n\n/**\n * @param {object} o\n * @param {string} o.key              this player's key in the session\n * @param {number} o.round\n * @param {string} o.coordinator      key of whoever runs round.js\n * @param {(msg:object) => void} o.post            to the game frame\n * @param {{send:Function}} o.net\n * @param {{now:()=>number}} [o.clock]             the coordinator's time, as seen here\n * @param {(issue:{key:string, problem:string}) => void} [o.onIssue]\n * @param {(id:number, progress:{score:number, label?:string}) => void} [o.onProgress]   a player's\n *        progress, this one's included, for the hub's scoreboard; it never reaches the games\n * @param {(on:boolean) => void} [o.onFullscreen]   the game asked for fullscreen (or out of it): the\nbridge.js:262: * Wire a bridge to an iframe: only messages from that frame's window count.\nharness.html:27:  .cell iframe { width: 100%; height: 100%; border: 0; background: #000; }\nharness.html:115: * address in an unsandboxed frame would run as this origin, where a signed-in player's session is kept.\nharness.html:146:        log(`${cell.name}: ${name}() called — not allowed in a session`);\nharness.html:210:    const frame = document.createElement('iframe');\nharness.html:211:    frame.allow = 'fullscreen; autoplay; gamepad; pointer-lock';\n * injected, so `scripts/hub.test.mjs` can walk a round through every timeout\n * without waiting for one.\n *\n * Players are known by `key` (whatever the session calls them); games see\n * contiguous ids 0..N-1 in slot order.\n */\n\nexport const TIMINGS = Object.freeze({\n  helloMs: 10_000,\n  readyMs: 45_000,\n  /** Gated rounds: a game still loading when the hub releases the round gets this long more. */\n  loadGraceMs: 20_000,\n  countdownMs: 3_000,\n  graceMs: 5_000,\n  hiddenMs: 20_000,\n  maxRoundMs: 15 * 60_000,\n});\n\n/** P1 red, P2 blue, P3 green, P4 yellow: the colour follows the seat. */\nexport const SLOT_COLORS = Object.freeze(['#e8443a', '#3b82f6', '#22c55e', '#f5b700']);\n\nexport const MODES = Object.freeze(['parallel', 'ghost', 'shared-world']);\n\n/** Each mode's name on screen. */\nexport const MODE_NAMES = Object.freeze({ parallel: 'Parallel', ghost: 'Ghost', 'shared-world': 'Shared world' });\n\n/** Older tags, still played: race as ghost, co-op and versus as a shared world. */\nconst MODE_ALIASES = Object.freeze({ race: 'ghost', 'co-op': 'shared-world', versus: 'shared-world' });\n\n/** The mode a tag or a hello names, older tags included; null for anything else. */\nexport function modeOf(word) {\n  const w = String(word ?? '').trim().toLowerCase();\n  return MODES.includes(w) ? w : (MODE_ALIASES[w] ?? null);\n}\n\n/** Modes where every copy runs its own world: each reports its own result, and nothing depends on a host. */\nexport const SELF_REPORTED = Object.freeze(['parallel', 'ghost']);\n\n/**\n * Whether a game's round carries the seed in its address, so the client seeds\n * Math.random and every copy starts the same: read from the game's own tag,\n * parallel or ghost. A game still tagged race was built for an unseeded start\n * (ids it makes from Math.random must differ between its copies), so it keeps one.\nREADME.md\nhub\nkit\nrapier\nthree\nthree-addons.exclude\nvendor\n# AI Hub sessions: the hub's side\n\nWhat the hub does around a round, and how a round travels between the\ndevices. Games never need this page: the protocol a game follows is\n`lib/hub/README.md` (served at\n<https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md>), and that is the only\nthing the game factory is given. This page is for whoever changes the hub:\n`lib/hub/` (client, bridge, round, harness) and `assets/js/session/` (the\nsession page).\n\n## Around a round\n\nA game only plays its round. Everything around it belongs to the hub and is\nthe same for every game, so players learn it once.\n\n### Points and wins\n\n- Each round, a player scores **one point for every player they beat**. A\n  player who left or sat out without reporting counts as beaten; one who\n  reported and then left keeps their place. Tied players share a place. Every\n  round of four is worth three points to its winner, whatever the game.\n- **First place is a win**; in a team result (a shared world's `finish` with\n  `won`) only when the team won.\n- The **table** orders players by points, then wins, then the better average\n  place (`assets/js/session/score.js`).\n\n### Awards\n\n- **Game awards** come from a game's `result()` or `finish()`. The hub shows\n  them on the results, in the player's colour, and counts them for the\n  session.\n- **Session awards** are the hub's, given when a session ends:\n  **Showstopper** (most game awards), **Hot streak** (most wins in a row, at\n  least two) and **Steady hand** (best average place).\n\n### Ways to win a session\n\nThe host picks a format in the lobby:\n\n| Format | Ends | Winner |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Party** (default) | after 8 rounds | most points, after the three session awards add **3 bonus points** each, so the last word is never certain |\n| **Sprint** | after 5 rounds | most points |\n| **First to 12** | when someone has 12 points after a round | that player |\n| **Endless** | when the host ends it | most points |\n\nTies go to more wins, then the better average place, and otherwise stay ties.\n\n### The deal\n\nThe host's own picks come first: **Up next** in the lobby, the games the host\nchose (`state.queue`, at most twelve slugs), played in their order before\nRandom Mode deals. The host picks from every game that seats the players\npresent and that every seat's browser can run; the deal's filters and the\ntouch-screen rule narrow only Random Mode. A pick that does not seat the\nplayers yet keeps its place for a later round. A session hosted from a game's\npage in the Friends view (`/session?first=<slug>`) starts with that game in\nthe list, and the address drops it once the session has it. The round's\nstate says `picked: true`, and its card reads *The host's pick*. A pick is\nadded to the played list, so Random Mode does not deal it again in the same","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b664ff9f-3df3-486c-8cc6-7a0b3ccfba7c","uuid":"eb4050cc-1bc8-4858-8534-735fb8a3b950","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:30:07.475Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"    if (m?.minutes != null) check('hub:session-minutes, when given, is a number', Number(m.minutes) > 0, `hub:session-minutes=${m.minutes}`);\n\n    // Every copy: the players, and in a server round the server's copy.\n    const copies = () => [...(state.status?.players ?? []), ...(state.status?.server ? [state.status.server] : [])];\n    const helloed = await until(() => copies().every((p) => p.hello), TIMINGS.helloMs + 1000);\n    check('every copy says hello', helloed, copies().filter((p) => !p.hello).map((p) => p.key).join(', '));\n    if (!helloed) return;\n    const caps = state.coordinator.seats().map((s) => s.caps);\n    check('hello names protocol 1 and the mode', caps.every((c) => c.v === 1 && modeOf(c.mode) === state.coordinator.mode), JSON.stringify(caps[0]));\n    check('hello seats cover this player count', caps.every((c) => Array.isArray(c.seats) && c.seats[0] <= Math.max(2, n) && c.seats[1] >= n), JSON.stringify(caps[0]?.seats));\n    if (state.cells.some((c) => !c.frame.contentDocument)) {\n      check('frames reachable for synthetic input', false, 'serve the game from the same origin as this harness');\n      return;\n    }\n\n    // The card collects ready: every game readies up by itself once loaded, with no prompt and no key.\n    const loaded = await until(() => copies().every((p) => p.ready), 20_000);\n    check('ready without a key press (the card asks the players)', loaded, copies().filter((p) => !p.ready).map((p) => p.key).join(', '));\n    // A game that shows its own card takes the players' answers on it: Enter is Ready.\n    if (caps.some((c) => c?.card)) {\n      const players =